// Generated by scripts/gen-drill-en.mjs — JLPT 詳解英文版
window.JLPT_Q_EN = {"n5-k1":"<b>学校=がっこう</b>. 「学(がく)」+「校(こう)」 combine and trigger gemination (a double consonant) → 「がっこう」.<br>「がくこう」 misses the gemination — the most common beginner mistake; 「がっこ」 drops the long vowel at the end; 「かっこう」 gets the initial consonant wrong (it's が, not か).","n5-k2":"<b>先生=せんせい</b>. 「先(せん)」+「生(せい)」.<br>「せんせ」 drops the final long vowel (it often sounds like this in speech, but the reading is written out in full); 「せいせん」 reverses the order; 「ぜんせい」 wrongly voices the first syllable.","n5-k3":"<b>建物=たてもの</b>, a kun'yomi reading.<br>「けんぶつ」 uses on'yomi for both characters (wrong — and 「見物けんぶつ」 is a different word meaning sightseeing); 「たちもの」 uses the wrong verb stem (建てる→たて); 「たてぶつ」 wrongly switches the second character to on'yomi.","n5-k4":"<b>時間=じかん</b>.<br>「じっかん」 adds an extra double consonant (「実感じっかん」 is a different word); 「ときかん」 wrongly uses the kun'yomi とき for 「時」; 「じがん」 wrongly voices the second character.","n5-k5":"<b>川=かわ</b>.<br>「がわ」 only appears as a voiced form at the end of a compound (e.g. 江戸川えどがわ); on its own it stays unvoiced as かわ; 「かは」 is historical kana orthography, no longer used today; 「いけ」 is 「池」 (pond).","n5-k6":"<b>毎朝=まいあさ</b>. A mixed reading: 「毎(まい)」 on'yomi + 「朝(あさ)」 kun'yomi.<br>「まいちょう」 wrongly uses the on'yomi ちょう for 朝 (the on'yomi-style word 「今朝」 is read 「けさ」, and 毎朝 never uses ちょう); 「ごとあさ」 wrongly uses a kun'yomi for 毎 (ごと appears in 「〜ごとに」); the last option is just a made-up combination.","n5-k7":"<b>名前=なまえ</b>, a kun'yomi reading.<br>「めいまえ」 wrongly uses the on'yomi めい for 名; 「なまい」 gets the ending wrong; 「みょうじ」 is the reading of 「名字」 (surname), not 名前.","n5-k8":"<b>電気=でんき</b>.<br>「てんき」 is 「天気」 (weather) — 電 and 天 differ only in whether the first consonant is voiced, which makes them easy to confuse; 「でんぎ」 wrongly voices the second character; 「でんきい」 adds a long vowel that isn't there.","n5-k9":"<b>会社=かいしゃ</b>.<br>「がいしゃ」voices the first syllable, which is wrong (「外車がいしゃ」means an imported car); 「かいさ」is missing the contracted sound しゃ on the second kanji; 「かしゃ」drops the い (「貨車かしゃ」is a different word — a freight car).","n5-k10":"<b>今日=きょう</b>, a 熟字訓 (the reading belongs to the whole word; you can't read the kanji one by one).<br>「こんにち」appears only in 「こんにちは」or written phrases like 「今日(こんにち)の社会」; 「いまひ」forces a character-by-character reading; 「きよう」has no contracted sound — that's 「器用(きよう)」, meaning skillful.","n5-c1":"It's cold, so you <b>close the window = しめて(閉めて)</b>.<br>「あけて(開けて)」means to open, which contradicts \"because it's cold\"; 「つけて/けして」are for switching appliances on and off (電気をつける/消す), not for windows.","n5-c2":"Before entering a (Japanese-style) room you <b>take off your shoes = ぬいで(脱いで)</b>.<br>The wearing verbs come as a set: 「着る」for clothing on the upper body, 「かぶる」for hats, 「はく」for shoes, socks and trousers — so 「はいて」means putting them on, the opposite direction.","n5-c3":"To describe how curry tastes, use <b>からい(辛い)= spicy</b>.<br>「たかい」= expensive/tall, 「はやい」= fast, 「おもい」= heavy — none of these describe taste.","n5-c4":"To ask someone to say it again: <b>言って(いって)ください</b>.<br>The other options (eat / go on foot / sit) make no sense after 「もう一度〜」. Note that 「言って」and 「行って」sound identical — tell them apart from context.","n5-c5":"To say \"about\" with a quantity or a length of time, use <b>ぐらい</b>: 10分ぐらい = about 10 minutes.<br>「ごろ」is for a point in time (3時ごろ = around 3 o'clock) and cannot attach to a duration; 「まで」means \"up until\"; 「じゅう」(中) means \"all through / throughout\" a period.","n5-c6":"Lots of people → <b>にぎやか (lively, bustling)</b>.<br>「しずか」means quiet, which contradicts a crowd; 「ひま」describes a person being free/idle; 「べんり」means convenient, which has no causal link to there being many people.","n4-c3":"Leaving early is so you'll <b>make it on time (間に合う)</b> for the appointment. 「〜ように」= so that…, in order to…<br>「間に合わない/遅れる」are the opposite of why you'd leave early; 「忘れる」doesn't fit the meaning.","n4-c4":"Asking how a kanji is pronounced: <b>読み方 (the reading)</b>. 「ます-stem + 方(かた)」= the way of doing…<br>「見方」means a viewpoint/way of looking at something; 「書き道/言い物」aren't real words (the correct forms are 書き方/言い方).","n4-c5":"「入る」is intransitive, so the resulting state uses <b>ています</b>: 牛乳が入っています (the state of there being milk inside).<br>「てあります」attaches to transitive verbs (入れてあります○); 「ておきます」means doing something in advance; 「てしまいます」marks completion or regret.","n4-c6":"「〜し、〜も」lists good points, and paired with 「歌も上手」the potential form <b>弾ける (can play)</b> is the natural fit: he plays guitar and sings well too.<br>「弾く」just means \"play,\" not \"can play\"; the past and negative forms clash with the praising tone of the second half.","n4-c7":"Important things → make sure you write them down, <b>必ず (without fail)</b>.<br>「たぶん」is probably (a guess); 「なかなか」+ negative = not … easily; 「決して」must be followed by a negative (決して忘れないで).","n4-c8":"<b>準備(じゅんび)</b> — the preparations for moving house.<br>「予約」is a reservation (a restaurant/ticket); 「約束」is a promise between people; 「用意事」isn't a word (「用意」alone is fine, but there's no 「用意事」).","n4-g1":"The teacher explained it (for me): 〜て<b>くださいました</b>. くださる is the honorific form of くれる, and the subject is the person doing you the favour (先生が) — honorific language is the right choice for a teacher.<br>「てくれました」is grammatical, but should be raised to honorific form for a teacher; 「てもらいました」requires me as the subject and marks the teacher with に (先生に説明してもらいました); 「てあげました」points the other way (I do something for someone else).","n4-g2":"「〜は〜に＋passive form」is the direct passive: the cake is the subject and gets eaten by your little brother → <b>食べられました</b>.<br>「食べさせました」is causative (made/let him eat), and its subject has to be the one making someone eat, so 「ケーキは」can't be the subject; 「食べさせられました」is causative-passive (was made to eat); 「食べました」doesn't work with 「ケーキは…弟に」.","n5-c7":"You don't know the kanji → so you ask someone to <b>write it in hiragana = 書いて(かいて)</b>.<br>「よんで」is read, 「はなして」is speak, 「きいて」is listen/ask — none of them pair naturally with 「ひらがなで」the way \"write\" does.","n5-c8":"For giving directions, use <b>まっすぐ (straight ahead)</b>: follow this road straight and you'll reach the station.<br>「だんだん」means gradually (for change over time); 「とても」means very (degree); 「あまり」must be followed by a negative.","n5-g1":"「聞く」is a transitive verb, so its object takes <b>を</b>: 音楽を聞く.<br>「が」marks the subject or the target of a preference/ability (音楽が好き); 「に」marks a target or point in time; 「で」marks a location or means.","n5-g2":"The place where an action happens takes <b>で</b>: borrowing a book at the library.<br>「に」marks where something exists (図書館に本がある) or a destination; 「を」marks the object; 「へ」marks direction of movement. The action 「借りる」takes place at the library → で.","n5-g3":"The person you meet with 「会う」is marked by <b>に</b>: 友だちに会う (meet a friend).<br>In English \"meet him\" looks like a direct object, but Japanese 「会う」never takes を — this is one of the most frequently tested fixed pairings at N5.","n5-g4":"Inside a clause that modifies a noun, the subject takes <b>が</b>: 妹が作ったケーキ (the cake my little sister made).<br>「は」can't be used inside such a clause (は marks the topic of the whole sentence); 「を」would make it \"made my little sister,\" which makes no sense; 「も」means \"also,\" which doesn't fit.","n5-g5":"「〜てもいいですか」(may I…?) attaches to the <b>て-form</b>: 撮って + もいいですか.<br>The dictionary form, た-form, and ます-stem can't connect directly to 「もいいですか」.","n5-g6":"「まだ」(not yet / still not…) calls for a negative: <b>not good at it yet = じょうずじゃありません</b>.<br>An affirmative or past form clashes with 「まだ」; 「だったです」is an invalid conjugation that doesn't exist (the past of a な-adjective is でした).","n4-k5":"<b>送り=おくり</b> (from 送る = to send). <br>「おこり」would be 「怒り/起こり」;「はしり」is 「走り」;「とどけ」is 「届け」— close in meaning (to deliver) but a different kanji. The point being tested is the kun'yomi of 「送」.","n4-k6":"<b>割引=わりびき</b> (discount). <br>「わりひき」misses the rendaku (引き becomes びき when it follows another word);「かつびき」wrongly gives 割 the on'yomi かつ;「わりいん」misreads 引 as いん (the on'yomi いん appears in words like 「引用」).","n4-k7":"<b>地理=ちり</b>. <br>「じり」voices the first syllable wrongly (地 is read じ in 「地震じしん」and「地面じめん」, but ち in 「地理/地図」— N4 loves this contrast);「ちりい」adds a long vowel that isn't there;「とち」is 「土地」.","n4-k8":"<b>港=みなと</b>, the kun'yomi. <br>「こう」is the on'yomi (as in 「空港くうこう」);「みなど」wrongly voices the final syllable;「はま」is 「浜」 (beach/shore).","n4-k9":"<b>紹介=しょうかい</b> (introduction). <br>「しょうがい」has the wrong voicing (that's the reading of 「障害/生涯」);「しょかい」drops the long vowel (「初回しょかい」= the first time, a different word);「そうかい」is 「爽快/総会」.","n4-k10":"<b>壊れた=こわれた</b> (broke / got broken). <br>「やぶれた」is 「破れた」 (torn — paper or cloth);「たおれた」is 「倒れた」 (fell over);「おれた」is 「折れた」 (snapped). All four belong to the \"break\" family, so the question tests which kanji goes with which.","n4-c1":"Missing the train → <b>遅刻</b> (being late). <br>「欠席」means being absent (not going at all), but the sentence says you couldn't catch it = you arrive late;「出席」(attendance) is the opposite;「残業」(overtime) is unrelated.","n4-c2":"「〜てみる」= to try doing something: <b>着てみる = to try on</b>. <br>Asking \"may I try this on?\" is the set phrase in a shop;「買ってみる」sounds odd (buying isn't trying);「washing」and「taking off」aren't what you'd ask about when shopping.","n4-g3":"The sky got dark → \"it started raining\" = 降り<b>はじめ</b>ました. \"ます-stem + はじめる\" = start doing….<br>「降りおわる」isn't used for rain (rain stopping is 「やむ」); 「降りすぎる」= rains too much, which clashes with the \"getting dark\" set-up pointing to a beginning; 「降りなおす」(rain again from the start) isn't natural. The first half 「空が暗くなって」clearly points to something starting.","n4-g4":"\"In order to memorise kanji\" — volitional verb + <b>ために</b>.<br>「ように」attaches to non-volitional verbs or potential forms (覚えられるように ○); 「ばかりに」= just because… (with a negative outcome); 「ことに」is part of 「ことにする」(decide to) and doesn't express purpose on its own.","n4-g5":"A 「と」conditional (as soon as… / whenever…) is followed by a <b>present-tense statement</b>: 押すと、お湯が出ます (press it and hot water comes out — a natural, automatic result).<br>「と」cannot be followed by a one-off past event, a request (てください), or a volitional/suggestion (ましょう) — this is the most important restriction on 「と」.","n4-g6":"\"According to… (source of hearsay)\" = 〜に<b>よると</b> + そうです, a fixed pairing.<br>「について」= about…; 「にとって」= for… / from the standpoint of…; 「にくらべて」= compared with…. When you see the hearsay 「そうです」at the end of the sentence, choose よると.","n4-g7":"Too heavy → <b>can't carry it = negative potential 持てません</b>.<br>「持ちません」only means \"won't carry it\" (a choice); 「持たないです」likewise means not carrying it; 「持ちたくないです」means \"don't want to carry it\" — the sentence is about ability, so the potential form is needed.","n4-g8":"\"Fell asleep with the light still on (that state left unchanged)\" = た-form + <b>まま</b>.<br>「ながら」is doing two things at once (both ongoing); 「あいだ」is a span of time; 「ばかり」is \"just did… / nothing but…\". When a state is left as it is, use まま.","n4-g9":"\"In order to study abroad\": volitional verb + <b>ため</b>に.<br>「ように」takes a non-volitional verb or potential form before it; 「ことに」doesn't express purpose; 「つもり」means intend to (留学するつもりです) but doesn't take 「に + action」.","n4-g10":"\"About to go out\" = dictionary form + <b>ところ</b>です (the action is just about to start).<br>「たばかり」means \"just finished doing\" but needs the た-form (出かけたばかり = just went out); 「ことです」and「あいだです」don't work here. The three ところ stages: するところ (about to do) / しているところ (in the middle of doing) / したところ (just finished doing).","n3-k1":"<b>遅刻=ちこく</b>.<br>「ちごく」wrongly voices the second syllable; 「おそこく」forces the kun-reading おそ (from 遅い) into a compound that takes on-readings; 「ちっこく」adds a geminate っ that isn't there.","n3-k2":"<b>慣れて=なれて</b> (to get used to something).<br>「ならって」is 習って (to learn); 「たおれて」is 倒れて (to fall over); 「つかれて」is 疲れて (to get tired) — a family of look-alike verbs that N3 loves to test.","n3-k3":"<b>状況=じょうきょう</b>.<br>「じょうけい」is 情景; 「じょきょう」drops the long vowel; 「しょうきょう」unvoices the start (状 = じょう, not しょう).","n3-k4":"<b>破片=はへん</b> (a fragment, shard).<br>「はかた」is the place name 博多; 「やぶへん」forces the kun-reading やぶ (from 破る) into an on-reading compound; 「はぺん」turns へ into a p-sound, which doesn't happen here.","n3-k5":"<b>値段=ねだん</b> (price).<br>「ちだん」uses the on-reading for 値 instead of the kun-reading ね; 「ねたん」misses the voicing (段 = だん); 「かかく」is the reading for 価格 — same meaning, but not these two kanji.","n3-k6":"<b>済ませて=すませて</b> (to finish/get something done).<br>From 済む (すむ) → transitive 済ませる (すませる). 「さませて」is 冷ませて/覚ませて; the rest are made-up readings.","n3-k7":"<b>営業=えいぎょう</b>.<br>「えいごう」loses the ぎょ contracted sound; 「えぎょう」drops the long vowel; 「けいぎょう」gets the first kanji wrong (営 = えい).","n3-k8":"<b>指摘=してき</b> (to point out, identify).<br>「しせき」is 史跡; 「じてき」voices the first syllable by mistake; 「しつてき」mixes in 質的.","n3-c1":"Breaking a promise is fixed as <b>約束を破る(やぶる)</b>.<br>壊す is for objects/machines, 折る for bones/branches, 割る for glass/plates — English \"break\" covers them all, but Japanese picks a different verb per noun, and N3 loves testing this.","n3-c2":"For recovering from illness, use <b>治る(なおる)</b>.<br>「直る」 is a homophone but is used when a thing gets fixed (時計が直る); 「戻る」 means to return; 「消える」 means to disappear. Telling 治 and 直 apart is a classic kanji question.","n3-c3":"To rush a piece of work, use <b>急ぐ(いそぐ)</b>: 仕事を急いでいる.<br>「焦る」 is feeling anxious inside (intransitive — it doesn't take を with a task); 「走る」 is to run; 「慌てる」 is to panic. All suggest haste, but \"speed something up\" is 急ぐ.","n3-c4":"Having time to spare = <b>余裕(よゆう)</b>がある.<br>「余分」 is a surplus portion; 「余計」 means unnecessary (余計なお世話); 「予定」 is a scheduled plan, so 「予定があって遅刻しない」 doesn't make sense.","n3-c5":"\"In accordance with…\" = 〜の<b>とおり</b>に: operating it as the manual says.<br>「まま」 is leaving a state unchanged; 「ながら」 is doing two things at once; 「うちに」 is \"while there's still time to.\" Only とおり means \"following/according to.\"","n3-c6":"Fatigue building up: <b>疲れ(つかれ)</b>がたまる is the set phrase.<br>「眠り」 is sleep (you don't say 眠りがたまる); 「痛み」 is pain; 「悩み」 (worries) can take たまる, but what working late into the night directly produces is fatigue.","n3-g1":"\"I read the manual, and *yet* I can't use it\" — the contrastive connector is <b>のに</b>.<br>ので/から/ため all mean \"because\" and link cause to expected result; plugging them in gives the contradictory \"because I read it, I can't use it.\"","n3-g2":"\"Whether or not (they) will come\" = dictionary form + <b>か</b>どうか.<br>「〜かどうか」 is a fixed pattern; only か can sit in that slot, and no other particle works.","n3-p3":"<b>でこぼこ = bumpy, uneven</b> — literally \"not flat\" (平らではない).<br>Narrow, curved, or crowded are all wrong — でこぼこ describes a surface with highs and lows in it.","n3-p4":"<b>めったに + negative = hardly ever</b> (ほとんど…ない).<br>Note it is not 絶対に (never, absolutely not) — めったに leaves room for \"it does happen once in a while.\" It is weaker than 絶対, and that difference in degree is exactly what's being tested.","n3-p5":"<b>あらかじめ = in advance = 前もって</b>. The two are a standard interchangeable pair.<br>あとで (afterwards) points the opposite way; すぐに (right away) and ときどき (sometimes) mean something else entirely.","n3-p6":"<b>検討 = to consider/examine carefully</b> (よく考える). 検討中 = still under review, not yet decided.<br>もう決まった (already decided) is the exact opposite. In a Japanese workplace, 検討します often carries the nuance of \"we'll think about it\" — a polite way of holding back.","n2-k1":"<b>滞る = とどこおる</b> (to be held up, to fall behind).<br>たまる is 溜まる (to pile up); こおる is 凍る (to freeze) — the 帯 element inside 滞 misleads you here; しまる is 閉まる.","n2-k2":"<b>普及 = ふきゅう</b> (to spread widely, become widespread).<br>ふうきゅう adds a long vowel that isn't there; ほきゅう is 補給 (resupply); ふっきゅう is 復旧 (restoration). All three are real words — the test is matching the kanji to the right reading.","n2-k3":"<b>謙虚 = けんきょ</b> (humble, modest).<br>けんそん is 謙遜 (modesty) — almost the same meaning but different kanji, a classic trap; けんちょ is 顕著 (marked, striking); かんきょ has the wrong first syllable.","n2-k4":"<b>妥当 = だとう</b> (appropriate, reasonable, valid).<br>だどう wrongly voices the second character; たとう wrongly leaves the first unvoiced; だった is just the past form of だ, thrown in to confuse you.","n2-k5":"<b>報われる=むくわれる</b> — \"to be rewarded / to pay off.\"<br>「おそわれる」is 襲われる (to be attacked) / 教わる (to be taught); 「すくわれる」is 救われる (to be saved); 「しあわれた」is not a word (it's bait pointing you toward しあわせ).","n2-k6":"<b>矛盾=むじゅん</b>. 矛(む) + 盾(じゅん), from the old story of a man selling a spear that pierces any shield and a shield no spear can pierce.<br>The other options are just plausible-sounding syllables; don't read 矛 as ぼう (that's confusing it with 棒).","n2-c1":"<b>計画を練る(ねる)</b> = to work out a plan carefully — a fixed collocation.<br>練る literally means to knead (dough), extended to mean refining something over and over. 蒸す / 焼く / 煮る stay purely culinary and never take 計画.","n2-c2":"<b>高く評価される</b> = to be highly rated / thought highly of — a fixed collocation.<br>「評判」is a noun meaning \"reputation\" (評判がいい ○, but it isn't a する-verb, so 評判される ×); 「批評/評論」refer to the act of critiquing, so they don't pair with 「高く」.","n2-c3":"<b>締め切り間際(まぎわ)</b> = right before the deadline.<br>「間近(まぢか)」also means \"close at hand,\" but it mostly works as a predicate, as in 「試験が間近だ」; 「締め切り間際の仕事」is the natural pairing. The last two options are made-up compounds.","n2-c4":"<b>徹底的に調べる</b> = to investigate thoroughly — a fixed collocation.<br>「絶対的に」expresses absoluteness (絶対的な信頼) and doesn't modify 調べる; 「積極的に」makes sense grammatically, but the point of investigating a cause is getting to the bottom of it = 徹底的; 「消極的に」says the opposite.","n2-c5":"<b>くよくよする</b> = to brood, to dwell on something. Even after failing, he doesn't dwell on it — he tries again.<br>わくわく = excited with anticipation; にこにこ = smiling brightly; ぺらぺら = fluent (in a language) / flimsy (of paper). Telling these mimetic words apart is a key N2 skill.","n2-c6":"<b>集中力が切れる</b> = your concentration snaps / runs out — a fixed collocation.<br>破れる (paper, cloth) / 割れる (glass) / 折れる (stick-shaped things) — the \"breaking\" family again, each locked to its own kind of object. 「心が折れる」(to be worn down and give up) does exist, but its subject is 心, not 集中力.","n3-g3":"\"I can't solve it (it's beyond my ability)\" = 私<b>には</b>解けません。The negative potential form often pairs with 「には」 to mean \"with my ability, it can't be done.\"<br>「では」 marks a place/scope; 「とは」 marks a definition; 「へは」 marks a direction.","n3-g4":"\"When I was a child\" = 子どもの<b>ころ</b>。The sentence-ending 「〜たものだ」 recalls a past habit with nostalgia, and it fits most naturally with 「ころ」.<br>「うち/あいだ」 would be grammatical as 「子どものうちに」 (while you're still a child), but that means something different; 「ながら」 attaches to verbs.","n3-g5":"Regret / doing something by accident = 〜て<b>しまった</b>: I left my umbrella on the train.<br>「ておいた」 is doing something in advance (deliberately leaving your umbrella? doesn't work); 「てみた」 is trying something out; 「ていった」 shows direction of movement. For regret, it's てしまう.","n3-g6":"Credit for a good outcome uses <b>おかげ</b> (thanks to…): I passed thanks to my senior's advice.<br>「せい」 is for bad outcomes (〜のせいで失敗した); 「ため」 is a neutral cause but loses the note of gratitude; 「わけ」 means reason/logic and doesn't take で to express cause.","n3-g7":"\"It looks like it's about to…\" = ます-stem + <b>そう</b> (appearance): 降りそうな空 (a sky that looks like it's about to rain).<br>「よう/みたい」 attach to the plain form (降るような); 「らしい」 is hearsay-based inference (降るらしい). 「今にも」 (any moment now) is そう's signature partner.","n3-g8":"When speaking to someone outside your company about your own department head, use the <b>humble form おります</b> (the uchi/soto distinction: to outsiders, you lower your own side).<br>「いらっしゃいます」 is honorific — you can't elevate your own boss when talking to outsiders; 「まいります」 is the humble form of \"go/come\"; 「いたします」 is the humble form of 「する」. This is the classic business-keigo test point.","n3-p1":"<b>延期 = to postpone to a later date</b> (あとの日にする).<br>Note that 延期 ≠ 中止: 中止 means calling it off entirely, while 延期 means holding it another day — this contrast is a frequent N3 test point.","n3-p2":"<b>大げさ(おおげさ) = exaggerated</b>, describing something as bigger than it really is.<br>It isn't about being loud or being funny — it's about the content being blown out of proportion.","n5-g7":"「て form + <b>から</b>」= do B after finishing A: finish your homework, then sleep.<br>「まえに」takes the dictionary form (寝る前に);「たら」takes the た form (したら);「ながら」means two actions at once and attaches to the ます stem (しながら).","n5-g8":"For the conditional \"if it rains,\" use the た form + ら: <b>降ったら</b>.<br>「ふって」merely links clauses with no conditional meaning;「ふるたら」is a conjugation error (たら attaches to the た form, not the dictionary form);「ふりながら」means \"while it is raining…,\" which puts the wrong subject in the sentence.","n5-g9":"The thing you want with 「ほしい」 is marked by <b>が</b>: 犬がほしい。<br>Since the thing wanted feels like an object, learners reach for を, but 「ほしい」 is an adjective, so its target is always marked with が — the same family as 「好き/きらい/じょうず」.","n5-g10":"\"Please don't do…\" = ない form + でください: 食べ<b>ない</b>でください。<br>「なくて」is the negative て form (giving a reason or linking clauses) and cannot take でください;「ません/なかった」are the polite and past forms, neither of which fits here.","n4-k1":"<b>急行=きゅうこう</b> (express train).<br>「きゅうぎょう」reads 行 as ぎょう (that reading appears in words like 「行事ぎょうじ」);「きこう」loses the contracted long vowel;「きゅこう」drops the う (きゅ→きゅう).","n4-k2":"<b>運転=うんてん</b> (to drive).<br>「うんでん」wrongly voices the consonant;「うんてい」has the wrong final vowel;「うてん」drops the ん (「雨天うてん」 is a different word, meaning rainy weather).","n4-k3":"<b>夕飯=ゆうはん</b> (evening meal).<br>「ゆうぱん」wrongly uses the p-sound (はん, not ぱん);「せきはん」is the reading of 「赤飯」 (red-bean rice);「ゆはん」is missing the long vowel.","n4-k4":"<b>説明=せつめい</b> (explanation).<br>「せいめい」is the reading of 「生命/姓名」;「せつめ」is missing the long vowel;「せっめい」puts the double consonant in the wrong place (つ+め does not form a geminate).","n2-g1":"<b>次第(しだい)では</b> = depending on ~: depending on the weather, the match may be cancelled.<br>「限りでは」= as far as ~ goes (私の知る限りでは); 「向き」= suited to (初心者向き); 「気味」= a slight touch of (風邪気味).","n2-g2":"\"Busy, <b>yet</b> still willing\" — the written-style concessive <b>にもかかわらず</b> (despite ~, even so).<br>「おかげで」= thanks to (positive result, not concessive); 「あまり」= so much that ~; 「ばかりか」= not only ~ but also. None of these fit the contrastive direction.","n2-g3":"<b>〜以上(は)</b> = now that ~ / since ~ (carrying duty or responsibility): now that you've taken it on, you should see it through.<br>「以来」= ever since; 「上に」= on top of that; 「末に」= after ~, finally. The sentence-final 「べきだ」 is the classic partner of 「以上」.","n2-g4":"<b>〜ば〜ほど</b> = the more ~, the more ~: the more you listen, the less you understand. The 「聞けば聞く」 already supplies the ば-form, so ほど must follow.<br>だけ / ばかり / ぐらい cannot build the \"the more ~, the more ~\" pattern.","n2-g5":"<b>時間どおり</b> = on time (exactly as scheduled). 「〜のことだから」 = judging from what that person is like: someone like him is sure to arrive on time.<br>「ながら」= although; 「がち」= tends to / often does (遅れがち); 「っぽい」= has a ~ feel to it (子どもっぽい).","n2-g6":"<b>〜あってこそ</b> = only because ~ exists (does this result follow): passing was possible precisely because of the teacher's guidance.<br>「からこそ」 also stresses a cause, but it attaches to a clause (指導があったからこそ ○); to attach directly to a noun, use 「あってこそ」. 「だけこそ / ばこそ」 are ungrammatical here (ばこそ needs a ば-form: あればこそ).","n2-p1":"<b>あいにく = unfortunately / as bad luck would have it = 運悪く</b>.<br>「偶然」= by chance (neutral); 「幸い」= luckily (the opposite); 「案の定」= just as expected. あいにく carries the regretful \"bad timing, sorry to say\" nuance.","n2-p2":"<b>ほぼ = roughly / almost all = だいたい</b>.<br>「完全に」 is too strong (ほぼ means \"just short of完全\"); 「少し」 is too weak; the last option means the opposite.","n2-p3":"<b>うつむく = to look down / hang one's head</b> = 下を向く.<br>Its opposite is 「あおむく」 (to look up); 「横になる」 means to lie down, and 「振り向く」 means to turn around and look back — a set of direction verbs, tested on precise correspondence.","n2-p4":"The idiom <b>骨が折れる = to take great effort, to be tough (大変だ)</b>.<br>It has nothing to do with actually breaking a bone! Idioms are tested with literal-reading traps: 「けがをする」 is exactly that literal trap, and 「簡単だ」 is the opposite.","n2-p5":"<b>もっともな = reasonable, perfectly understandable (当然で正しい)</b>.<br>Trap: 「もっとも(尤も)」 ≠ 「最も(もっとも)」! Same sound, different kanji — 「一番/最も」 is the literal trap, and 「疑わしい」 is the opposite.","n1-k1":"<b>錯綜 = さくそう</b> (tangled, intricate).<br>「さくしゅう」 is meant to pull you toward 「搾取」; 「そうさく」 reverses the order, giving 「創作/捜索」; 「さっそう」 is 「颯爽」.","n1-k2":"<b>暴く = あばく</b> (to expose, to uncover).<br>「さばく」 is 「裁く (to judge)/捌く (to handle)」; 「くじく」 is 「挫く (to sprain, to crush)」; 「うつむく」 is 「俯く (to look down)」.","n1-k3":"<b>頓挫 = とんざ</b> (to be derailed, to come to a standstill).<br>挫 is read ざ here (rendaku); 「とんさ/とんそ」 get the ending wrong; 「どんざ」 wrongly voices the opening (頓 = とん, as in 「頓知 とんち」).","n1-k4":"<b>猶予 = ゆうよ</b> (postponement, grace period): 執行猶予 (suspended sentence), 猶予なく (without delay).<br>「ゆうじょ」 is meant to pull you toward 「宥恕」; 「ようよ/ゆよ」 put the long vowel in the wrong place.","n1-k5":"<b>廃れる = すたれる</b> (to fall out of use, to go out of fashion).<br>「はいれて」 forces 廃's on'yomi はい into a kun'yomi slot; 「さびれる」 is 「寂れる (to decline, to become deserted)」 — close in meaning but a different kanji, a classic trap; 「つぶれる」 is 「潰れる (to go under, to collapse)」.","n1-k6":"<b>醸す = かもす</b>: 物議を醸す = to stir up controversy (a fixed idiom). 醸 originally means \"to brew\" (sake), extended to \"to brew up / give rise to\" something intangible.<br>「じょうした」 forces the on-reading じょう onto the verb, which doesn't exist; 「もよおした」 is 催した (to hold/host an event).","n1-c1":"<b>〜にかかっている</b> = to depend on…: success or failure hinges on securing the funding. A fixed pattern.<br>「たよる」 is to rely on (a person); 「もたれる」 is to lean against (physically) / feel heavy in the stomach; 「ゆだねる」 is to entrust something to someone (判断を委ねる ○, but the pattern needs を〜に委ねる, and there's no を here).","n1-c2":"<b>異議を唱える(となえる)</b> = to raise an objection, a fixed collocation (also 「異を唱える」).<br>The other three are made-up compounds that don't exist — N1 loves using \"words that look like words\" as distractors.","n1-c3":"<b>遅々(ちち)として進まない</b> = to make painfully slow progress, a fixed idiom.<br>「漠々」 is vast and hazy; 「浩々」 is vast and expansive; 「転々」 is moving from one to the next (職を転々とする). Pairing a reduplicated compound with として is a classic N1 test point.","n1-c4":"<b>涙を誘う(さそう)</b> = to move someone to tears, a fixed collocation.<br>「招く」 goes with misunderstandings/accidents (誤解を招く); 「呼ぶ」 goes with topics/emotion (感動を呼ぶ ○ — but 「涙」 fixedly takes 誘う); 「引く」 goes with attention/colds. Knowing which near-synonym takes which noun is the core of N1.","n1-c5":"<b>こだわる</b> = to fixate on: fixating too much on details makes you lose sight of the essentials.<br>「たずさわる(携わる)」 is to be engaged in (work) (仕事に携わる); 「さしかかる」 is to come up to / reach a point (峠に差し掛かる); 「うけたまわる(承る)」 is the humble form of 聞く/受ける.","n1-g1":"<b>〜なしには…ない</b> = without …, cannot …: without the president's approval, we can't move forward.<br>「なしでは」 also exists (なしでは生きられない), but 「なしには」 is the standard partner for a negative potential like 「進められない」; とは and かは don't form this pattern.","n1-g2":"<b>〜なくして(は)…ない</b> = without …, there would be no …: without his hard work, this success wouldn't have happened.<br>「ならでは」 = unique to / only possible with (positive traits: 京都ならではの風情); 「をもって」 = by means of / as of (a method or deadline); 「にひきかえ」 = in contrast to.","n1-g3":"The answer is <b>をよそに</b> = ignoring / in disregard of… (the government pushed through the reform even though the public was hoping for something else).<br>「を背に」 means \"with … at one's back / carrying,\" and 期待を背に is grammatical — but it's positive (\"buoyed by expectations\"), which clashes with 断行 (forcing something through over objections), so をよそに (\"in defiance of\") fits best. 「を機に」 = taking … as an opportunity; 「を皮切りに」 = starting with …. ※ 断行 already implies brushing objections aside, so \"ignoring expectations\" is the natural reading.","n1-g4":"<b>にもかかわらず</b> = in spite of / even though (after being warned that many times, he still made the same mistake). The sentence-final 「とは」 expresses surprise or displeasure.<br>「がゆえに」 = precisely because (straightforward cause and effect); 「とあって」 = given that … (a special circumstance); 「べく」 = in order to (marks the purpose of the following action).","n1-g5":"<b>〜をおして</b> = pushing through (hardship or physical condition): attending the meeting despite being ill. 「病を押して」 is a set collocation.<br>「をおいて」 = other than … (彼をおいて適任者はいない); 「をもって」 = by means of / as of …; 「をかねて」 = doubling as … (散歩をかねて). おして vs. おいて — one letter apart, and a classic N1 trap.","n1-g6":"<b>〜のことだから</b> = judging from what that person is like (their character or ability): with his wealth of experience, he should get through this. <br>「ときたら」 = as for … (used to complain); 「ともなると」 = once it reaches the point of …; 「はおろか」 = let alone … (not even …). When the sentence ends in 「だろう」 — a personal guess about someone — のことだから is the match.","n1-p1":"<b>しぶしぶ = reluctantly = 嫌々(いやいや)ながら</b>. It's the reduplicated form of 渋る (to be unwilling, to hesitate).<br>「喜んで」 is the opposite (gladly); 「すぐに」 is about speed; 「こっそり」 means secretly.","n1-p2":"The idiom <b>暗礁に乗り上げる = to run aground = to hit a deadlock (行き詰まる)</b>.<br>「白紙に戻る」 means going back to square one — a deadlock is being stuck, not yet reset to zero, so the degree differs; 「順調に」 is the opposite (smoothly).","n1-p3":"<b>極めて = extremely = 非常に</b> (a written-style adverb at the top of the intensity scale).<br>「わりと」 = fairly, rather (a middling degree); 「意外に」 = unexpectedly; 「次第に」 = gradually.","n1-p4":"<b>円滑(えんかつ)に = smoothly, without friction = スムーズに</b>.<br>「ゆっくり」 means slowly (smooth ≠ slow); 「部分的に」 is about scope; 「強引に」 means forcibly — the opposite in feel, since forcing something through is exactly what 円滑 is not.","n1-p5":"The idiom <b>根も葉もない = completely groundless, utterly made up</b> (全く事実ではない). Literally \"no root and no leaves\" = nothing factual to stand on.<br>\"Partly true\" is the trap answer; \"there is evidence\" is the exact opposite.","n5-o1":"Full sentence: これは<b>母が作ったケーキ</b>です (This is the cake my mother made). Order: 母 → が → <b>作った</b> → ケーキ, so ★ = slot 3 = <b>作った</b>.<br>Key point: the plain past form 作った modifies the noun ケーキ directly (noun-modifying clause), and the clause subject 母が sits at the very front of that clause.","n5-o2":"Full sentence: 駅の前に<b>新しいパン屋ができました</b> (A new bakery opened in front of the station). Order: 新しい → パン屋 → <b>が</b> → できました, so ★ = <b>が</b>.<br>The i-adjective 新しい attaches straight to the noun; できる here means \"come into existence / appear,\" and what appears is marked with が.","n5-o3":"Full sentence: 日本語の<b>勉強は難しいですが、とても</b>おもしろいです (Studying Japanese is hard, but it's very interesting). Order: 勉強は → 難しいです → <b>が</b> → とても, so ★ = <b>が</b> (the contrastive が).<br>〜ですが = \"although…, …\"; とても modifies the おもしろい that follows.","n5-o4":"Full sentence: きのう<b>買った本をもう読みました</b> (I've already read the book I bought yesterday). Order: 買った → 本を → <b>もう</b> → 読みました, so ★ = <b>もう</b>.<br>きのう買った modifies 本; the adverb もう (already) goes right before the verb.","n4-o1":"Full sentence: <b>先生に教えてもらったおかげで</b>、試験に合格できました (Thanks to my teacher teaching me, I passed the exam). Order: 先生に → 教えて → <b>もらった</b> → おかげで, so ★ = <b>もらった</b>.<br>〜てもらう = have someone do something for you (the doer takes に); た-form + おかげで = \"thanks to…\".","n4-o2":"Full sentence: 雨が<b>降りそうだから、傘を持って行ったほうが</b>いいですよ (It looks like rain, so you'd better take an umbrella). Order: 降りそうだから → 傘を → <b>持って行った</b> → ほうが, so ★ = <b>持って行った</b>.<br>た-form + ほうがいい gives advice; そうだ (appearance) + から = \"because it looks like…\".","n4-o3":"Full sentence: この町は<b>静かで住みやすいので人気</b>です (This town is quiet and easy to live in, so it's popular). Order: 静かで → 住みやすい → <b>ので</b> → 人気, so ★ = <b>ので</b>.<br>The na-adjective te-form 静かで links the two qualities; ます-stem + やすい = \"easy to…\"; ので introduces the reason.","n4-o4":"Full sentence: 日本に<b>来たばかりのころは、言葉がわからなくて</b>大変でした (When I had just come to Japan, I couldn't understand the language and had a hard time). Word order: 来たばかりの→ころは→<b>言葉が</b>→わからなくて, ★=<b>言葉が</b>.<br>「たばかり」= have just done something; 「なくて」links the clause as the reason for what follows.","n3-o1":"Full sentence: 彼は<b>忙しいのに、手伝いに来て</b>くれた (Even though he was busy, he came to help me). Word order: 忙しい→のに→<b>手伝いに</b>→来て, ★=<b>手伝いに</b>.<br>「のに」= even though...; ます-stem +「に来る」= come in order to do something; 「てくれる」= does it for my benefit.","n3-o2":"Full sentence: 天気が<b>よければ、山の上から富士山が見える</b>はずだ (If the weather is good, you should be able to see 富士山 from the top of the mountain). Word order: よければ→山の上から→<b>富士山が</b>→見える, ★=<b>富士山が</b>.<br>「ば」form marks the condition; the thing seen with the potential verb 「見える」takes が, not を; 「はずだ」= it ought to be so.","n3-o3":"Full sentence: 説明書に<b>書いてあるとおりに組み立てたのに</b>、動かない (I assembled it exactly as written in the manual, and yet it doesn't work). Word order: 書いてある→とおりに→<b>組み立てた</b>→のに, ★=<b>組み立てた</b>.<br>「てある」= a state someone has put in place (here: it stands written); 「とおりに」= just as / following; 「のに」= and yet, contrary to expectation.","n3-o4":"Full sentence: 彼女は<b>歌手としてだけでなく、女優としても</b>活躍している (She is active not only as a singer but as an actress too). Word order: 歌手→として→<b>だけでなく</b>→女優としても, ★=<b>だけでなく</b>.<br>「として」= in the role of / as; 「だけでなく〜も」= not only... but also...","n2-o1":"Full sentence: 締め切りに<b>間に合わせるためには、今日中に資料を完成させる</b>しかない (To make the deadline, there's no choice but to finish the documents today). Word order: 間に合わせるためには→今日中に→<b>資料を</b>→完成させる, ★=<b>資料を</b>.<br>「ためには」= in order to..., you have to...; 「しかない」= there is no option but to.","n2-o2":"Full sentence: 彼の提案は、<b>コストの面から言うと、現実的とは</b>言いがたい (Speaking from the cost side, his proposal is hard to call realistic). Word order: コストの→面から言うと→<b>現実的</b>→とは, ★=<b>現実的</b>.<br>「〜から言うと」= judging from / speaking in terms of...; 「とは言いがたい」= it's hard to say that... (an N2 pattern).","n2-o3":"Full sentence: あの店は<b>味はもちろん、サービスにおいても</b>評判が高い (That restaurant has a great reputation for its service as well as, needless to say, its taste). Word order: 味は→もちろん→<b>サービス</b>→においても, ★=<b>サービス</b>.<br>「はもちろん」= ... goes without saying; 「において」= in / with regard to (formal, written style).","n2-o4":"Full sentence: 社会人になった<b>以上、自分の行動に責任を持つ</b>べきだ (Now that you've become a working adult, you should take responsibility for your own actions). Order: 以上→自分の行動に→<b>責任を</b>→持つ, ★=<b>責任を</b>.<br>「た-form+以上」= now that / given that…; 「責任を持つ」is a set phrase (to take responsibility); 「べきだ」= ought to.","n1-o1":"Full sentence: 環境問題は、<b>一国のみならず、世界全体で取り組む</b>べき課題だ (Environmental problems are an issue that not just one country but the whole world should tackle). Order: 一国のみならず→世界全体で→<b>取り組む</b>→べき, ★=<b>取り組む</b>.<br>「のみならず」= not only… (formal/written); 「べき+noun」= a … that should be done.","n1-o2":"Full sentence: あの政治家は、<b>批判を浴びるや否や、前言を</b>撤回した (The moment that politician came under fire, he retracted what he had said). Order: 批判を→浴びる→<b>や否や</b>→前言を, ★=<b>や否や</b>.<br>「dictionary form+や否や」= the instant… , … (N1); 「批判を浴びる」= to come under criticism, a set collocation.","n1-o3":"Full sentence: <b>実力もさることながら、運も味方した</b>からこその優勝だ (His skill goes without saying, but the win came precisely because luck was on his side too). Order: 実力も→さることながら→<b>運も</b>→味方した, ★=<b>運も</b>.<br>「もさることながら」= … goes without saying, and what's more… (N1); 「からこそ」= precisely because.","n1-o4":"Full sentence: 調査の<b>結果いかんでは、計画の見直しも</b>あり得る (Depending on the results of the survey, the plan may even be reconsidered). Order: 結果→いかんでは→<b>計画の</b>→見直しも, ★=<b>計画の</b>.<br>「いかんでは」= depending on… (N1, written style); 「あり得る」= is possible / could happen.","n5-p1":"<b>ゆうべ(昨夜) = last night = きのうの夜</b>.<br>The time-word family: けさ = this morning, おととい = the day before yesterday, ゆうべ = last night — easy to mix up, so learn them one by one.","n5-p2":"<b>やさしい(易しい) = easy = かんたん</b>.<br>Watch out for 「優しい」(kind, gentle) — same sound, different word; 「むずかしい」(difficult) is the exact opposite.","n5-p3":"<b>まいばん(毎晩) = every night = 夜はいつも</b>.<br>毎〜 = every…, a fixed regular frequency; 「ときどき」(sometimes) and 「週に一度」(once a week) are not frequent enough.","n5-p4":"<b>そうじする(掃除)= to clean = to make a room tidy (きれいにする)</b>.<br>「新しく/広く/明るく する」mean to make something new / bigger / brighter — none of them mean cleaning.","n4-p1":"<b>まじめに = seriously, earnestly ≒ 一生懸命 (with all one's effort)</b>.<br>「ときどき」is frequency, 「ゆっくり」is speed, 「楽しそうに」is mood — none of them express \"in earnest\".","n4-p2":"<b>だんだん = gradually = 少しずつ</b>.<br>「急に」means suddenly (the opposite pace); 「とても」is degree; 「たまに」means occasionally.","n4-p3":"<b>簡単に = easily, without trouble ≒ 楽(らく)に</b>.<br>「丁寧に」is carefully; 「自由に」is freely; 「特別に」is specially — only 「楽に」carries the sense of \"with little effort\".","n4-p4":"<b>込む(こむ)= to be crowded</b>: a crowded train = lots of people on board.<br>「ぜんぜんいない」pairs with 空いている (すいている), the exact opposite; the other two are about delays or departures, which is unrelated.","n5-k11":"<b>外国 = がいこく</b>.<br>「かいこく」has no voicing at the start (開国 is a different word); 「げこく」uses the wrong reading here — 外 does have げ in 外科(げか), but the N5 word to remember is がいこく; 「そとくに」forces the kun reading そと where an on reading belongs.","n5-k12":"<b>半分 = はんぶん</b>. After ん, ふ becomes ぶ (rendaku).<br>「はんふん」leaves out that voicing (compare 一分(いっぷん), where the sound shifts to ぷ after a small つ — these sound changes in 分 are exactly what's being tested); 「なかぶん / はんべん」are just made-up combinations.","n5-c9":"To <b>put sugar in</b> coffee = (さとうを)入れて.<br>「出して」means to take out (the opposite direction); 「切って」is to cut; 「立って」is to stand — with the pattern 「さとうを…に」only 入れる works.","n5-c10":"A sensible reason not to buy something: <b>たかい(expensive)</b>.<br>「やすい(cheap)/あたらしい(new)/かるい(light)」are all selling points, so \"...so I won't buy it\" makes no sense. This item tests whether the cause before 「から」 actually fits the result after it.","n5-g11":"Use <b>に</b> to mark where something exists: へやにテレビがあります (there is a TV in the room).<br>Compare 「で」, which marks where an action takes place (へやで見ます). 「あります/います」 (existence) take に; action verbs take で. This contrast is a guaranteed N5 question.","n5-g12":"「わたし<b>の</b>です」= it's mine. 「の」 can stand alone meaning \"...'s (one)\", with the following noun left out.<br>「が/を/に」 cannot attach to です and form a sentence.","n4-k11":"<b>特急=とっきゅう</b>. 「特(とく)」+「急(きゅう)」 undergoes gemination (a small っ) → とっきゅう.<br>「とくきゅう」 lacks the gemination (same point as 学校 がっこう); 「とっきゅ」 drops the long vowel; 「どっきゅう」 wrongly voices the first syllable.","n4-k12":"<b>屋上=おくじょう</b> (rooftop) — both characters take their on'yomi reading.<br>「や」 is the kun'yomi of 屋 (本屋 ほんや) and 「うえ」 the kun'yomi of 上, so 「やじょう/おくうえ/やうえ」 are all mismatched on/kun combinations.","n4-c9":"<b>タクシーを呼ぶ</b> = to call a taxi — a set collocation.<br>「取る」 is to take; 「持つ」 is to hold; 「かける」 is for phone calls (電話をかける). For calling a cab, only 呼ぶ works.","n4-c10":"When a machine isn't working properly: <b>調子(ちょうし)が悪い</b>.<br>「気分が悪い」 means a person feels unwell; 「都合が悪い」 means the timing is inconvenient; 「様子」 means appearance/state (it doesn't fit 「が悪いので直す」). Telling 調子/気分/都合 apart is a frequent N4 test point.","n4-g11":"The teacher is coming — use the honorific <b>いらっしゃいます</b> (the respectful form of 来る) for a teacher's actions.<br>「まいります/おります/いたします」 are all humble forms that lower the speaker, so using them for your teacher points the respect the wrong way. Getting this direction right — honorific vs. humble — is the first hurdle in keigo.","n2-k8":"<b>貢献=こうけん</b> (contribution).<br>「こうかん」 is 交換 (exchange) / 好感 (good impression); 「こうげん」 is 高原 (plateau); 「くけん」 has the wrong first syllable — 貢 is read こう here (the ぐ in 年貢 ねんぐ is an exception).","n2-c7":"Talks breaking down is <b>交渉が決裂(けつれつ)する</b> — a fixed collocation.<br>「破裂」 is for physical bursting (パイプが破裂); 「分裂」 is an organisation or cell splitting; 「決壊」 is a levee giving way. English lumps these together as \"burst/break down,\" but each Japanese word takes its own fixed subject.","n2-c8":"<b>板挟み(いたばさみ)に立たされる</b> = to be caught between two sides (torn between work and childcare).<br>「中断/停滞」 mean a process has stopped; 「迷惑」 means trouble or nuisance. Only 板挟み expresses being squeezed between two conflicting demands.","n2-g7":"<b>た-form + 末(すえ)に</b> = after (a long period of) …, finally … — the result is neutral or positive: after practising every day, they finally took the title.<br>「あげく」 also means \"in the end,\" but it is <b>fixed to negative outcomes</b> (悩んだあげく、あきらめた), so it can't be used for this happy ending. 「ばかりに」 = simply because … (with a bad result); 「ように」 doesn't express a process leading to an outcome. The positive/negative split between 末に and あげく is a classic N2 point.","n2-g8":"<b>わけがない</b> = there's no way …: someone that serious couldn't possibly be lying (a strong denial of possibility).<br>「わけにはいかない」 = can't (for moral or social reasons) — うそをつくわけにはいかない means \"I can't bring myself to lie,\" a different subject's viewpoint. 「ないことはない」 = it's not that … isn't possible (weak affirmation); 「どころではない」 = this is no time for …. Telling these four わけ/ところ patterns apart is the big N2 hurdle.","n1-k7":"<b>示唆=しさ</b> (to hint at, to suggest): 示唆に富む = full of insight.<br>「じさ」 is 時差 (time difference); 「しそ」 points to 思想 しそう (ideology) or 紫蘇 (shiso); 「じしゃ」 wrongly voices the first syllable.","n1-k8":"<b>逝去=せいきょ</b> (the polite word for someone's passing, used of others).<br>「せいきょう」 is 盛況 (great success, a full house); 「そっきょ」 is bait for 即興 そっきょう (improvisation); 「いきょ」 is 依拠 (to be based on). ※ For a death in your own family you say 死去 しきょ; for someone else's you use the honorific ご逝去. This usage distinction is itself an N1 test point.","n1-c6":"<b>並々(なみなみ)ならぬ</b> = extraordinary, out of the ordinary: realising a dream through extraordinary effort — a set phrase.<br>「延々」 = on and on, endlessly; 「重々」 = fully, well aware (重々承知); 「深々」 = deeply (頭を深々と下げる). Reduplicated adverbs plus their fixed collocations are a standard N1 test point.","n5-g14":"For comparisons like \"taller than my older brother,\" use <b>より</b>: 兄よりせが高い。<br>「ほど」needs a negative (兄ほど高くない = not as tall as my brother);「と」means \"and\";「か」means \"or\". より (positive comparison) and ほど (negative comparison) are a matched pair — learn them together.","n5-o5":"Full sentence: わたしは毎朝<b>パンを食べてから、学校へ</b>行きます (Every morning I eat bread and then go to school). Word order: パンを → 食べて → <b>から</b> → 学校へ, so ★ = <b>から</b>.<br>「て form + から」= after doing….","n5-o6":"Full sentence: 日曜日は<b>そうじをして、それからせんたくを</b>します (On Sunday I clean, and after that do the laundry). ★ = the third blank = <b>それから</b>.<br>「それから」= \"and then\"; it follows a て form to link two actions in sequence.","n5-p5":"<b>おととい(一昨日) = the day before yesterday = two days ago (二日前)</b>.<br>Time words: きのう = 1 day ago, おととい = 2 days ago, さきおととい = 3 days ago. Memorise them as one ordered set and you won't mix them up.","n5-p6":"<b>有名(ゆうめい) = famous = everyone knows it</b>.<br>A shop being big, new, or quiet could all be *reasons* it became famous, but in meaning 有名 equals only 「広く知られている」(widely known).","n4-k13":"<b>予習 = よしゅう</b> (preparing/previewing a lesson beforehand).<br>「よしゅ」drops the long vowel;「ようしゅう」— 予 is not read よう;「よじゅう」has the wrong voicing. Learn it alongside its opposite, 復習(ふくしゅう) \"review\".","n4-k14":"<b>親切 = しんせつ</b> (kind).<br>「しんぜつ」wrongly voices the せ;「おやぎり」forces the kun readings (親 おや + 切 きり);「ちかせつ」confuses 親 with 近(ちか).","n4-c11":"To turn the temperature down = <b>温度を下げる</b>.<br>「消して」takes the air conditioner itself as its object (エアコンを消す ○ / 温度を消す ✕);「閉めて」is for doors and windows;「落として」means to drop/lower something like brightness or volume (音量を落とす ○, but it doesn't pair naturally with 温度).","n4-g12":"<b>てばかりいる</b> = \"do nothing but…\", \"always be …ing\" (carries a note of criticism): my little brother plays video games all day long.<br>「だけ」= only (neutral, and it can't attach in this ています slot); 「しか」must be paired with a negative; 「ほど」marks degree.","n3-k9":"<b>土産=みやげ</b> is a 熟字訓 — a reading assigned to the whole compound, so you can't split it kanji by kanji. Usually said as 「おみやげ」.<br>「どさん」forces the on'yomi (土産どさん only survives in a handful of words like 土産物どさんぶつ; normally it's みやげ); the rest are made-up readings.","n3-k10":"<b>留守=るす</b> (not at home / out).<br>留 has two on'yomi, りゅう and る — here it's る, so 「りゅうしゅ」and the like are just the common on'yomi forced onto it. Learn it together with 「留守番るすばん」(house-sitting, minding the house).","n3-c7":"<b>第一印象(だいいちいんしょう)</b> = first impression, a set phrase.<br>「感想」is your own thoughts/impressions of something; 「印刷」is printing (a look-alike trap: 印○刷 ≠ 印○象); 「様子」is the state or appearance of something.","n3-c8":"A clash of opinions: <b>意見がぶつかる</b> (opinions collide = they conflict), a fixed collocation.<br>「はなれる」is to separate/move away; 「はずれる」is to come off or to miss the mark (予想がはずれる); 「たおれる」is to fall over.","n3-g9":"Transitive verb in て form + <b>ある</b> = someone did it and the result is left in place: the window has been left open (by someone). The following sentence \"who opened it?\" points to a deliberate human action → てある.<br>「ている」shows a state only with intransitive verbs (窓が開いている ○ — note it's 開いて, not 開けて); 「ておく」is doing something in advance; 「てみる」is trying something out. The 開けてある / 開いている contrast is a core N3 test point.","n3-g10":"ない form + <b>ように</b> = \"so that … doesn't happen\" (a non-volitional or negative purpose): I went to bed early so I wouldn't oversleep.<br>「ために」needs an affirmative volitional verb in front of it (合格するために ○ / しないために ✕ unnatural); 「ことに」doesn't express purpose; 「ばかりに」= \"just because …\" (leading to a bad outcome). The division of labour between ように and ために is a guaranteed N3 question.","n2-k7":"<b>募集=ぼしゅう</b> (recruitment, calling for applicants).<br>「ぼしゅ」drops the long vowel; 「むしゅう」is 「無臭」(odourless); 「ぼじゅう」wrongly voices the second syllable. Learn the verb 「募る(つのる)」alongside it.","n4-c12":"You apologise because you <b>forgot the promise (約束を忘れて)</b>. 「てしまう」 signals regret, so it flags something negative — a cue that the blank must be a mistake.<br>「守って (kept it)」 is a good thing, so no apology is needed; 「決めて (decided)/覚えて (remembered)」 don't make sense before an apology.","n4-g13":"Experience pattern: た-form + <b>ことがある</b> = have (once) done…: I've been to Kyoto once.<br>「たところ」 = have just finished doing; 「たばかり」 = just did… (it doesn't take があります); 「つもり」 = intend to (and attaches to the dictionary form).","n4-g14":"<b>場合(ばあい)は</b> = in the case that…/when…: when you don't know how to use it, please ask a store clerk.<br>「ように」 = so that/like; 「ままに」 = leaving it as it is; 「とおりに」 = just as/according to. For \"if you run into this situation,\" use ばあい.","n4-o5":"Full sentence: 兄は<b>車を買うために、お金をためて</b>います (My older brother is saving money in order to buy a car). Order: 車を→買うために→<b>お金を</b>→ためて, so ★ = <b>お金を</b>.<br>「ために」 takes the purpose before it (buying a car) and what you do for that purpose after it (saving money).","n4-o6":"Full sentence: 手を<b>きれいに洗ってから、ごはんを</b>食べましょう (Let's wash our hands well and then eat). Order: きれいに→洗って→<b>から</b>→ごはんを, so ★ = <b>から</b>.<br>The adverb 「きれいに」 modifies 洗う and must come right before the verb; 「てから」 = after doing….","n4-p5":"<b>最初に = first (of all) = はじめに</b>.<br>「さいごに (last)」 is the opposite; 「つぎに (next)/あとで (later)」 put it at a different point in the sequence. 最初 ⇔ 最後 is a standard antonym pair.","n4-p6":"<b>危険(きけん) = dangerous = あぶない</b>. Swapping a Sino-Japanese word for its native-Japanese equivalent is a classic 言い換え item.<br>「きたない」 is dirty; 「うるさい」 is noisy; 「せまい」 is cramped — all possible reasons you wouldn't want to go in, but none of them mean 危険.","n3-k11":"<b>偶然 = ぐうぜん</b> (by chance, coincidentally).<br>「ぐぜん」 drops the long vowel; 「ごうぜん」 is 「傲然」 (haughty); 「ぐうねん」 has the wrong final reading (然 = ぜん).","n3-g12":"<b>反面(はんめん)</b> = \"on the other hand / the flip side\": the flip side of being convenient is that it tires your eyes easily. A pattern for stating the good and bad sides together.<br>「おかげ」= thanks to (good outcome);「せい」= because of / blame (bad outcome);「とおり」= just as / following — none of them express two sides of one thing.","n3-g13":"<b>はずです</b> = \"should be / ought to be\" (an inference with grounds): Sato should be out right now.<br>「べき」= should do (obligation);「わけ」= reason / in other words;「ため」= for the sake of / because. はず for inference, べき for obligation — this pair is a guaranteed exam item.","n3-g14":"<b>だれでも</b> = \"anyone at all\" (affirmative): anyone can make it.<br>「だれも」takes a negative (だれも作れない);「だれか」= someone;「さえ」needs a concrete noun (子どもでさえ). Question word + でも = fully affirmative \"any-\".","n3-o5":"Full sentence: 弟は<b>宿題をしないで、ゲームばかり</b>している (My little brother plays games all the time instead of doing his homework). Word order: 宿題を→しないで→<b>ゲーム</b>→ばかり, ★=<b>ゲーム</b>.<br>「ないで」= without doing…/instead of doing…;「noun+ばかり」= nothing but.","n3-o6":"Full sentence: 彼女は<b>日本語が話せるだけでなく、韓国語も</b>ぺらぺらだ (She not only speaks Japanese, she's fluent in Korean too). Word order: 日本語が→話せる→<b>だけでなく</b>→韓国語も, ★=<b>だけでなく</b>.<br>「だけでなく〜も」= not only… but also….","n3-p7":"<b>突然(とつぜん) = suddenly = 急に</b>.<br>「だんだん」= gradually (the opposite pace);「ときどき」= sometimes (frequency);「やっと」= finally (after waiting).","n3-p8":"<b>正直(しょうじき) = honest = doesn't tell lies (うそをつかない)</b>.<br>Hardworking, loud-voiced and clever are all other virtues — 正直 matches only \"honest, doesn't deceive\".","n2-k9":"<b>傾向 = けいこう</b> (tendency, trend).<br>「けいきょう」wrongly adds a contracted sound to the second character;「かたむこう」mixes in the kun reading かたむ(く) of 傾;「けんこう」is 健康 — a real-word trap sharing the same first sound.","n3-k12":"<b>豊富=ほうふ</b>(abundant, plentiful).<br>「ほうぶ」wrongly voices the second syllable; 「ゆたふ」drags in the kun reading ゆた(か) of 豊; 「ほふ」drops the long vowel. Note 「抱負ほうふ」(one's ambition) is a homophone — tell them apart by context.","n3-k13":"<b>比較=ひかく</b>(comparison).<br>「ひこう」is 「飛行」(flight); 「びかく」voices the first syllable, which 比 never takes here; 「ひきょう」is 「卑怯」(cowardly) or 「秘境」(unexplored region) — a classic trap of words sharing the same first kana.","n3-k14":"<b>迷惑=めいわく</b>(trouble, nuisance — causing bother to others).<br>「みわく」is 「魅惑」(fascination) — a real word with a similar-looking kanji, so it's the classic trap; 「めわく」drops the long vowel; 「まいわく」gets the first syllable wrong.","n3-c9":"Giving a presentation in front of people → <b>緊張(きんちょう)している</b> (to be nervous, tense).<br>「心配」is to worry (about something else); 「遠慮」is holding back out of politeness; 「我慢」is enduring or putting up with something — none of them describe the physical jitters you get before going on stage.","n3-c10":"Cutting the electricity bill = <b>電気代を節約(せつやく)する</b>.<br>「貯金」is saving money (putting the money itself aside); 「削除」is deleting (files or text); 「遠慮」is holding back politely. 「節約」specifically means using less of something to economise.","n3-c11":"Changing a time = <b>時間を変更(へんこう)する</b>.<br>「変化」is change that happens by itself (intransitive in nature — you can't say「時間を変化させてください」); 「交換」is swapping one thing for another; 「移動」is moving something to a different place. Use 変更 for deliberately altering a plan or arrangement.","n3-c12":"<b>見かける</b> = to happen to see / spot by chance: you spot a foreigner in trouble and go over to speak to them.<br>「見つめる」= to stare at; 「見送る」= to see someone off, or to hold off on something; 「見直す」= to review again, or to see someone in a new light — the 「見〜」compound verbs form a family, each with its own fixed meaning.","n3-g11":"<b>につれて</b> = as… (two things changing in step): as you get used to life in Japan, your friends increase too.<br>「によって」= depending on… / due to…; 「にとって」= for… / from the standpoint of…; 「に対して」= toward… (a target, or a contrast). When A changes and B changes along with it, use につれて.","n1-c7":"<b>事態の収拾(しゅうしゅう)を図る</b> = to try to bring a situation under control — a fixed collocation.<br>Trap: 「収拾」 and 「収集」 are <b>homophones written with different kanji</b> — 収集 means collecting (切手の収集), while 収拾 means getting a mess back under control; 「回収」 is recovering/collecting back in; 「修復」 is repairing (関係の修復 ○, but it doesn't fit the set phrase 「事態の…を図る」).","n1-g7":"<b>というより</b> = rather than saying…(it's more that…): rather than calling him a genius, he's someone who works hard.<br>「とばかりに」 = as if to say…; 「ともなしに」 = without meaning to (見るともなしに); 「というもの」 = for the whole stretch of… (この一年というもの).","n1-g8":"<b>たが最後</b> = once you…, that's it (there's no going back / you have no choice): once he decides, he will never change his mind.<br>「かと思うと」 = no sooner than… (one thing right after another); 「たところで」 = even if… (it's pointless); 「までも」 = even to the point of…. 「が最後」 carries a strong sense of <b>irreversibility</b>.","n5-k13":"<b>お金=おかね</b> — the kun reading かね plus the polite prefix お.<br>「きん」 is the on reading (used in 「金曜日きんようび」 and 「料金りょうきん」) — 「金」 meaning money on its own is read かね; 「おがね」 wrongly voices the か; the last option has the syllables out of order.","n5-k14":"<b>出口=でぐち</b>. When 口 comes second in a compound it takes rendaku (sequential voicing): くち→ぐち.<br>「でくち」 misses the rendaku; 「しゅつこう」 forces on readings; 「いりぐち」 is the reading of 「入口(entrance)」 — the opposite meaning.","n5-c11":"At the post office you <b>buy stamps = きってを買いました</b>.<br>「売り」 is to sell (that's what the clerk does); 「書き/読み」 don't go with きって (stamps).","n5-c12":"Tired → <b>rest = 休みましょう</b>.<br>「走り(run)/働き(work)/急ぎ(hurry)」 all contradict the cause-and-effect of being tired — this tests the logic on either side of 「から」.","n5-g13":"The object of 「見る」 takes <b>を</b>: 映画を見に行く (go to see a movie).<br>「ます-stem + に行く」 = go in order to do…; the object still takes を. 「映画に見に行く」 is a common mistake — に is already used up by 「見に」.","n1-g12":"<b>世に送り出す</b> = to release (a work) into the world — a fixed collocation.<br>「送り込む」= to send someone in (人員をスパイとして送り込む);「送り届ける」= to escort/deliver (goods or people) safely;「送り渡す」isn't an established compound. In compound verbs the second verb decides the meaning, and the only one that pairs with 「世に」 is 送り出す.","n1-o5":"Full sentence: 一流の<b>選手ともなると、プレッシャーの大きさも</b>想像を絶する (Once you become a top-class athlete, the sheer scale of the pressure is beyond imagination). Order: 選手 → ともなると → <b>プレッシャーの</b> → 大きさも, so ★ = <b>プレッシャーの</b>.<br>「ともなると」= once you reach the level of… (N1);「想像を絶する」= to defy imagination.","n1-o6":"Full sentence: <b>国民の信頼を回復するべく</b>、政府は改革に乗り出した (In order to restore the public's trust, the government set about reforming). Order: 国民の → 信頼を → <b>回復する</b> → べく, so ★ = <b>回復する</b>.<br>「dictionary form + べく」= in order to… (formal written purpose, N1);「乗り出す」= to set about, embark on.","n1-p6":"<b>おもむろに = slowly, unhurriedly = ゆっくりと</b>.<br>A classic trap: many learners — and plenty of native speakers — assume it means \"suddenly,\" when the real meaning is the exact opposite (徐ろに = calmly and slowly). Both the JLPT and Japanese vocabulary surveys love testing this misuse.","n1-p7":"The idiom <b>的を射る(まとをいる) = to hit the mark, to be right on target</b>.<br>※「的を得る」is a widespread mistake (the correct verb is 射る = to hit the target with an arrow).「間違っている」means the opposite; \"aggressive\" and \"outdated\" are unrelated.","n5-c13":"<b>シャワーをあびる(浴びる)</b> = to take a shower — a fixed collocation.<br>「はいる」goes with the bath (おふろにはいる);「かかる」means to cost, or to get splashed on;「やる」is too vague to pair with シャワー.","n5-c14":"Stamps are thin, flat objects, so the counter is <b>まい(枚)</b>.<br>「ほん(本)」counts long thin things;「だい(台)」counts machines and vehicles;「ひき(匹)」counts small animals — counters match an object's shape, and they're guaranteed to show up on the N5.","n5-c15":"The room is dark → <b>turn on the light = でんきをつける</b>.<br>「あける/しめる」open and close doors and windows;「けす(消す)」means to turn the light off — the opposite of what 「暗いから」 leads to.","n1-k10":"<b>貪欲=どんよく</b> (voracious, insatiable).<br>「たんよく」 is wrong because 貪 is read どん here, not たん (compare the kun-reading 貪る=むさぼる); 「どんく/ぼんよく」 aren't words. In the context of a thirst for knowledge, 貪欲 is a compliment.","n1-k11":"<b>滑らか=なめらか</b> (smooth, fluent).<br>「すべらか」 forces the reading of 滑る=すべる onto it; 滑 has two kun-readings (すべ/なめ), and 滑らか is always なめらか. 「かろやか」 belongs to 軽やか, and 「しなやか」 means supple — all real words, just written with different characters.","n1-c8":"<b>困難を乗り越える</b> = to overcome difficulties, a set collocation.<br>「乗り換える」 means to transfer (trains) — a trap because it looks almost identical; 「通り越す」 means to go past a point (怒りを通り越してあきれる); 「追い越す」 means to overtake a car or pass someone.","n1-c9":"<b>悪化(あっか)の一途をたどる</b> = to go steadily from bad to worse, a fixed idiom (一途 = heading in one direction only).<br>「老化」 is ageing; 「硬化」 is hardening, whether an attitude or a substance; 「消化」 is digestion — only 悪化 pairs with 事態 and with 一途.","n1-c10":"<b>膠着(こうちゃく)状態を打開する</b> = to break a deadlock, a set collocation (膠着 = stuck fast like glue).<br>「混雑」 is crowding (with people); 「渋滞」 is a traffic jam; 「停滞感」 doesn't fit the idiomatic 「〜状態を打開」 pattern.","n1-g9":"<b>怒りを禁じ得ない</b> = cannot help feeling angry (cannot suppress an emotion), a fixed pairing.<br>「にたえない」 goes with 見る/聞く (too painful to watch/hear) or 感謝 (deeply grateful); 「を余儀なくされる」 = to be forced into (中止を余儀なくされる); 「にかたくない」 goes with 想像 (not hard to imagine) — each of these four N1 patterns has its own fixed partner.","n1-g10":"<b>とあれば</b> = if it's a matter of… (a special circumstance): since the company president is coming, we have no choice but to prepare.<br>「ときたら」 = as for… (introducing a complaint); 「とばかりに」 = as if to say…; 「はおろか」 = let alone…, not even…. The \"have no choice but to\" in the second clause matches the \"special circumstance\" sense → とあれば.","n1-g11":"<b>貫き通す(つらぬきとおす)</b> = to carry through to the end, a fixed compound verb: to hold to your convictions all the way.<br>「〜通す」 attaches to the ます-stem and means to do something through to the finish (やり通す、隠し通す) — the other three aren't valid compounds (貫き渡す✕ etc.).","n2-k10":"<b>頻繁=ひんぱん</b> (frequent). After ん, 繁 shifts to the half-voiced ぱん.<br>「ひんばん」 wrongly voices it; 「びんぱん」 has the wrong first reading (頻=ひん); 「ひんはん」 misses the half-voicing.","n2-k11":"<b>曖昧=あいまい</b> (vague, ambiguous).<br>「あいめい」 misreads the second kanji (昧=まい); 「あんまい」 has the wrong first reading; 「まいまい」 is just a made-up combination.","n2-k12":"<b>促す=うながす</b> (to urge, to prompt).<br>「そくす」 forces the on-reading そく (as in 促進 そくしん) into a kun-reading slot; 「ほどこす」 belongs to 施す (to give, to carry out); 「せかす」 belongs to 急かす (to hurry someone) — almost the same meaning but a different kanji, a classic trap.","n2-c9":"<b>要領(ようりょう)が悪い</b> = to be inefficient, to not get the point — a fixed collocation.<br>「要点」 is the main point itself (要点をまとめる); 「要素」 is an element; 「要求」 is a demand — only 要領 pairs with 「が悪い」.","n2-c10":"<b>台風の影響(えいきょう)で</b> = due to the typhoon — a fixed collocation.<br>「影」 is a shadow; 「効果」 is a (positive) effect; 「台風の結果」 sounds unnatural — consequences of a disaster take 影響.","n2-c11":"Restoring a relationship = <b>関係が回復(かいふく)する</b>.<br>「修理」 is for repairing objects; 「復旧」 is for restoring infrastructure (電力/鉄道が復旧); 「治療」 is for treating illness — English lumps these together as \"repair/restore,\" but Japanese fixes each to its own object, and N2 loves testing this set.","n2-c12":"<b>指示を無視して</b> = ignoring the boss's instructions and going ahead anyway. The clue is 「勝手に」 (without permission).<br>「尊重して/確認して/待って」 all contradict 「勝手に」 — if you followed the instructions, you weren't acting on your own.","n2-g9":"<b>それどころか</b> = far from it / what's more — bad enough that he broke the promise, but he even lied about it. The 「まで」 in 「うそまで」 echoes this escalation.<br>「それにしても」 = even so; 「それなのに」 = and yet; 「そこで」 = so then — none of them carry that sense of things getting even worse.","n2-g10":"<b>にともなって(に伴って)</b> = along with / as ~ progresses: as the heavy rain continued, the water level rose. Use it when one change accompanies another.<br>「のすえに」= after a long process, finally…;「のあまり」= so ~ that… (takes an emotion noun: 悲しみのあまり);「にわたって」= spanning / over a range (10年にわたって).","n2-g11":"<b>からこそ</b> = precisely because (emphasises the reason): it was only because he helped that we made it in time. The sentence-final 「のだ」 reinforces that emphasis.<br>「ばこそ」 must attach to the ば-form (手伝ってくれればこそ);「おかげさまで」 is a set greeting phrase used at the start of a sentence, not mid-sentence;「ゆえに」 is literary and attaches differently (〜が/の+ゆえに).","n2-g12":"<b>うちに</b> = while / in the course of ~ (a change you don't notice happening): you gradually improve while repeating the practice. 「少しずつ〜てきた」 signals that gradual change.<br>「あいだに」= during a period (a single event happens in it);「ところに」= just at the moment when…;「とたんに」= the instant ~ (one-off). Gradual change pairs with うちに.","n2-o5":"Full sentence: <b>環境にやさしい製品の開発が</b>求められている (there is demand for developing eco-friendly products). Word order: 環境に→やさしい→<b>製品の</b>→開発が, so ★ = <b>製品の</b>.<br>「環境にやさしい」= eco-friendly (a set expression); the whole phrase modifies 「製品」.","n2-o6":"Full sentence: <b>事前に連絡したにもかかわらず、席が用意されて</b>いなかった (even though we contacted them in advance, no seat had been prepared). Word order: 事前に→連絡した→<b>にもかかわらず</b>→席が用意されて, so ★ = <b>にもかかわらず</b>.<br>「にもかかわらず」= even though / despite, and it follows the plain form.","n2-p6":"<b>たちまち = instantly, in no time = すぐに</b>.<br>「だんだん」= gradually (the opposite speed);「ようやく」= finally (after a long wait);「一時的に」= temporarily (about duration, not speed).","n2-p7":"<b>あやふや = vague, unclear = はっきりしない</b> (≒ 曖昧).<br>None of the other choices (outstanding / dangerous / new) carry the sense of \"not definite\". Learn the vagueness set together: あやふや / あいまい / うやむや.","n1-k9":"<b>拒む = こばむ</b> (to refuse, to reject).<br>「いどむ」 is 「挑む」 (to challenge);「のぞむ」 is 「望む/臨む」;「はばむ」 is 「阻む」 (to obstruct) — the distractors are all real words with related meanings, so what's really being tested is the kun-reading of the kanji 「拒」.","n5-p8":"<b>げんき(元気)= healthy and full of energy = not sick</b>.<br>Busy, kind, and young are all different qualities — the core of 元気 is \"physically well.\"","n5-p9":"<b>かんたん(簡単)= easy = やさしい(易しい)</b>.<br>「むずかしい」is the opposite; long and bad are different kinds of judgement. The かんたん⇔やさしい swap is a classic N5 言い換え (paraphrase) pair.","n5-p10":"<b>しごとのあとで = after work = はたらいてから</b> (after finishing work).<br>「まえに」= before (the opposite); 「ながら」= while doing at the same time; 「ないで」= doing … without doing …. Rewriting a noun phrase as a verb phrase is the point being tested.","n5-o7":"Full sentence: つくえの<b>上に、かぞくの写真が三まい</b>あります (There are three family photos on the desk). Word order: 上に→かぞくの→<b>写真が</b>→三まい, ★=<b>写真が</b>.<br>「かぞくの」must sit directly in front of the noun it modifies, 写真; the counter phrase 「三まい」goes just before the verb.","n5-o8":"Full sentence: バス<b>より電車のほうがはやい</b>です (The train is faster than the bus). Word order: より→電車の→<b>ほうが</b>→はやい, ★=<b>ほうが</b>.<br>The comparison pattern 「AよりBのほうが〜」has a fixed order.","n5-o9":"Full sentence: きのう、わたしは<b>姉に、たんじょうびのプレゼントを</b>あげました (Yesterday I gave my older sister a birthday present). Word order: 姉に→たんじょうびの→<b>プレゼントを</b>→あげました, ★=<b>プレゼントを</b>.<br>The 「あげる」pattern is: (person)に (thing)をあげる.","n5-r1":"Order of events in the passage: getting up → <b>a walk</b> → breakfast → going to work. So the walk comes <b>before breakfast</b>.<br>What follows 「あさごはんのあとで」is going to the office, not the walk — reading questions love to test the order of events, so follow the text rather than your intuition.","n5-r2":"The colour wanted was black, but it was <b>out of stock</b>, so a <b>brown (ちゃいろい)</b> one was bought instead.<br>「くろいくつ」is the trap — that is what was wanted, not what was bought. Golden rule for reading: answer with what actually happened, and don't get pulled off by a sentence about a wish.","n5-c16":"Every night at 12 you <b>go to sleep = ねます(寝ます)</b>.<br>「おきます(get up)」goes with 毎朝; 「あるきます(walk)/すわります(sit down)」make no sense with 「毎晩12時に」.","n5-c17":"To say water is clean, use <b>きれい</b>.<br>「じょうず/へた」only describe how good or bad someone is at a skill; 「にぎやか」describes a lively place — none of them can describe water.","n5-g15":"The subject of 「来る」takes <b>が</b>: 友だちが来ました (a friend came).<br>「を」marks an object; 「で」marks a place or means; 「へ」marks direction (うちへ is already there).","n5-g16":"To modify one noun with another, use <b>の</b>: 漢字<b>の</b>読み方 (the reading OF this kanji).<br>「が/を/に」cannot link two nouns together.","n5-g17":"「〜<b>の</b>中で…がいちばん」= \"of all …, … is the most\", a set pattern: of all Japanese food, sushi is my favourite.<br>No other particle can build this \"comparison within a group\" pattern.","n5-g18":"Haven't eaten yet = <b>まだ</b>食べていません. 「まだ+ていません」= not yet.<br>「もう」= already (which contradicts いいえ); 「よく/とても」express frequency or degree. The もう⇔まだ question-and-answer pair is a guaranteed N5 exam item.","n5-g19":"The weather is nice, \"so\" let's go to the park: use <b>から</b> for the reason.<br>「でも」= but; 「のに」= even though … (contrast); 「ながら」= while doing … (attaches to the ます-stem) — only から can lead into a 「〜ましょう」suggestion.","n5-p7":"<b>つまらない = boring = おもしろくない</b>.<br>「おもしろい」is the exact opposite; 「かなしい(sad)/こわい(scary)」are different emotions.","n4-g18":"<b>までに</b>=deadline (finish something <i>by</i> a point in time): hand it in by tomorrow.<br>「まで」=continuously up until (あしたまで書く=write until tomorrow);「から」=starting from;「ごろ」=around… (attaches to a point in time). までに (cut-off) vs. まで (duration) is the classic N4 pair.","n4-g19":"With potential verbs, the object takes <b>が</b>: ピアノがひけます (I can play the piano).<br>The plain form is「ピアノをひく」; when it becomes potential, を→が — this particle shift is the point always tested alongside the potential form.","n4-p7":"<b>うかがう(伺う) = the humble form of 行く/聞く</b>; here it means \"to go (to visit).\"<br>Matching honorific language to plain language is a regular 言い換え item: 伺う=行く・聞く、申す=言う、参る=行く・来る.","n4-p8":"<b>急に = suddenly</b>, the standard swap.<br>「ゆっくり(slowly)/だんだん(gradually)」give the opposite sense of speed;「やっと」= finally, at last.","n4-p9":"<b>あぶない=危ない=危険(きけん)</b> — a native-Japanese word swapped for its Sino-Japanese equivalent.<br>「安全」is the exact opposite; convenient / famous are unrelated.","n4-p10":"<b>留守(るす) = not at home = 家にいませんでした</b>.<br>「家にいました」is the opposite;「寝ていた/忙しかった」mean being home but unavailable — 留守 refers specifically to the person being out.","n4-o7":"Full sentence: この写真を<b>見ると、国の家族を</b>思い出します (Whenever I see this photo, I think of my family back home). Order: 見る→と→<b>国の</b>→家族を, ★=<b>国の</b>.<br>「dictionary form+と」= as soon as / whenever…, ….","n4-o8":"Full sentence: 日本で<b>生活するのに、漢字の知識が</b>必要です (Knowledge of kanji is necessary for living in Japan). Order: 生活する→のに→<b>漢字の</b>→知識が, ★=<b>漢字の</b>.<br>「dictionary form+のに」= for (the purpose of) doing…, expressing use or necessity.","n4-c13":"Fighting with your little brother → you <b>got scolded = しかられました (passive of 叱る)</b> by your mother.<br>「ほめられ (be praised)」 reverses the cause and effect; 「さそわれ (be invited)/たのまれ (be asked to do something)」 make no sense here.","n4-c14":"<b>Erase = けす (消す)</b> with an eraser: 字を消しゴムで消す.<br>「きる (cut)/わる (break)/やぶる (tear)」 can't take 「a mistakenly written character」 as their object — the tool 「消しゴム」 is the clue.","n4-c15":"It's getting dark → <b>it's about time we headed home = そろそろ帰りましょう</b>. 「そろそろ」 = it's about time to….<br>「始め (begin)」 contradicts a context about wrapping up; 「着き (arrive)/立ち (stand)」 don't work as a ましょう suggestion.","n4-c16":"You <b>put in too much = 入れすぎて</b>, so the bag broke. 「ます-stem + すぎる」 = do something to excess.<br>「出しすぎ (take out too much)」 wouldn't break a bag; 「見/読み」 have nothing to do with a かばん.","n4-c17":"To be invited to a wedding = <b>招待(しょうたい)される</b>.<br>「相談 (consult)/遠慮 (hold back)/練習 (practice)」 none work in the pattern 「結婚式に…される」.","n4-g15":"To advise 「you'd better not do…」 = <b>ない-form + ほうがいい</b>: 無理をしないほうがいい.<br>「なくて (negative て-form)/ません (polite form)/ないで」 can none of them attach directly to ほうがいい. The affirmative version is 「た-form + ほうがいい」 — affirmative takes た, negative takes ない; that asymmetry is the tested point.","n4-g16":"「見える」 is an intransitive verb (something naturally comes into view), so its object is marked with <b>が</b>: 富士山が見えます.<br>を is used with 「を見ます (look at something on purpose)」 — the transitive/intransitive pair 見える/見る with their matching particles is a high-frequency N4 point.","n4-g17":"Hearsay 「I hear that…」 = <b>plain form + そうです</b>: できるそうです.<br>「でき/できて」 are the wrong connections; 「できました+そうです」 is grammatically possible but the past tense contradicts 「来年 (next year, i.e. the future)」. ※ Only the appearance-based そう attaches to the ます-stem (できそう); hearsay そう takes the plain form — this contrast is always tested.","n3-g15":"「カメラ<b>を</b>」marks a direct object → you need the transitive verb <b>壊す</b> + てしまう = 壊してしまった (broke it, with a note of regret).<br>「壊れて」is intransitive (the subject would be the camera: カメラが壊れて); 「壊し/壊れ」are incomplete forms. Transitive/intransitive pairs × てしまう is core N3 material.","n3-g16":"<b>causative て-form + いただく</b> = \"allow me to…\" (humble): 帰らせていただきます = please let me take my leave.<br>「帰っていただきます」means \"I'll have YOU go home\" (roles reversed!); 「帰られて」is passive/honorific; 「帰り」doesn't connect here. This させていただく pattern is the single most common polite expression in the workplace.","n3-g17":"<b>たびに</b> = every time…: every time I go home to my country, I buy lots of souvenirs.<br>「うちに」= while…; 「までに」= by (a deadline); 「ながら」= while doing (attaches to the ます-stem). Only たびに expresses \"every single time, without fail.\"","n3-g18":"<b>わりに</b> = considering / for… (yet): he's lived there 10 years yet still can't speak it well — a gap between what you'd expect and reality.<br>「とおりに」= just as; 「ばかりに」= simply because… (leading to a bad result, but \"living there 10 years\" isn't the cause here); 「おかげで」= thanks to (positive).","n3-g19":"B is the one who has to <b>go</b> pick someone up: <b>迎えに行く</b> (ます-stem + に行く = go in order to do…).<br>「迎えに来る」points the wrong way — 来る moves toward the speaker, and since B is the one setting out, it has to be 行く; 「迎えて行く/来る」is the wrong connection (purpose takes ます-stem + に). The 行く/来る viewpoint is the point learners trip on most.","n3-p9":"<b>がっかりする = to be disappointed = 残念に思う</b>.<br>「うれしい」is the opposite; 「びっくり」is being surprised; 「安心」is feeling relieved — match the emotion precisely.","n3-p10":"<b>引き受ける = to take on, to accept responsibility for = agree to do it</b>.<br>「断った (refused)」is the exact opposite; 「やめた」is to quit; 「頼んだ」is asking someone else (the other direction).","n3-p11":"<b>しばらく = for a while, for a moment = 少しの間</b>.<br>「ずっと」= the whole time (too long a stretch); 「ときどき」= sometimes; 「最初から」= from the very beginning.","n3-p12":"<b>そっくり = identical / exactly alike</b> — a very strong degree of resemblance.<br>「少し似ている」 is too weak (そっくり is the top of the resemblance scale); 「全然違う」 is the opposite; price has nothing to do with it.","n3-o7":"Full sentence: 先生に<b>相談したところ、いいアドバイスを</b>もらえた (After consulting the teacher, I got good advice). Order: 相談した → ところ → <b>いい</b> → アドバイスを, ★ = <b>いい</b>.<br>「た-form + ところ」 = after doing…, (and as a result, this is what happened / what I found out).","n3-o8":"Full sentence: 漢字は<b>書けば書くほど</b>覚えられる (The more you write kanji, the better you remember them). Order: 書け → ば → <b>書く</b> → ほど, ★ = <b>書く</b>.<br>「ば-form + dictionary form + ほど」 = the more… the more…. The same verb is repeated twice, ば-form first and dictionary form second — the order is absolutely fixed.","n3-o9":"Full sentence: <b>意見がある人は、遠慮なく言って</b>ください (If you have an opinion, please say it without holding back). Order: 意見が → ある人は → <b>遠慮なく</b> → 言って, ★ = <b>遠慮なく</b>.<br>「意見がある」 modifies 「人」; 「遠慮なく」 = without hesitation, and it modifies 言う.","n3-r1":"The key point of the notice: <b>the water will be off tomorrow from 9 a.m. to 12 noon</b>.<br>「午後も」 goes beyond what the text says (it only mentions up to 12:00); recruitment and price increases are never mentioned at all — in notice-type reading questions, the answer always stays within what is actually written, so don't fill in the gaps yourself.","n3-r2":"The last sentence is the main point: <b>what matters is whether you make noise that disturbs other people</b>.<br>「スマホ全面不行」 over-generalises (the text says typing and games are fine); 「ゲームも違反」 contradicts the text; 「電話小聲就行」 is never stated (phone calls themselves count as a violation) — for main-idea questions, look for the concluding sentence, which often ends in 「〜のだ」.","n2-c13":"<b>役割(やくわり)を果たす</b> = to play a (key) role, to serve a function — a set collocation.<br>「役所」 is a government office; 「役者」 is an actor; 「配役」 is casting — all from the 「役」 family, but the one that pairs with 「を果たす」 is 役割.","n2-c14":"<b>順調(じゅんちょう)に進む</b> = to go smoothly, to progress well — a set collocation.<br>「調整/調査/強調」 are all action nouns and can't take 「に」 to modify 「進む」 — distractors from the 「調」 family.","n4-o9":"Full sentence: <b>きのう、兄が作ったカレーは</b>とてもおいしかった (The curry my older brother made yesterday was delicious). Word order: きのう→兄が→<b>作った</b>→カレーは, ★=<b>作った</b>.<br>「兄が作った」 is a relative clause modifying 「カレー」 (inside a relative clause the subject takes が, not は); the time word 「きのう」 goes at the head of the sentence.","n4-r1":"Key points in the memo: the meeting is <b>moved to 11:00</b>, and the documents are <b>no longer needed</b>. So the only thing left to do is attend the 11:00 meeting.<br>「10時」 is the old time that was changed; 「資料準備」 is explicitly cancelled; the memo only says there was a phone call, it never asks for a call back — in reading questions, grab the latest information after the change.","n4-r2":"The passage says: <b>far</b> from the station + rent is <b>cheap</b> + the room is <b>big</b> + there is a park nearby. The only match is \"far from the station but the room is big.\"<br>The other three contradict or distort the text (近くに公園 ≠ 公園の中) — the wrong choices swap just one word, so check each one back against the original.","n3-c13":"To work up one's courage = <b>勇気(ゆうき)を出す</b>, a set collocation.<br>「元気を出して」 means \"cheer up\" (comforting someone); 「人気」 is popularity; 「空気」 is air/atmosphere (空気を読む) — each word in the 「気」 family has its own fixed partner.","n3-c14":"<b>利用(りよう)できる</b> = can be used.<br>「採用」 is to hire/adopt (a proposal); 「信用」 is trust; 「器用」 is dexterous (a な-adjective, not a する-verb) — this is how the 「用」 family divides up the work.","n3-c15":"The match was <b>called off = 中止(ちゅうし)</b> because of the typhoon.<br>「中古」 is second-hand; 「禁止」 is prohibited (by rule); 「停車」 is a vehicle stopping — 「試合が…になった」 only pairs with 中止.","n3-c16":"<b>なかなか + negative</b> = not … for a long time: even though it was the appointed time, they still hadn't come.<br>「だんだん/どんどん」 mean gradually/steadily (used with change); 「そろそろ」 means \"it's about time\" — only なかなか is tied to a negative.","n3-c17":"<b>説明書(せつめいしょ)</b> = instruction manual, a fixed compound.<br>「紹介書」 is not a real word; 「案内書」 is a guidebook; 「報告書」 is a report — 「〜書」 compounds are fixed combinations.","n2-p8":"<b>かろうじて = barely / only just = ぎりぎりで</b> (came within a hair of missing it).<br>「余裕で」is the opposite (with time to spare);「偶然」means by chance;「いつもどおり」= as usual.","n2-p9":"Set phrase <b>白紙に戻る = to go back to a blank sheet, to start over from scratch</b>.<br>「中止」means it's called off,「延期」means it's moved to another date, and「白紙に戻る」means the whole thing restarts from zero — three different ways a plan can go wrong, so learn them as a contrast (this builds on N3's 延期 ≠ 中止).","n2-p10":"<b>いやいや = reluctantly, unwillingly = 気が進まないまま</b> (= しぶしぶ).<br>「喜んで/積極的に」are the opposite;「すぐに」is about speed, not willingness.","n2-p11":"<b>あらゆる = every, all kinds of = すべての</b> (a 連体詞 — it can only attach directly to a noun).<br>「一部の」covers the opposite range; \"new\" and \"unexpected\" are unrelated. Don't confuse it with the similar-looking「いわゆる (so-called)」.","n2-o7":"Full sentence: 試験に<b>合格できるかどうかは、これからの努力</b>次第だ (Whether you pass the exam depends entirely on the effort you put in from here on).<br>Word order: 合格できる → かどうかは → <b>これからの</b> → 努力, ★ = <b>これからの</b>.<br>「noun + 次第だ」= depends on….","n2-o8":"Full sentence: 彼は<b>反対されるのを承知の上で</b>、計画を発表した (Knowing full well he would be opposed, he announced the plan anyway).<br>Word order: 反対される → のを → <b>承知の</b> → 上で, ★ = <b>承知の</b>.<br>「〜を承知の上で」= to do something while fully aware that….","n2-o9":"Full sentence: インターネットの<b>普及によって、生活は便利になる</b>一方だ (With the spread of the internet, life keeps getting more convenient).<br>Word order: 普及によって → 生活は → <b>便利に</b> → なる, ★ = <b>便利に</b>.<br>「dictionary form + 一方だ」= keeps changing steadily in one direction.","n2-r1":"The main point is in the second sentence: <b>the quote you requested has been sent</b>.「お問い合わせください」is just a polite add-on, and「今月末まで有効」is a side note (なお = by the way).<br>Reading business emails: the main point is the first action sentence, and anything after「なお/ちなみに」is supplementary.","n2-c15":"The idiom <b>腹を立てる(はらをたてる)</b> = to get angry.<br>Each body part has its own fixed idiom: anger with 「頭」 is 「頭に来る」 (never 頭を立てる); 「気」 pairs with 「気にする/気になる」; 「胸」 pairs with 「胸を張る」 (to puff out one's chest with pride). These body-part idioms are tested on the exact pairing.","n2-c16":"To make a dream come true = <b>実現(じつげん)する</b>.<br>Trap: 「現実(げんじつ)」 is the noun \"reality\" — reversing the kanji changes the meaning completely, and it doesn't take する as a verb. 「実験」 is an experiment; 「実行」 is carrying something out (計画を実行). With 夢が…した, only 実現 works.","n2-c17":"<b>目撃者(もくげきしゃ)</b> = eyewitness.<br>「目標」 is a goal; 「目次」 is a table of contents; 「注目」 is attention (注目者 is not a real word). This is a fixed compound from the 「目」 family plus 「者」 (person).","n2-g13":"<b>おそれがある</b> = there is a risk/fear that… (negative possibility; used in news and official announcements): the flight may be delayed.<br>「ものがある」 = there really is something … about it (expressing admiration); 「ことか」 = how very…!; 「ばかりだ」 = it just keeps getting worse (遅れるばかりだ is forced here — in the announcement context of 「天候不良のため」 the standard phrasing is おそれがある).","n2-g14":"<b>ことなく</b> = without doing… (written style): decided to study abroad without consulting the family.<br>「ものなく/ところなく/わけなく」 are not grammar patterns at all (わけなく does exist, but it means \"easily,\" which doesn't fit here). ことなく is the written-style equivalent of ないで.","n2-g15":"<b>Noun + の + あまり</b> = so much … that…: so overwhelmed with work that they completely forgot to contact their friend.<br>「うちに」 = while (the chance lasts); 「ついでに」 = while you're at it; 「かわりに」 = instead of. 「あまり」 attaches to nouns of emotion or state (忙しさ、うれしさ) to express excess.","n2-g16":"<b>〜の観点から言えば</b> = speaking from the viewpoint of… — a fixed pattern (same as から見ると / からすると).<br>「に/を/で + 言えば」 are all ungrammatical: a starting point such as a 「立場・角度」 (standpoint, angle) takes から.","n2-g17":"<b>に違いない</b> = must be, I'm sure that… (a confident inference): knowing how serious they are, they're certain to arrive on time.<br>Trap: the correct form is 「に相違<b>ない</b>」 (the option given, 「に相違ある」, is the opposite); 「にほかならない」 is correct (「にほかない」 is missing なら); 「に限らない」 = not limited to. This item tests the complete form of the pattern — one character off and it's wrong.","n2-r2":"The author states the point through a rhetorical question, 「〜のではないか」: <b>chasing efficiency too hard makes you lose what actually matters</b> (room to think slowly, to build deep relationships).<br>\"Time is more valuable than money\" is a quoted proverb, not the author's claim; \"wasting time\" is the opposite of 「節約するあまり」; \"relationships should be efficient\" is the exact reverse of the point — ironic essays love to bait you with this.","n1-c11":"<b>真っ向から対立する</b> = to be in head-on opposition; a set collocation (真っ向から = head-on, straight from the front).<br>「対面」 is meeting face to face; 「対応」 is responding/dealing with; 「対策」 is a countermeasure (a noun, not used to describe an ongoing 「〜している」 state) — the 「対」 family splits the work between them.","n1-c12":"<b>証拠隠滅(いんめつ)</b> = destruction of evidence; fixed legal terminology.<br>「隠居」 is retiring into seclusion; 「消滅」 is intransitive disappearance (証拠が消滅); 「絶滅」 is extinction (of a species) — each 「滅」 word has its own territory.","n1-c13":"<b>巧み(たくみ)に描く</b> = to depict skillfully; a set collocation.<br>「頻繁に」 is frequently; 「円滑に」 is smoothly (of something proceeding); 「盛大に」 is on a grand scale (of holding an event) — each な-adjective + に pairs with its own fixed set of verbs.","n1-c14":"<b>めど(目処)が立たない</b> = there is no prospect / no end in sight; a fixed idiom.<br>「すじ(筋)」 goes with 「筋が通る」; 「わく(枠)」 is a frame or quota; 「あて」 goes with 「あてがない/あてにする」 — memorize 「めどが立つ/立たない」 as one whole unit.","n1-c15":"<b>反感を買う</b> = to incur antipathy; a set collocation (買う = to bring upon oneself, used with negatives: 恨みを買う, ひんしゅくを買う).<br>「好感/共感」 are positive feelings and don't take 「買う」 (好感を持たれる ○); 「実感」 is a felt sense of reality — 「を買う」 only accepts the negative ones.","n1-g13":"<b>いくら〜てもしすぎることはない</b> = you cannot do it too much / it can't be overdone: his achievements cannot be praised too highly.<br>「ものがある」 = there really is a sense of...; 「までもない」 = there's no need to...; 「きらいがある」 = has a (bad) tendency to... — once a sentence opens with 「いくら〜ても」, the ending is locked in as 「ことはない」.","n1-g14":"<b>をめぐって</b> = surrounding / over (something that sparks dispute or commotion): the political world was thrown into turmoil over the prime minister's remarks.<br>「をこめて」 = filled with (心を込めて); 「をふまえて」 = based on... (followed by a judgment or action); 「をとわず」 = regardless of... (昼夜を問わず). Words like 「波紋/議論/対立」 are almost always preceded by をめぐって.","n1-g15":"<b>をものともせず</b> = undaunted by, refusing to let something stop you: despite everyone's worry, they headed into the disaster area alone (carries a tone of admiration).<br>「をかぎりに」= as of …, for the last time (今日を限りに); 「をかわきりに(を皮切りに)」= starting with …; 「をおいて」= there is no one/nothing but …. ※ The similar-looking 「をよそに」 leans toward \"ignoring\" and is critical in tone, while をものともせず is praising — opposite attitudes, which is exactly why N1 loves testing this pair.","n1-g16":"<b>たあげく(に)</b> = and in the end … (a negative outcome): he showed up late and then didn't even apologize — unbelievable. Here it stacks one complaint on another: \"after already doing X, he even went and did Y.\"<br>「あまり」 attaches to noun+の or the dictionary form (嬉しさのあまり); 「かぎり」 = as long as; 「しだい」 = as soon as … (attaches to the ます-stem).","n1-g17":"For actions you (or your side) perform, use the <b>humble form いたす</b>: 用意いたしておきます.<br>「されて/なさって/ご〜になって」 are all honorific forms — applying them to yourself points the respect in the wrong direction, the single most damaging mistake in business Japanese. 「こちらで」 (on our side) is the clue telling you which direction to use.","n1-p8":"<b>回りくどい = roundabout, long-winded = 遠回しで長い</b>.<br>「簡潔だ」 (concise) is the exact opposite; accuracy and speed are unrelated dimensions. Related words: 遠回し (indirect, euphemistic), くどい (tediously repetitive).","n1-p9":"<b>にわかに = suddenly, abruptly = 急に</b> (literary register). 「にわか雨」 = a sudden shower.<br>「徐々に」 = gradually (the opposite); 「完全に」 = completely; 「不思議と」 = strangely, mysteriously.","n1-p10":"<b>一蹴する(いっしゅうする) = to kick aside = to reject flatly</b>.<br>「受け入れた」 (accepted) is the opposite; 「検討した」 means it's still under consideration; 「延期した」 means postponed — 一蹴 carries the sense of dismissing something outright, without a second thought.","n1-p11":"The idiom <b>腕がいい = skilled, highly accomplished at a craft</b>. 腕 here means skill (腕を磨く = to hone one's skills).<br>「力が強い」 is the literal-reading trap (physically strong arms?); personality and years of experience are beside the point — body-part idioms are always testing \"don't read it literally.\"","n1-o7":"Full sentence: 彼の<b>長年にわたる研究は、尊敬に</b>値する (His many years of research deserve respect). Word order: 長年に → わたる → <b>研究は</b> → 尊敬に, so ★ = <b>研究は</b>.<br>「にわたる」 = spanning, extending over (modifies a noun); 「に値する(あたいする)」 = to be worthy of.","n1-o8":"Full sentence: <b>プロですら解けない問題を</b>、彼は解いてみせた (He solved a problem that not even the pros could crack — and showed everyone he could). Word order: プロで → すら → <b>解けない</b> → 問題を, ★ = <b>解けない</b>.<br>「ですら」 = even… (the written-style counterpart of さえ); 「てみせた」 = to do something to prove a point — demonstrating one's resolve or ability to others.","n1-o9":"Full sentence: 環境の<b>激しい変化に対応できる企業だけが</b>生き残れる (Only companies that can adapt to drastic environmental change will survive). Word order: 激しい → 変化に → <b>対応できる</b> → 企業だけが, ★ = <b>対応できる</b>.<br>「激しい」 modifies 「変化」, and 「対応できる」 modifies 「企業」 — a sentence-building item that stacks two layers of modification.","n1-r1":"「尊重すべきだが盲信は禁物」 + 「自分の頭で考える」 = <b>even expert opinions should be examined critically</b>.<br>「常に正しい」 is blind faith — exactly what the text rejects; 「素人の意見が大切」 is never stated (rejecting blind faith ≠ rejecting experts); 「一つに絞る」 is the opposite of 「複数の情報源」. On N1 main-idea questions every distractor is plausible-but-wrong, so check each one back against the text sentence by sentence.","n1-r2":"The concluding sentence 「偶然手に取った一冊こそが〜もたらしてくれるのだ」 = <b>it is the chance encounter with a book that has real value</b> (こそ + のだ = emphatic assertion).<br>「効率が悪い」 belongs to the efficiency-first view the writer is criticising, not to their own claim; 「早く買うべき」 runs against the negative intent of the rhetorical question 「果たして〜だろうか」; reading volume is irrelevant. A rhetorical question (だろうか) signals that the author opposes the idea being raised.","n5-r3":"The first sentence says it outright: <b>かぞくは4人</b> (father, mother, older sister, and me).<br>Basic reading skill: for number questions, go back to the text instead of doing your own arithmetic (「父と母と姉とわたし」 is exactly 4 people — it matches).","n5-r4":"「きのう<b>北海道に着きました</b>」 + 「来週東京に<b>帰ります</b>」 → the writer is in 北海道 right now.<br>「東京」 is where they will return later; 「湖」 is tomorrow's destination. The tense markers (着いた / 帰ります) are what tell you where the person is at this moment.","n5-r5":"Open when: <b>not Monday, and between 10:00 and 19:00</b> → Tuesday at 3 p.m. ✓<br>Closed Mondays; 9 a.m. is before opening (opens at 10); 8 p.m. is after closing (closes at 7). Notice-board questions require both conditions — hours and closing day — to line up.","n5-r6":"「アニメが好き<b>で</b>、勉強を始めました」 — the <b>て-form here marks a reason</b>: because they like anime.<br>The other three reasons are never mentioned in the text. For reason questions, look for 「から / ので / て-form」.","n4-r3":"The notice says: on closed days, return books → <b>the 返却ポスト (drop slot)</b>.<br>「中で返す」won't work (the library is closed); \"wait until Saturday\" isn't asked for anywhere; \"call them\" is never mentioned — 「〜たい方は…てください」(\"those who wish to…, please do…\") is exactly how a notice gives you your instruction.","n4-r4":"The last sentence: 「今度は<b>もっと暖かい服を持って行こう</b>と思います」.<br>The weather was fine (not the problem); neither the summit nor the friends are criticised — 「(よ)うと思う」= intend to, so the plan for next time is right there in that sentence.","n4-r5":"「場所が…<b>変わりました</b>。時間は<b>同じ</b>」→ only the place changed.<br>The time is explicitly stated as unchanged; nothing about cancelling or rescheduling — contrast sentences like 「AはXに変わった、Bは同じ」 are a favourite of notice-reading questions.","n4-r6":"「たたみの部屋に入るときは、<b>スリッパもぬがなければなりません</b>」= in a tatami room you have to take off even your slippers.<br>「はく」is the opposite action (to put on) — and 「も」is the key word: the shoes are already off, so the slippers have to come off \"too\".","n3-r3":"Inside the brackets: <b>22:00–6:00 the next day = 1,375 円 per hour</b> (late-night rate).<br>1,100 円 is the daytime rate; 2,475 is the two added together (don't invent your own arithmetic) — this is an information-retrieval question, so match the condition and copy the figure straight out.","n3-r4":"「レジ袋が有料になった<b>ので</b>」— the reason sits right before ので: plastic bags now cost money.<br>The cute designs describe what happened *after* they became common, not the cause; 「レジ袋がなくなった」is wrong (they're charged for, not gone) — for reason questions, hunt down 「ので/から」.","n3-r5":"「印刷は<b>こちらで用意します</b>」(the sender handles it) + 「<b>各自パソコンだけ</b>お持ちください」→ just bring your laptop.<br>No need to bring printed handouts (the other side prints them) — who 「こちら/そちら」 points to, and what 「だけ」 limits, is the heart of reading business email.","n3-r6":"The closing line 「まずは<b>15 分だけ早く起きることから始めてみては</b>どうだろうか」— the suggestion is simply \"start by getting up 15 minutes earlier\".<br>「てみてはどうだろうか」= a soft suggestion pattern; the bit about 3 o'clock is an exaggerated distractor; the proverb is just an opening hook, not the writer's claim.","n5-l1":"Audio: 「すみません、この電車は<b>新宿に行きますか</b>。」— the question is whether this train goes to Shinjuku.<br>Neither the fare (いくら) nor the time (何時/どのくらい) comes up — in listening, lock onto the question word.","n5-l2":"Audio: 「駅の<b>南口(みなみぐち)</b>で会いましょう」.<br>南口 and 北口 differ by one character, and listening loves direction words: 東(ひがし)、西(にし)、南(みなみ)、北(きた) + 口.","n5-l3":"Dialogue: the woman asks \"coffee or black tea?\" → the man says 「じゃ、<b>紅茶を</b>お願いします」.<br>In a two-choice dialogue the answer is in the *reply*; whatever follows 「じゃ」 is the decision.","n5-l4":"Audio: 「<b>雨が降っていますから</b>、傘を…」— the reason sits before 「から」: because it's raining.<br>Don't mix up 雨(あめ) and 雪(ゆき); 暑い and 風 never appear.","n4-l1":"Audio: 「3時からでした<b>が</b>、<b>4時からに変わりました</b>」— it's been changed to 4 o'clock.<br>Listening rule: what's said first is usually the *old* information; the answer comes after 「が」 or 「でも」. Every number will be spoken — take the one after the change.","n4-l2":"Audio: 「朝と夜、<b>食事のあとで</b>」= morning and night, after meals.<br>あと(後) vs. まえ(前) is a standard listening contrast; the 「だけ」 option drops half of the condition.","n4-l3":"Audio: 「使い方を<b>教えていただけませんか</b>」= please show me how to use it (a polite request form).<br>They're not asking someone to print it for them, or to repair it — the verb right before 「ていただけませんか」 is what's being requested.","n4-l4":"The man first says 6:30, then immediately corrects himself: 「あ、<b>でも</b>今日は金曜日だから、<b>7時から</b>だ」.<br>「あ、でも」 signals that he's overturning what he just said — when a speaker corrects themselves in a listening item, the answer is always the later one.","n1-l1":"「<b>ペーパーレス化</b>を全面的に推進」= a full push toward going paperless = there will be less printed material.<br>You need to turn an abstract loanword concept like 「ペーパーレス」 into a concrete consequence; \"scrapping paper entirely\" overreads it (it's a gradual reduction).","n1-l2":"「<b>概ね(おおむね)好意的</b>」= broadly favourable, plus 「価格面で<b>再考の余地あり</b>」= the price still needs discussion.<br>「概ね」 is not \"across the board\" (so 「全面的に賛成」 is wrong); the business euphemism 「再考の余地あり」 means \"we have objections\" — reading between the lines like this is exactly what N1 listening tests.","n1-l3":"「今回は<b>見送らせていただきます</b>」— 「見送る」 here means \"we'll pass this time\" (the standard polite refusal).<br>Opening with 「せっかくですが」 plus 「予算の都合上」 is the classic three-part soft rejection. Japanese business language never says 「だめ」 outright; catching the indirect \"no\" is N1 level.","n1-l4":"「技術<b>もさることながら</b>、<b>表現力が群を抜いている</b>」— 「AもさることながらB」 means \"A is impressive, of course, but B even more so,\" so the highest praise goes to the expressiveness.<br>「群を抜く」 = to stand head and shoulders above the rest. Find where the sentence puts its weight; don't be pulled to 「技術」 just because it comes first.","n5-c18":"To ride or board transport, use <b>乗る(のる)</b>: バスに乗って学校へ行く.<br>「おりて(降りて)」 means getting off — the opposite direction; 「はいって/でて」 are for entering and leaving buildings or rooms (部屋に入る/出る), not for taking transport somewhere.","n5-c19":"To <b>wipe</b> your hands with a handkerchief, use <b>ふく(拭く)</b>.<br>「あらう(洗う)」 means to wash with water, which isn't what a handkerchief does; 「みがく(磨く)」 means to brush or polish (歯をみがく); 「きる(切る)」 means to cut, which makes no sense here.","n5-c20":"Brushing your teeth is always <b>歯をみがく(磨く)</b>.<br>「歯をあらう」 and 「歯をふく」 are not natural Japanese collocations; 「そうじする」 is for cleaning places like rooms and floors.","n5-c21":"The second half says \"please teach me one more time\" → so the first half must be <b>forgot = わすれて</b>.<br>「おぼえて(記住了)」 (remembered) contradicts \"teach me again\"; 「かいて/よんで」 (wrote / read) don't connect to asking to be taught again.","n3-l1":"Announcement: 「3階の紳士服売り場では、<b>セールを行っております</b>」= a sale is being held. <br>In department-store announcements, 「お客様にご案内いたします」 is just the opening formula — the actual topic comes in the sentence right after it.","n3-l2":"The woman says 「<b>でも</b>部長が、<b>あさってでもいい</b>って」 — the deadline has been pushed back to the day after tomorrow. <br>「あした」 was the original deadline (old info); 「って」 marks it as a quote of what the manager said. In rescheduling dialogues, listen for the final conclusion.","n3-l3":"「<b>先に店に入っていてください</b>」 — please go into the restaurant first (and wait there). <br>The first half (the train is delayed, he won't make it) is the reason; the request itself is in the 「てください」 sentence — listen for reason and request separately.","n3-l4":"「来月から<b>ジムに通うことにしました</b>」= she has decided to start going to the gym (ことにする = a decision you make yourself). <br>「通う」 means going somewhere regularly; lack of exercise is the reason, not the action; 「やめる」 points the opposite way.","n2-l1":"Announcement: 「エレベーターを<b>使わず</b>、<b>階段で</b>…お降りください」. <br>「〜ず」 is a written-style negative meaning \"without doing ~\": don't use the elevator, go down by the stairs. With instruction questions, catch both what's forbidden and what you should do.","n2-l2":"The man says 「すぐ最新のものに<b>差し替えます</b>」= he'll swap it for the latest data right away. <br>The only problem pointed out is 「データが古い」 (内容はいい = the content itself is fine, no rewrite needed); the apology is already done, so it isn't the next action — for 「これから何を?」 listen for the final statement of intent.","n2-l3":"「閉店時間は8時でございます<b>が</b>、本日は…<b>7時に閉店</b>」 — normally 8:00, but today they close an hour early at 7:00. <br>Again the 「が」 pattern: old info before, new info after. It's fine if you don't catch 「棚卸し」 (stocktaking) — just track the change in the numbers.","n2-l4":"「大阪支社に<b>転勤することになりました</b>」= he's being transferred to the Osaka branch (ことになる = decided by someone else / circumstances). <br>「お世話になりました」 is a standard transfer greeting, not a resignation — 「辞める」 is the trap; neither a promotion nor Tokyo is mentioned.","n2-r3":"What follows 「<b>一方で</b>」 is the downside: work and private life become hard to separate, and hours actually get longer.<br>Commuting time \"disappears\" (a benefit); free time increases; pay is never mentioned — 「一方で/しかし」 are signposts for a good-to-bad turn, and the problem always sits after the turn.","n2-r4":"「<b>事前のお申し込みが必要</b>」 → you must sign up in advance, and 「定員になり次第終了」 = it closes as soon as it fills, so sign up early.<br>Free ≠ no registration; it is held online, so there is no venue to go to — with 「無料ですが」, the important condition comes after the が.","n2-r5":"「大切なのは<b>目的に合っているかどうか</b>」 — the writer states the claim outright.<br>「高いほどいい」 is the common assumption that 「必ずしもそうとは限らない」 knocks down; and far from saying not to buy cheap ones, the text says cheap ones are sometimes more useful — the writer's real stance hides after 「〜がちだが / とは限らない」.","n2-r6":"The opening 「発送に<b>遅れが生じており</b>、誠に申し訳ございません」 apologises for a shipping delay, and the rest gives the fix and its timing (shipments going out in batches by next Monday).<br>Nothing about being out of stock, cancelled orders, or the business closing — in an apology notice, the main point is the sentence saying what they are apologising for.","n1-r3":"「問題は変化そのものではなく、<b>学び直しの機会がすべての人に開かれているかどうか</b>」 — this is the 「AではなくB」 pattern, and the point is B.<br>The writer is not against AI (they even cite history showing that technological innovation creates new jobs), and they never predict which jobs will appear — N1 main-idea questions often bury the answer in a \"not A, but B\" sentence.","n1-r4":"「自らの非を<b>認めない</b>言い回し」 + 「不信感を<b>かえって増幅させかねない</b>」 = refusing to admit fault, which only makes things worse.<br>「とすれば」 (\"if that were the case\") is exactly the hedging used to avoid admitting a fact, and 「かねない」 means \"could well end up (badly)\" — for evaluation questions, look for the writer's words of praise or blame.","n1-r5":"The conclusion: 「形が変わった<b>だけ</b>で、欲求は<b>衰えていない</b>のかもしれない」.<br>「読まなくなった」 is the received wisdom that the statistics contradict; the writer never judges which is better or how the industry is growing — that softened conclusion marked by 「〜だけで / のかもしれない」 is the writer's position.","n1-r6":"The sequence: a defect is found → <b>free replacement</b> → please contact the support desk. Purpose = a recall-and-replacement notice.<br>「ご連絡ください」 is the means, not the purpose; there is no advertising, no price increase, no closing of the desk — with company notices, catch \"what customers are asked to do + what the company is offering\".","n5-c22":"You wash clothes in a washing machine: <b>洗=あらう(洗う)</b>.<br>「ぬぐ(脱ぐ)」means to take off (clothes) — you can't \"take clothes off with a washing machine\";「きる(着る)」is to put on;「かう(買う)」is to buy. None of them fit with a washing machine.","n5-c23":"Being hungry is <b>おなかがすく</b>, so the natural follow-up is to go eat.<br>「いっぱいです(I'm full)」contradicts \"let's go eat\";「かわく(渇く)」means thirsty and pairs with「のどがかわく」;「つかれる」means tired, and おなか can't be its subject.","n5-c24":"You <b>borrow=かりる(借りる)</b> from the library.<br>「かす(貸す)」means to lend (I lend to someone else) — the opposite direction, and this pair is the most commonly tested one;「うる(売る)」to sell and「あげる」to give don't fit the meaning.","n5-c25":"It's cold, so you <b>put on a coat=きる(着る)</b>.<br>「ぬぐ(脱ぐ)」means to take off, which contradicts being cold;「とる」to take and「しめる」to close (a door or window) have nothing to do with wearing clothes.","n5-c26":"Doing homework is <b>宿題をする→してから</b>.<br>「しって(知って)」is from 知る (to know) — the て-forms of「する」and「知る」differ by just one っ, so they're easy to misread;「すんで(住んで)」to live and「たべて」to eat don't fit the meaning.","n5-c27":"You don't know the way, so you <b>ask=きく(聞く)</b> the police officer.<br>「こたえる」means to answer (opposite direction);「おしえる」means (I) teach someone else — here it should be the officer telling me;「いう」just means to say and carries no sense of asking.","n4-c18":"Sending a parcel to family is <b>おくる(送る)</b>.<br>「くばる(配る)」means to hand out to many people (プリントを配る);「ひろう(拾う)」to pick up;「すてる(捨てる)」to throw away — none of them fit.","n4-c19":"The set phrase for catching a cold is <b>かぜをひく</b>→「かぜをひかないように」= so as not to catch a cold.<br>「かぜをとる/する/なる」are not real Japanese collocations —「病気になる」is fine, but「かぜをならない」also uses the wrong particle (を with なる is ungrammatical).","n3-c18":"The set phrase for \"inexperienced\" is <b>経験が浅い(あさい)</b>.<br>「薄い」goes with 味/色/本;「低い」with 背/温度/レベル;「短い」with 時間/髪——「経験」only pairs with「浅い/豊富」. Collocations (コロケーション) are the heart of N3 vocabulary questions.","n3-c19":"For a disease spreading, use <b>流行(りゅうこう)する</b>.<br>「人気/評判」mean popularity and reputation (and they don't take する this way);「活躍」means being active, and its subject has to be a person. 病気・風邪・インフルエンザ + 流行する is the fixed combination.","n3-c20":"To mind or worry about something is <b>気にする</b>: 細かいことを気にしない = not to fuss over details.<br>None of「心に/頭に/胸に」can take「〜にする」to mean \"mind\" (心に残る, 頭にくる, 胸に刻む are separate set phrases).","n3-c21":"A lie coming to light is <b>うそがばれる</b>——a fixed collocation.<br>「やぶれる」means to tear (paper, dreams);「たおれる」to fall over;「ながれる」to flow or (of a plan) to fall through——none of them pair with「うそ」.","n3-c22":"Being financially strapped is <b>生活が苦しい(くるしい)</b>.<br>「痛い」is physical pain;「悲しい」sad and「眠い」sleepy——all describe how a person feels, so they can't take「生活」as the subject.","n3-c23":"The mimetic word for sleeping soundly is <b>ぐっすり(眠る)</b>: ぐっすり眠れなかった = I couldn't sleep soundly.<br>「しっかり」means firmly, thoroughly (準備, 勉強);「はっきり」clearly (言う, 見える);「すっかり」completely (忘れる)——each of these four「〜り」adverbs has its own fixed partners, a frequent N3 test point.","n3-c24":"<b>〜のわりに</b> = \"for ~\" (more than you'd expect from it): for this price, it tastes great.<br>「ため」marks a cause or purpose;「くせに」carries blame and needs the same subject in both clauses;「せいで」pins the blame on a cause and must be followed by a bad result——but here the result is good (味がいい).","n3-c25":"The basic microwave verb is <b>温める(あたためる)</b>, to heat up.<br>「冷やす/こおらせる」are what a fridge or freezer does;「ゆでる」requires boiling water, not a microwave——matching kitchen verbs to the right appliance is a common test point.","n4-c20":"<b>おくれそう(遅れそう)</b> = \"looks like I'll be late,\" so they ran.<br>「まにあいそう(will make it in time)」 removes any reason to run — it contradicts the sentence; 「いそぐ」 is your own action, so it doesn't fit 「〜そうなので走った」; 「とまる」 means to stop, which makes no sense here.","n4-c21":"Getting used to a new environment = <b>〜に慣れる(なれる)</b>: 生活に慣れましたか。<br>「すむ(住む)」 takes a place — 日本に住む, never 生活に住む; 「かよう(通う)」 is commuting to work or school; 「つづく」 means to continue, and the subject doesn't work here.","n4-c22":"A battery running out = <b>電池が切れる(きれる)</b> — a fixed collocation.<br>「やぶれる(破れる)」 is for paper and cloth; 「おれる(折れる)」 is for long, rigid things (bones, branches); 「さめる(冷める)」 is for something hot going cold.","n4-c23":"Making 20 copies of a document = <b>コピーする</b>; the counter 「〜部」 is used for printed material.<br>「ちゅうもん(注文)」 is ordering food or goods; 「よやく(予約)」 is booking or reserving; 「けいかく(計画)」 is planning — none of them would ever count 「20部」 of documents.","n4-c24":"To say a third person wants something, use <b>ほしがる</b> (ほしい + がる): 弟は〜をほしがっている。<br>「こわがる」 is to be scared, 「いたがる」 is to act like something hurts, 「さむがる」 is to act cold — none of them work after 「新しいゲームを」.","n4-c25":"A broken bicycle gets <b>fixed = なおす(直す)</b> by someone.<br>「こわす」 means to break something (breaking what's already broken? contradictory); 「つくる」 means building a new one, which doesn't match 「壊れた自転車を」; 「かす」 means to lend out.","n4-c26":"The following line, 「兄弟ですか?」, tells you the first part must be <b>they look alike = 似ている(にている)</b>.<br>「見ている」 is grammatical but leads nowhere near 「兄弟ですか」; 「来て/出て」 don't fit the meaning. Note that the て-form of 「似る」 is just one kana — easy to overlook.","n4-c27":"To write a report you <b>gather materials = あつめる(集める)</b>.<br>「ひろう(拾う)」 is picking something up off the ground; 「かぞえる(数える)」 is counting; 「むかえる(迎える)」 is going to meet or greet a person — none of them fit.","n2-c24":"To hold back tears = <b>涙をこらえる(堪える)</b>.<br>「ささえる(支える)」means to support/prop up;「かかえる(抱える)」means to carry or be burdened with (問題を抱える);「そなえる(備える)」means to prepare against something — all four end in 〜える and are easily confused.","n2-c25":"To change the direction (of a meeting) = <b>流れを変える</b>.<br>「曲げる」goes with「事実を曲げる(distort the facts)」and「腰を曲げる」;「折る」goes with「骨を折る」;「破る」goes with「約束を破る」—「流れ」only pairs with verbs like「変える/読む」.","n2-c26":"<b>〜を契機(けいき)に</b> = taking ~ as the trigger/turning point.<br>「目安」is a rough standard (を目安に = as a guideline, which doesn't fit \"the cause prompting a review\");「手本」is a model to copy;「口実」is an excuse — \"revising the process using the failure as an excuse\" makes no sense.","n2-c27":"Completely opposite personalities = <b>正反対(せいはんたい)</b>.<br>「反対側」is the other (physical) side;「逆さま」is upside down;「裏返し」is inside out — only「正反対」is used for abstract things like personality or opinions.","n1-c16":"To admit something frankly = <b>率直(そっちょく)に認める</b>.<br>「頑固に」means stubbornly (which contradicts「認める」);「柔軟に」goes with「対応する」;「慎重に」goes with「判断する」— none of them are natural partners for admitting a mistake.","n1-c17":"The four-character compound <b>一触即発(いっしょくそくはつ)</b> = on the verge of exploding at the slightest touch.<br>「連発/突発/再発」are all real words, but the only four-character idiom starting with「一触〜」is「即発」— completing four-character compounds is a standard N1 vocabulary question type.","n1-c18":"<b>準備万端(ばんたん)</b> = fully prepared — a fixed four-character phrase.<br>「万能」means all-purpose/almighty (万能薬);「万事」means everything (万事休す);「万来」appears in「千客万来」— none of them combine with「準備」.","n1-c19":"Being self-contradictory = lacking <b>一貫性(いっかんせい)</b>.<br>「一体性」is a sense of unity (組織の一体性);「一方性/一律性」are almost never used with「主張」—「主張・話に一貫性がない」is the set phrase.","n3-c26":"Getting a job after graduating = <b>卒業(そつぎょう)</b>.<br>「入学」means entering school (which contradicts the order of finding a job afterwards);「退院」means leaving hospital, which applies to a 病院;「合格」requires「大学に合格」— the particle is に, not を.","n3-c27":"The second clause says you always get nervous → bad at it = <b>苦手(にがて)</b>.<br>「得意/上手」mean being good at something, which contradicts being nervous;「熱心」means enthusiastic, which makes no logical sense leading into「緊張する」.","n2-c18":"To exceed expectations = <b>予想を上回る(うわまわる)</b>.<br>「下回る」means to fall below — contradicting「好調」;「見上げる」means to look up at;「追いかける」means to chase. None of them pair with「予想を」.","n2-c19":"What convinces listeners and draws them in = <b>説得力(せっとくりょく)がある</b>.<br>「影響力」is influence exerted on the outside world;「実力/体力」are a person's own ability or stamina — what makes listeners「引き込まれる(be drawn in)」is the persuasive force of the words themselves.","n2-c20":"The <b>真相(しんそう)</b> of the incident is unclear, so all kinds of speculation are flying around.<br>「正解」is the correct answer to a question;「本音」is a person's true feelings;「本物」is the genuine article (as opposed to a fake) — the only one that pairs with「事件の」is 真相.","n2-c21":"Management difficulties = <b>経営難(けいえいなん)</b>:「〜難」is a word-forming suffix (住宅難、人手難).<br>「経営苦/経営悪/経営害」are not Japanese words at all — suffix collocations like this are a standard N2 vocabulary question type.","n2-c22":"<b>〜を余儀なくされる</b> = to be forced to do ~ (formal written style): 修正を余儀なくされた.<br>「やむを得ず/仕方なく」are close in meaning but are adverbs, so they cannot take「された」;「是非なく」is not used in modern Japanese.","n2-c23":"<b>検討の余地(よち)がある</b> = there is room for reconsideration — a set collocation.<br>「空間/場所/すき間」all refer to physical space and cannot be used for the abstract sense of \"worth reconsidering\".","n5-g22":"To shower = <b>シャワーを浴びる</b>; the thing you \"bathe in\" takes を.<br>「が」marks the subject;「に/へ」mark a target or direction — but「あびる」is a transitive verb, so it takes its object directly with を.","n5-g23":"The answer is \"it was fun\" → the question asks for an impression = <b>どう (how / what was it like)</b>.<br>「どこ」asks where,「だれ」asks who,「いつ」asks when — the question word must match the answer, a point tested in both listening and grammar.","n5-g24":"Quantity + <b>で</b> = \"~ for a total of (this much)\": 三つで500円 = three for 500 yen altogether.<br>「を/に/が」have no \"total\" usage — this is the essential pattern for shopping.","n5-g25":"Means of transport takes <b>で</b>: 何で来ましたか = How did you get here? Answer:「飛行機(で)」.<br>「に」marks a destination or point in time;「を/が」don't express means.","n5-g26":"「働く」is an activity, and the place it happens takes <b>で</b>: 東京で働く.<br>Note:「勤める」is the one that takes に (会社に勤める) — this contrast is a classic N5→N4 trap.「を/へ」don't mark the place of an action.","n5-g27":"To contrast the two halves (likes dogs / doesn't like cats), use the adversative <b>が</b>.<br>「から」is cause and effect (likes dogs \"therefore\" dislikes cats? that doesn't follow);「と/や」link nouns and cannot join clauses.","n5-g28":"To go (see) a movie = <b>映画に行く</b>; the destination/purpose takes に.<br>The を in「映画を見る」belongs to「見る」; with「行く」you use に — particles follow the verb, not the noun.","n5-g29":"Question word + <b>も</b> + negative = a blanket negation: だれもいません = nobody is here.<br>「だれがいません」isn't a valid sentence;「だれか」means \"someone\" and goes with affirmative questions (だれかいますか);「は」doesn't attach to question words.","n4-g20":"「変得~」= <b>上手になる</b>: to study <em>in order to</em> get better = 上手になるように。<br>「する」 is transitive (to make something into ~); 「いる/ある」 express existence — 「なる」 marks a natural change of state, and adding 「ように」 turns it into a purpose/goal.","n4-g21":"<b>〜ているところ</b> = right in the middle of doing ~.<br>「〜てあるところ/ておくところ/てみるところ」 are not this pattern — 「ところ」 must follow ている to mean an action in progress.","n4-g22":"「なければなりません」 attaches to the verb's <b>ない form minus ない</b>: しない→<b>し</b>+なければ。<br>「する/して/しよう」 are all the wrong connection — conjugation form is the bread and butter of grammar questions.","n4-g23":"The humble form of 「先生<b>に</b>~てもらう」 is <b>ていただく</b>。<br>「くださる」 pairs with 「先生が」 (先生が直してくださいました) — the particle に vs が decides which giving/receiving verb you use; 「さしあげる/あげる」 mean <em>I</em> do something for someone else, the opposite direction.","n4-g24":"Movement toward the speaker = <b>〜てくる</b>: 風が入ってきた。<br>「ていく」 moves away from the speaker; 「てみる」 is to try doing; 「ておく」 is to do in advance — direction is always judged from the speaker's standpoint.","n4-g25":"The そう of appearance attaches to an <b>い-adjective minus い</b>: おいしい→<b>おいし</b>そう = it looks delicious.<br>「おいしいそう」 would be hearsay (I hear it's tasty), and even that needs the plain form + そうだ;「おいしくそう/おいしいだそう」 are not valid forms.","n4-g26":"The object of 「できる」 takes <b>が</b>: 運転ができる。<br>「を」 goes with transitive verbs (運転をする is fine); potential-type verbs like できる and わかる always take が in front — the same rule as N5's 「日本語がわかる」.","n4-g27":"Purpose before a motion verb uses the <b>ます form minus ます + に</b>: 遊びに来る = to come to play.<br>「を/で/が」 cannot express the purpose of coming — 「買い物に行く」 and 「食べに行く」 follow the same pattern.","n1-c20":"To be pressed for payment = <b>催促(さいそく)される</b>.<br>「挑発」is to provoke; 「摘発」is to expose/report (an illegal act); 「勃発」is (a war or incident) breaking out — none of these can take a passive form with a person as the target. Four similar-sounding 「〜発/〜促」words compared.","n1-c21":"<b>不断(ふだん)の努力</b> = unceasing effort — a set collocation.<br>「不意」means sudden (不意をつく); 「不朽」means immortal/undying (不朽の名作); 「不覚」means a careless blunder (不覚を取る) — each 「不」compound has its own fixed partner word.","n1-c22":"For information being tangled and intertwined, use <b>錯綜(さくそう)する</b>.<br>「錯誤」means error (試行錯誤) and isn't used as a verb about information; 「混同」is mixing up two things (公私混同) and needs 「AとBを」; 「沈滞」is stagnation/slump (景気が沈滞).","n1-c23":"To nitpick someone's slip of the tongue = the idiom <b>揚げ足を取る</b>.<br>「手足となる」is to act as someone's right hand; 「足元を見る」is to take advantage of someone's weakness; 「足跡を残す」is to leave one's mark/achievements behind — all 「足」idioms, but with completely different meanings.","n1-c24":"To capture people's hearts = <b>心をつかむ(掴む)</b>.<br>「にぎる」is to physically grip (手を握る) or to hold power (実権を握る); 「おさえる」is to suppress (怒りを抑える); 「ひろう」is to pick up — only 「つかむ」collocates with 「聴衆の心」.","n1-c25":"To come through hell and back = <b>修羅場(しゅらば)をくぐり抜ける</b> — a set collocation.<br>「独壇場」is a stage where one person shines alone; 「正念場」is the crucial moment (を迎える/乗り切る); 「土壇場」is the very last minute (土壇場で) — four 「場」words used differently, and only 修羅場 pairs with 「くぐり抜ける」.","n5-g20":"The thing you understand with 「わかる」is marked by <b>が</b>: 日本語がわかる.<br>「を」marks the object of a transitive verb (日本語を勉強する works, but わかる doesn't); 「に/で」mark a target or a means, neither of which fits — が before 「わかる・できる・好き」is an ironclad N5 rule.","n5-g21":"The place where something exists (います/あります) takes <b>に</b>.<br>「で」marks where an action happens (公園で遊ぶ); 「を」marks the object; 「へ」marks direction — 「いる/ある」takes に while action verbs take で, the most basic place-particle distinction.","n4-g28":"Noun + <b>な</b> + んです: 会議中<b>な</b>んです = \"it's because the meeting is going on.\"<br>With a noun or な-adjective, だ turns into な before んです, so 会議中だんです is wrong; の and である don't attach here either. This な insertion is the single most common N4 connection mistake.","n4-g29":"Honorific pattern <b>お + ます-stem + になる</b>: お帰りになります.<br>帰りになります is missing the お; お帰りしになります mixes in the humble お〜する; 帰られになります stacks the honorific られる on top of になる. All three are wrong.","n3-g20":"<b>〜ことになっている</b> = it's the rule / the established practice to ~.<br>ものになっている, ところになっている and ばかりになっている are not patterns at all — for regulations, school rules and company policy, use ことになっている.","n3-g21":"Use <b>せいで</b> to blame a cause for a bad result: 寝坊したせいで電車に間に合わなかった = \"because I overslept, I missed the train.\"<br>おかげで is for good results (thanks to ~); ため takes no で when it marks a cause (寝坊したため); わけで means \"that's how it is,\" not \"it's the fault of ~.\"","n3-g22":"<b>〜たばかり</b> = have just ~ed: 来たばかりのころ = \"back when I had just arrived.\"<br>だけ, しか and ほど cannot attach to た-form + のころ to mean \"just now\" — only たばかり says the action finished a moment ago.","n3-g23":"<b>〜ないわけではない</b> = it's not that I don't ~ at all (partial negation).<br>ないはずではない, ないものではない and ないことではない are not this pattern — わけ is the only noun that works in this fixed double negative.","n3-g24":"<b>〜ているうちに</b> = while ~ was going on, something happened without you noticing: 聞いているうちに眠くなった = \"as I listened, I got sleepy.\"<br>ているなかに isn't a pattern; ところに means \"right at the moment when ~ (and got interrupted)\"; ままに means leaving something as it is. None of them fit.","n3-g25":"<b>〜たとたん</b> = the instant ~ happened, something unexpected followed: 駅に着いたとたん電車が行ってしまった = \"the moment I reached the station, the train pulled away.\"<br>とおり means \"just as ~\" (説明書のとおり); ながら is \"while doing ~\"; つもり is \"intend to.\" Only とたん carries \"instantly + unexpectedly.\"","n3-g26":"<b>まるで〜かのように</b> = just like / as if ~.<br>「のように」attaches directly to a noun (先生のように), but to attach a clause you need か;「と/が」don't work here — the か marks an uncertain, hypothetical comparison.","n3-g27":"A polite request = <b>ていただけませんか</b> (the potential form of いただく + ませんか).<br>「いただきませんか」means offering food to someone;「くださりませんか」is unnatural (use くださいませんか);「もらいませんか」is rude — the key point is the potential form いただけ.","n3-g28":"The adverb <b>たとえ</b> is always paired with <b>〜ても</b>: たとえ雨が降っても = even if it rains.<br>「たら/ば/なら」can't pair with たとえ — these fixed adverb pairings (たとえ〜ても、もし〜たら、まるで〜ようだ) are an important N3 test point.","n3-g29":"Use <b>に</b> + なる for the result of a change: 夜になる = night falls / it becomes night.<br>「が/で/へ」can't precede なる to express change — noun + になる, い-adjective + くなる: the basic pattern for sentences about change.","n2-g18":"<b>〜かたわら</b> = doing ~ on the side while keeping a main occupation (long-term): writing novels alongside a day job.<br>「あまり」follows an emotion noun (うれしさのあまり);「あげく」is followed by a bad outcome;「いたり」appears as「〜の至り」(光栄の至り) — none of these fit.","n2-g19":"<b>〜てからというもの</b> = ever since ~ (and it has stayed that way): ever since living alone, it's been nothing but takeout.<br>「てからということ/ところ/わけ」are not real patterns —「というもの」is fixed in this expression.","n2-g20":"<b>〜ざるを得ない</b> = have no choice but to ~: 従わざるを得ない.<br>「ず/ない/ぬ」can't attach to「を得ない」— ざる is the attributive form of the classical negative「ず」, and only an attributive form can take を.","n2-g21":"<b>〜かねない</b> = could well happen (something bad): an accident might well occur.<br>「かねる」means the opposite = find it hard to ~ (お答えしかねます);「きれない」means can't finish doing ~;「えない(得ない)」means impossible — the easiest set to mix up, since two of them are near-opposites.","n2-g22":"<b>〜に越したことはない</b> = nothing beats ~ / it's best to ~: Not smoking is best.<br>「が/を/より」 before 「越したことはない」 are all wrong — this is a set phrase, so memorise it together with its particle 「に」.","n2-g23":"<b>〜はぬきにして</b> = leaving ~ aside: Joking aside, let's get down to business.<br>「はずしにして/のぞきにして/とりにして」 are not Japanese — 「ぬきで/ぬきにして」 is the fixed form (挨拶ぬきで = skipping the greetings).","n2-g24":"<b>〜つつある</b> = to be gradually ~ing (written style): The economy is steadily recovering.<br>「ながらある/ておりある/がけある」 do not exist — 「つつ」+ある shows a change in progress, and it is common in news writing.","n2-g25":"<b>〜どころではない</b> = to be in no position / have no time for ~: Work is piled sky-high; travelling is out of the question.<br>「ばかりではない」 means \"not only ~\"; 「どおり」 means \"in accordance with ~\"; 「くらいではない」 is not a pattern — どころ strongly rejects the option being raised.","n2-g26":"<b>〜ずにはいられない</b> = can't help ~ing: I can't help laughing whenever I see it.<br>「ない/なくて/ぬ」 before 「にはいられない」 are all wrong — 「ず」 is the fixed classical negative form (する→せずには).","n2-g27":"<b>〜てはじめて</b> = only after ~ (do you first realise something): Only after falling ill do you understand the value of health.<br>「たら/ても/たり」 do not combine with 「はじめて」 — the fixed connection is て-form + はじめて.","n1-g18":"<b>〜んがために</b> = in order to ~ (at all costs; classical style): 勝たんがため = in order to win.<br>ん comes from the classical volitional auxiliary 「む」 and attaches to the 未然形 (勝た); 「い/ぬ/ろう」 cannot be used here.","n1-g19":"<b>〜ながらに</b> = while in the state of ~ (idiomatic): 涙ながらに語る = to tell something in tears.<br>「ばかりに」 means \"simply because ~\"; 「がちに」 means \"tend to ~\"; 「ずくめ」 means \"nothing but ~\" — 「涙ながらに・生まれながらに」 are set idioms.","n1-g20":"<b>〜にたえない</b> = cannot bear to ~: 見るにたえない = too painful/awful to watch.<br>「を/が/へ」 don't work before 「たえない」 — 「見るにたえない・聞くにたえない」 always take に.","n1-g21":"To modify a noun, use the attributive form <b>べからざる</b>: 欠くべからざる人材 = indispensable talent.<br>「べからず」 is the conclusive form and can only end a sentence (立ち入るべからず = No entry); 「べきでない」 cannot attach to a noun; 「べくもない」 means \"there is no way to ~\" — this is the classical-Japanese split between attributive and conclusive forms.","n1-g22":"<b>〜にあるまじき</b> = unbecoming of ~, something a ~ should never do: 教師にあるまじき行為.<br>「あるべき行為」 would mean \"the conduct one ought to show,\" which contradicts the earlier mention of deceiving students; 「らしき/ごとき」 mean \"seeming / like,\" which doesn't fit the meaning.","n1-g23":"<b>〜を皮切りに</b> = starting with ~: the tour began with the Tokyo performance.<br>「が/に/で」 don't work before 「皮切りに」 — the pattern is fixed with を, so memorise set phrases together with their particle.","n1-g24":"<b>〜ずくめ</b> = nothing but ~, all ~ (いいことずくめ = one good thing after another; also 黒ずくめ = dressed all in black, 規則ずくめ = hemmed in by rules).<br>「まみれ」 means covered in something dirty (泥まみれ, 汗まみれ — negative only); 「がかり」 means taking ~ to do (三日がかり = taking three days); 「めいた」 means having a hint of ~ (謎めいた = mysterious).","n1-g25":"<b>〜きらいがある</b> = to have a (bad) tendency to ~: to assume the worst about everything.<br>The きらい here does not mean \"dislike\"; 「このみ(好み)」 is a preference; 「ならい(習い)」 is a custom (世の習い = the way of the world); 「さかい(境)」 is a boundary — all homophone traps.","n1-g26":"<b>AがBとあいまって</b> = A combined with B, each reinforcing the other: talent plus hard work.<br>「に/を/へ+あいまって」 are all ungrammatical — 「と相まって」 is fixed with と.","n1-g27":"<b>〜なりに</b> = in one's own way, in a way suited to ~: even children have worries of their own.<br>「ずつ」 means ~ at a time; 「ぶり」 marks an interval (三年ぶり = after three years) or a manner (話しぶり = way of speaking); 「げ」 attaches to an adjective stem (悲しげ = looking sad) — neither the connection nor the meaning fits.","n5-p11":"<b>きたない (dirty) = きれいじゃない (not clean)</b>.<br>「きれいだ」 is the opposite; 「ひろい (spacious) / あかるい (bright)」 describe other qualities — the negative of an antonym equals the synonym, which is the basic pattern of 言い換え (paraphrase) questions.","n5-p12":"<b>もうすぐ = あとすこしで (in just a little while)</b>.<br>「ずっとまえに」 means long ago; 「いま」 means right now (already started — a different meaning); 「ゆっくり」 means slowly.","n5-p13":"<b>へた (bad at something) = じょうずじゃない</b>.<br>「じょうずだ」 is the opposite; 「すき / きらい」 are about liking or disliking — skill (上手/下手) and preference (好き/嫌い) are two separate things, so don't mix them up.","n5-p14":"<b>おおぜい = たくさん (many)</b>, but used only for <b>people</b>.<br>「すこしの」 is the opposite; 「せがたかい (tall) / ゆうめいな (famous)」 mean something else — おおぜい can only describe people; for large numbers of things, use たくさん.","n5-p15":"<b>やすい (cheap) = ねだんが たかくない (the price is not high)</b>.<br>「たかい」 is the opposite; 「ふるい (old) / ちいさい (small)」 describe other qualities of the shop.","n5-p16":"<b>はしる (to run) ≒ ジョギングする</b> (running in the park every morning = jogging).<br>「あるく」 is to walk (a different speed); 「およぐ」 is to swim and 「やすむ」 is to rest — neither fits.","n5-p17":"<b>ゆうがた (evening) = the time <b>before it gets dark</b></b>.<br>「あさはやく」 is early morning, 「ひるの12時」 is noon, 「よるおそく」 is late at night — time words (あさ/ひる/ゆうがた/よる) are essential N5 material.","n5-p18":"<b>じょうぶ (sturdy) = strong and hard to break = こわれにくい</b>.<br>「こわれやすい」 is the opposite; 「かるい (light) / たかい (expensive)」 describe other qualities — learn 「〜にくい (hard to ~) / 〜やすい (easy to ~)」 together as a pair.","n3-p13":"<b>たまたま = by chance, happened to</b>.<br>「いつも」 = always; 「わざと」 = on purpose (the exact opposite idea); 「必ず」 = without fail — the たまたま/わざと contrast is a frequent test point.","n3-p14":"<b>あわてる = to panic, to rush in a fluster = 急いで</b>.<br>「ゆっくり」 is the opposite; 「静かに」 = quietly, 「喜んで」 = gladly — あわてて carries a flustered, flustered-and-hasty nuance, while 急いで is a neutral \"quickly\"; in paraphrase questions, pick the closest match.","n3-p15":"<b>一致する = to come to match, to agree = 同じになる</b>.<br>「違う」 = to differ (the opposite); 「変わる」 = to change; 「なくなる」 = to disappear — 「意見が一致/対立」 is very common in news writing.","n3-p16":"<b>わざと = deliberately = そうしようと思って</b>.<br>「偶然」 = by chance (the opposite); 「仕方なく」 = having no other choice; 「知らないで」 = without knowing — deliberate vs. accidental is the classic contrast pair in synonym questions.","n3-p17":"The compound verb <b>〜直す = to do ~ over again</b>: 書き直す = to rewrite.<br>「書き終える」 = to finish writing, 「書き始める」 = to start writing, 「やめる」 = to quit — the compound-verb endings 「〜直す/〜終える/〜始める/〜かける」 are a key N3 set.","n3-p18":"<b>曖昧(あいまい) = vague, unclear = はっきりしない</b>.<br>「よくわかる」 = easy to understand (the opposite); 「長い/正しい」 describe entirely different qualities.","n3-p19":"<b>おおよそ = roughly, approximately = だいたい</b>.<br>「正確に」 = precisely (the opposite); 「何度も」 = over and over; 「最後に」 = lastly — おおよそ/およそ/約/だいたい all belong to one group.","n3-p20":"<b>行方(ゆくえ) = whereabouts, where someone has gone = どこにいるか</b>: 行方不明 = missing.<br>The other options ask what someone wants to do, who they are, or when they will return — none of these is what 「ゆくえ」 means.","n4-p11":"<b>出発する = 出る (to depart / leave)</b>.<br>「着く」 is the opposite end of the trip (to arrive); 「止まる」 is to stop; 「曲がる」 is to turn — 出発⇔到着 is a pair that often gets tested together.","n4-p12":"<b>一生懸命 = がんばって (with all one's effort)</b>.<br>「ときどき」 is sometimes; 「いやいや」 is reluctantly; 「ゆっくり」 is slowly — none of them carry the sense of giving it everything you've got.","n4-p13":"<b>びっくりする = おどろく (to be surprised)</b> — the casual word and its written-style counterpart.<br>「よろこぶ」 (to be glad), 「こまる」 (to be troubled) and 「わらう」 (to laugh) are all different emotions.","n4-p14":"<b>すいている = empty, not crowded</b>.<br>「人が多かった」 = こんでいる (crowded), the exact opposite; 「おくれて/とまって」 mean delayed or out of service — すく⇔こむ is a must-know pair.","n4-p15":"<b>やっと = finally, after a lot of effort</b>.<br>「すぐに/かんたんに」 mean quickly or easily (the opposite of taking effort); 「まだ」 means not yet — やっと carries the nuance that it wasn't easy to get there.","n4-p16":"<b>しかる = おこる (to scold)</b>, and the passive しかられる = おこられる.<br>「ほめられる」 (to be praised) is the opposite; 「よばれる」 is to be called, 「たすけられる」 is to be helped.","n4-p17":"<b>複雑(ふくざつ) = not simple = かんたんではない</b>.<br>「かんたんだ」 is the opposite; 「おもしろい/あたらしい」 describe other qualities — 複雑⇔簡単.","n4-p18":"<b>治る(なおる) = to get better, to recover = よくなる</b>.<br>「わるくなる」 is to get worse; 「はじまる」 is to begin; 「うつる」 is to be passed on (かぜがうつる) — while you're at it, note that 「治る」 is intransitive and 「治す」 is transitive.","n2-p12":"<b>直ちに(ただちに)= immediately = すぐに</b>(written, formal register).<br>「ゆっくり/あとで」mean slowly / later (the opposite);「いやいや」means reluctantly — 直ちに/至急/即座に all belong to the \"right away\" family.","n2-p13":"<b>妥当(だとう)= appropriate and reasonable = 適切</b>.<br>「無理だ」means unworkable (the opposite);「複雑/新しい」describe other qualities entirely — 妥当な判断・妥当な価格, a high-frequency word in editorials and opinion pieces.","n2-p14":"<b>うんざりする= to be thoroughly fed up (from repetition or excess)= すっかりいやになる</b>.<br>「うれしそう」is the opposite;「おどろいた/ねむそう」describe other expressions — 長い話にうんざり carries the feeling of \"I've had more than enough of this.\"","n2-p15":"<b>無関係 = unrelated = つながりがない</b>.<br>「関係が深い」is the opposite;「似ている/同時に起きた」describe resemblance or simultaneity, which is not the same as whether a connection exists.","n2-p16":"<b>スムーズに = smoothly = 順調に</b>.<br>「難航して」means to run into difficulty (the opposite);「ゆっくり」only refers to slow speed;「急に」means suddenly — pairing a loanword with its 漢語 equivalent is a classic N2 言い換え pattern.","n2-p17":"<b>とっさに = in an instant, reflexively = 瞬間的に</b>.<br>「ゆっくりと」is the opposite;「前もって」means in advance (あらかじめ);「しぶしぶ」means reluctantly — とっさの判断 = a split-second decision.","n2-p18":"<b>緩和(かんわ)= easing, relaxing (of rules)= ゆるくなる</b>.<br>「厳しくなる」= tightening / 厳格化 (the opposite);「なくなる」= abolition;「新しく作られる」= newly established — 規制緩和 is common in the news, so don't get the direction backwards.","n2-p19":"<b>そっけない = curt, cold and indifferent = 冷たい感じ</b>.<br>「親切だ」is the opposite;「落ち着いている」means composed (neutral);「あやしい」means suspicious — そっけない返事 = a curt, dismissive reply.","n5-p19":"<b>おもい(heavy) = かるくない(not light)</b>.<br>「かるい(light)」is the opposite; 「やすい(cheap)/ちいさい(small)」describe other qualities — heavy ⇔ light.","n5-p20":"<b>ちかい(near) = とおくない(not far)</b>.<br>「とおい」is the opposite; 「ひろい/あたらしい」describe other qualities — near ⇔ far.","n5-p21":"<b>むずかしい(difficult) = やさしくない(not easy)</b>.<br>「やさしい」is the opposite; 「たのしい/みじかい」describe other qualities.","n5-p22":"<b>しずか(quiet) = うるさくない(not noisy)</b>.<br>「うるさい」is the opposite; 「きたない/せまい」describe other qualities.","n5-p23":"<b>あさ(morning) = the stretch of time before noon</b>.<br>「よるおそく」is late at night, 「ひるごはんのあと」is after lunch, 「いちにちじゅう」is all day long — all time expressions.","n5-p24":"<b>おいしい = tastes good(あじがいい)</b>.<br>「あじがわるい」means it tastes bad (the opposite); 「たかい/おおきい」describe other qualities.","n5-p25":"<b>いそがしい(busy) = ひまじゃない(not free)</b>.<br>「ひまだ」is the opposite; 「やさしい/わかい」describe other qualities — busy ⇔ free.","n5-p26":"<b>つめたい(cold to the touch) = あたたかくない(not warm)</b>.<br>「あたたかい」is the opposite; 「あまい/おおい」describe other qualities.","n1-p12":"<b>暗に(あんに) = implicitly, indirectly = 遠回しに (hinting without saying it outright)</b>.<br>「はっきりと」means clearly, stating it outright (the opposite); 「繰り返し」= repeatedly; 「公の場で」= in public — 暗に示す means to imply or hint at something.","n1-p13":"<b>氷解(ひょうかい) = (doubts) melting away completely, like ice = すっかり解けた</b>.<br>「深まった/増えた/残った」all mean the doubts are still there or have even grown. It's a metaphor: 誤解が氷解する = a misunderstanding dissolves completely.","n1-p14":"<b>気兼ねなく = without worrying what others think = 遠慮せずに</b>.<br>「おそるおそる」= timidly, nervously (the opposite); 「怒りながら/仕方なく」describe different attitudes entirely — 気兼ね is the hesitation you feel out of consideration for other people.","n1-p15":"The idiom <b>軌道に乗る = to get on track = 順調に進み始める</b>.<br>The other three describe failure, still being at the planning stage, or being called off — either the opposite of getting on track, or a different stage altogether.","n1-p16":"<b>ひたむきに = wholeheartedly, single-mindedly</b>.<br>「いやいや」= reluctantly; 「時々思い出したように」= only now and then, when it crosses your mind; 「人に言われて」= only because someone told you to (passive) — ひたむきな努力 is effort with nothing else pulling your attention away.","n1-p17":"The idiom <b>物議を醸す(ぶつぎをかもす) = to stir up public controversy</b>.<br>「注目されなかった/忘れられた」mean no one paid attention; 「高く評価された」means it was well received — 物議を醸す carries a distinctly negative, controversial nuance.","n1-p18":"The four-character compound <b>時期尚早(じきしょうそう) = too early, premature = まだ早すぎる</b>.<br>「遅すぎる/期限が過ぎた」point the opposite way; 「ちょうどよい」= timely — don't let the 尚 fool you into reading it as \"there's still time.\"","n1-p19":"The idiom <b>犬猿の仲(けんえんのなか) = like cats and dogs</b> (in Japanese it's dogs and monkeys, who fight on sight).<br>「仲がよい/昔からの友人」are the opposite; 「初対面」means meeting for the first time — learn the animal idioms in pairs (犬猿の仲, 馬が合う).","n5-p27":"<b>まっすぐ = straight ahead, without turning (まがらないで)</b>.<br>「みぎにまがって」means turning right (the opposite); 「ゆっくり/はやく」describe speed, not direction.","n5-p28":"<b>こんでいる = crowded, lots of people (ひとがおおい)</b>.<br>「ひとがすくない」= すいている, the opposite; 「しまっていた/とおかった」describe entirely different situations (closed / far away).","n4-p19":"<b>毎日 = ひをあけずに (every single day, without skipping a day)</b>.<br>「ときどき/たまに」mean sometimes or occasionally; 「いちども(〜ない)」means not even once — 毎日 vs たまに is a contrast of frequency.","n4-p20":"<b>まずい = おいしくない (bad-tasting)</b>.<br>「おいしい」is the opposite; 「高い/辛い」describe other qualities (expensive / spicy).","n4-p21":"<b>延期 = postponed to a later day (あとの日に)</b>.<br>「中止」means cancelled (≠ postponed); 「早く/みじかく」mean moved earlier or shortened — 延期 only changes the date, and it is often tested against 中止.","n4-p22":"<b>明るい性格 = a cheerful, lively personality</b>.<br>「くらくて静か」means gloomy and quiet (the opposite); 「まじめ/こわい」describe other traits (serious / scary).","n4-p23":"<b>たいへん (tough) = らくではない (not easy-going)</b>.<br>「らくだ/かんたんだ」mean easy or simple (the opposite); 「おもしろい」describes a different impression (interesting).","n4-p24":"<b>中止 = やめる (cancelled, not held at all)</b>.<br>「おくれる/はやまる」mean delayed or moved up (≠ cancelled); 「つづく」means it continues — 中止 means it simply won't happen.","n3-p25":"<b>約束を守る = to keep a promise (not break it: やぶらない)</b>。<br>「やぶる」means to break/go back on a promise (the opposite);「わすれる/うたがう」(forget/doubt) are different actions.","n3-p26":"<b>便利 = easy to use and helpful (つかいやすくて役に立つ)</b>。<br>「つかいにくい」means hard to use (the opposite);「こわれている/たかい」(broken/expensive) describe different situations.","n3-p27":"<b>あわてて = in a panic, in a flustered rush (急いで落ち着かず)</b>。<br>「ゆっくり/しずかに」(slowly/quietly) are the opposite;「よろこんで」(gladly) is a different attitude.","n3-p28":"<b>対立 = to clash with each other (ぶつかりあう)</b>。<br>「よくにて/一致して」(be alike/agree) are the opposite;「関係なくて」means unrelated — 意見が対立 and 意見が一致 form a natural pair.","n3-p29":"<b>はやる (to do good business) = 客がたくさん来る, plenty of customers come</b>。<br>「客が来ない」is the opposite;「しずか/たかい」(quiet/expensive) describe different situations — 店がはやる = the shop is thriving (繁盛).","n3-p30":"<b>礼儀正しい = polite and well-mannered (マナーがよくてていねい)</b>。<br>「らんぼう」(rough, violent) is the opposite;「つめたい/おもしろい」(cold/interesting) are different qualities.","n2-p20":"<b>賛成 = to agree, to be in favour</b>。<br>「反対」(oppose) is the opposite;「おどろく/むしする」(be surprised/ignore) are different actions.","n2-p21":"<b>順調 = going smoothly, without trouble (問題なく)</b>。<br>「難航して」(hitting a deadlock)「おくれて」(delayed)「とまって」(stopped) are the opposite or signs of trouble.","n4-p25":"<b>うそ = not true (不是真的 / a lie)</b>.<br>「ほんとう」is the opposite; 「ながい/おもしろい」describe entirely different qualities.","n4-p26":"<b>やっと = finally, after much effort (ようやく)</b>.<br>「すぐに/かんたんに」mean quickly or easily (the opposite); 「まだ」means not yet — やっと carries the feeling that it took real effort to get there.","n4-p27":"<b>ほめる (to praise) = よく言う (to speak well of someone)</b>. The passive ほめられる means to be praised.<br>「しかられた」means to be scolded (the opposite); 「よばれた/たすけられた」are different actions.","n4-p28":"<b>正直 = honest, i.e. not telling lies (うそをつかない)</b>.<br>「うそをつく」means to tell a lie (the opposite); 「あかるい/しずか」describe different qualities.","n3-p21":"<b>のんき = easygoing, laid-back, not fussed about details</b>.<br>「急いでいる/厳しい/よく怒る」are all opposite or simply different personality traits.","n3-p22":"<b>ほっとする = あんしんする (to feel relieved)</b>.<br>「がっかり/おどろく/おこる」express different emotions.","n3-p23":"<b>くわしい (detailed) = 細かくてよくわかる (fine-grained and easy to understand)</b>.<br>「みじかくて不十分」(short and insufficient) is the opposite; 「うそが多い/おもしろい」describe different qualities.","n3-p24":"<b>無理がある = there is something unworkable or unreasonable about it (できそうにない)</b>.<br>The other choices mean things like \"no problem at all\" — either the opposite or unrelated.","n2-p22":"<b>ずうずうしい = shameless, pushy, having no reserve (あつかましい)</b>.<br>「遠慮ぶかい」means modest and reserved (the opposite);「おとなしい/しんせつ」describe entirely different traits.","n2-p23":"<b>やっかい = troublesome, hard to deal with (めんどうで解決しにくい)</b>.<br>「かんたん」is the opposite;「どうでもいい/おもしろい」describe a different attitude toward the matter.","n2-p24":"<b>もくもくと = silently and single-mindedly (だまって一心に)</b>.<br>The others mean chatting while working, doing it reluctantly, or stopping and starting — none of them carry the sense of quiet concentration.","n2-p25":"<b>一瞬 = あっという間に (in an instant)</b>.<br>「ゆっくり/なかなか/だんだん」all express slowness or gradual change (the opposite).","n2-p26":"<b>的確 = accurate and right on the point</b>.<br>「あいまい」means vague (the opposite);「ながい/まちがっていた」point to a different problem altogether.","n2-p27":"<b>却下 = to reject, to turn down (みとめずに退ける)</b>.<br>「うけいれた」means accepted (the opposite);「ほめた/検討中」describe a different response.","n2-p28":"<b>一貫して = consistently from start to finish (ずっと)</b>.<br>「ときどき/とちゅうから」suggest something intermittent or starting partway through;「しぶしぶ」means reluctantly — 一貫 means the stance never changes.","n2-p29":"<b>よそよそしい = distant and cold, as if toward a stranger</b>.<br>「親しげ」means friendly and close (the opposite);「らんぼう/あかるい」describe different qualities.","n1-p20":"<b>頓挫 = to break down / fail partway through (途中でだめになる)</b>.<br>The other options mean completed, about to begin, or going smoothly — all the opposite of hitting a setback.","n1-p21":"The idiom <b>腹を割る = to open up and speak your true feelings (本音で正直に)</b>.<br>「うわべだけ」means only on the surface (the opposite); 「おこりながら/みじかく」describe a manner of speaking, not honesty.","n1-p22":"<b>一理ある = there is some truth to it / it has a point (もっともな点がある)</b>.<br>「まったく間違い」means completely wrong (the opposite); the rest mean not understanding, or not caring.","n1-p23":"<b>徒労 = wasted effort, labour that comes to nothing (むだになる)</b>.<br>「みのる (bears fruit)/評価された (was recognised)」are the opposite; 「つづいている」just means it is still going on.","n1-p24":"The idiom <b>眉唾 = dubious, not to be taken at face value (うたがわしい)</b>.<br>「信頼できる/たしか」are the opposite; 「有名」is beside the point.","n1-p25":"<b>気が置けない = requires no formality; someone you can relax with</b> (often misread as meaning the opposite!).<br>「油断できない/つきあいにくい」are exactly that mistaken reading; 「よく知らない」is unrelated.","n1-p26":"The idiom <b>しのぎを削る = to compete fiercely</b>.<br>「協力/無関係/話し合って」none of them convey fierce competition.","n1-p27":"The four-character phrase <b>慇懃無礼 = so excessively polite that it becomes rude</b>.<br>「親切/親しみやすい」are the opposite; 「あからさまに乱暴」is openly rough behaviour — plain rudeness, which is different.","n4-o10":"Full sentence: <b>夜おそくまでゲームするの</b>は、体によくない (Gaming until late at night is bad for your health). Word order: 夜 → おそく → <b>まで</b> → ゲームするの, ★ = <b>まで</b>.<br>「夜おそくまで」 = until late at night; 「の」 nominalises the clause so it can serve as the topic.","n4-o11":"Full sentence: <b>日本語を勉強する人の</b>ための本 (a book for people who study Japanese). Word order: 日本語 → を → <b>勉強する</b> → 人の, ★ = <b>勉強する</b>.<br>The whole clause 「日本語を勉強する」 modifies 「人」, and 「の+ため」 then marks who it is for.","n4-o12":"Full sentence: 彼が<b>何時に帰ってくる</b>か、だれも知りません (Nobody knows what time he is coming home). Word order: 何時 → に → <b>帰って</b> → くる, ★ = <b>帰って</b>.<br>The time expression 「何時に」 comes first, and 「帰ってくる」 is a compound verb of direction whose order is fixed.","n4-o13":"Full sentence: <b>へやを出るときは</b>、電気を消してください (When you leave the room, please turn off the light). Word order: へや → を → <b>出る</b> → ときは, ★ = <b>出る</b>.<br>In 「(place)を出る」 the を marks the place you exit from; 「dictionary form + とき」 = when ~.","n4-o14":"Full sentence: わたしが<b>いちばんよく聞くの</b>は、この曲です (The one I listen to most often is this song). Word order: いちばん → よく → <b>聞く</b> → の, ★ = <b>聞く</b>.<br>「いちばんよく」 = most often (いちばん modifies よく, so the order cannot be reversed), and 「の」 nominalises the clause.","n4-o15":"Full sentence: <b>熱が出たせい</b>で、学校を休みました (Because I ran a fever, I took the day off school). Word order: 熱 → が → <b>出た</b> → せい, ★ = <b>出た</b>.<br>「熱が出る」 = to run a fever (a set phrase); 「た-form + せいで」 blames the result on that cause.","n3-o10":"Full sentence: <b>留学の費用をためる</b>ために (in order to save up the money for studying abroad). Word order: 留学の → 費用 → <b>を</b> → ためる, ★ = <b>を</b>.<br>「留学の」 modifies 「費用」, the object takes を before the verb, and 「dictionary form + ために」 expresses purpose.","n3-o11":"Full sentence: <b>どの方法を選ぶか</b>によって (depending on which method you choose). Word order: どの → 方法 → <b>を</b> → 選ぶか, ★ = <b>を</b>.<br>「どの」 must sit directly before the noun; the embedded question 「〜か」 works as a single noun phrase, which 「によって」 then attaches to.","n1-p28":"Idiom: <b>絵に描いた餅 = a pie in the sky; something that looks good but is useless or unachievable</b>.<br>The other options mean achievable, already achieved, or the literal sense (a painted rice cake) — none of them fit.","n1-p29":"Idiom: <b>間髪を入れず = without a moment's hesitation, instantly (すぐさま)</b>.<br>「しばらく考えてから」is the opposite; 「しぶしぶ/あいまいに」describe a different attitude altogether.","n5-o10":"Full sentence: それは<b>だれがかいた絵</b>ですか (Whose painting is that? / Who painted that picture?). Order: だれ→が→<b>かいた</b>→絵, so ★ = slot 3 = <b>かいた</b>.<br>Key point: 「だれが」is the subject of a relative clause, and the verb 「かいた」modifies 「絵」(noun modification) — the same frame as 「母が作ったケーキ」.","n5-o11":"Full sentence: <b>めがねをかけている人</b> (the person wearing glasses). Order: めがねを→かけて→<b>いる</b>→人, so ★ = <b>いる</b>.<br>「めがねをかける」is a set collocation (not する), 「〜ている」expresses an ongoing state, and the whole phrase modifies 「人」.","n5-o12":"Full sentence: 山田さんは<b>料理を作るの</b>がじょうずです (Yamada is good at cooking). Order: 料理→を→<b>作る</b>→の, so ★ = <b>作る</b>.<br>「の」nominalises 「料理を作る」so it can take 「が じょうず」— a verb cannot attach directly to が上手.","n5-o13":"Full sentence: 音楽を<b>ねるまえに聞きます</b> (I listen to music before going to bed). Order: ねる→まえ→<b>に</b>→聞きます, so ★ = <b>に</b>.<br>「dictionary-form verb + まえに」= before doing ~; 「聞きます」is the main verb, so it must come last.","n5-o14":"Full sentence: トイレは<b>このろうかのおく</b>にあります (The toilet is at the far end of this hallway). Order: この→ろうか→<b>の</b>→おく, so ★ = <b>の</b>.<br>「この」must sit directly in front of its noun (このろうか); 「AのB」= B of A, and the position noun 「おく」comes last, followed by に.","n5-o15":"Full sentence: 去年わたしが<b>京都のお寺でとった</b>写真です (This is a photo I took at a temple in Kyoto last year). Order: 京都→の→<b>お寺で</b>→とった, so ★ = <b>お寺で</b>.<br>「京都の」modifies 「お寺」, place + で marks where the action happened, and the verb 「とった」closes the clause modifying 「写真」.","n3-o12":"Full sentence: <b>食事のあとに</b>飲んでください (Take it after meals). Word order: 食事→の→<b>あと</b>→に, ★=<b>あと</b>.<br>「名詞+の+あとに」= after ~. Note the difference in how it attaches compared with 「動詞た形+あとで」, which takes a verb in た-form instead of a noun+の.","n3-o13":"Full sentence: <b>今にも泣き出しそうな</b>顔 (a face that looks about to burst into tears). Word order: 今にも→泣き→<b>出し</b>→そうな, ★=<b>出し</b>.<br>「今にも〜そうだ」is a fixed pairing (on the verge of ~); 「泣き出す」= to burst out crying, a compound verb, so its two halves can't be split or reordered.","n3-o14":"Full sentence: <b>この歌を聞く</b>たびに (every time I hear this song). Word order: この→歌→<b>を</b>→聞く, ★=<b>を</b>.<br>「辞書形+たびに」= every time ~; 「この」must sit directly before the noun it modifies, 「歌」.","n3-o15":"Full sentence: 事故の<b>原因がどこにあるか</b>について (concerning where the cause of the accident lies). Word order: 原因→が→<b>どこ</b>→にあるか, ★=<b>どこ</b>.<br>The embedded question ends in 「〜か」, which turns the whole clause into a noun phrase; 「原因がどこにある」= where the cause lies.","n2-o10":"Full sentence: <b>医者に止められたの</b>にもかかわらず (despite having been stopped by the doctor). Word order: 医者→に→<b>止められた</b>→の, ★=<b>止められた</b>.<br>「医者に止められる」is a passive construction; 「の」nominalises the clause so that 「にもかかわらず」can follow it.","n2-o11":"Full sentence: 彼の<b>費用を度外視した意見</b>には、賛成しかねる (I can hardly agree with that opinion of his, which disregards the cost). Word order: 費用→を→<b>度外視した</b>→意見, ★=<b>度外視した</b>.<br>The whole clause 「費用を度外視した」modifies 「意見」; 「〜かねる」= be unable to / find it hard to ~.","n2-o12":"Full sentence: <b>だれの助けも借りる</b>ことなく (without borrowing anyone's help). Word order: だれ→の→<b>助け</b>→も借りる, ★=<b>助け</b>.<br>「だれの〜も+negative」= not anyone's ~ at all; 「〜ことなく」= without doing ~ (written style).","n2-o13":"Full sentence: <b>操作が簡単だという</b>点で (in that it is simple to operate). Word order: 操作→が→<b>簡単だ</b>→という, ★=<b>簡単だ</b>.<br>「〜という点で」= in respect of ~; the quoted content 「操作が簡単だ」goes before という.","n5-o16":"Full sentence: これは<b>母が作ったケーキ</b>です。(This is the cake my mother made.)<br>★(3rd slot) = 作った. 母が作った is a relative clause modifying a noun: 母 → が → 作った → ケーキ. Note that が (not は) marks the subject inside a relative clause.","n5-o17":"Full sentence: つくえの<b>上に本が</b>あります。(There is a book on the desk.)<br>★(3rd slot) = 本. Pattern: location 「上に」 + subject 「本が」 + the existence verb ある. In existence sentences the place takes に and the thing that exists takes が.","n5-o18":"Full sentence: わたしは<b>りんごを三つか</b>いました。(I bought three apples.)<br>★(3rd slot) = 三つ. Object を + a counter/quantity, then か of 「か+いました=買いました」. The quantity word goes after を, not before りんご.","n5-o19":"Full sentence: きのう<b>ともだちとこうえんを</b>さんぽしました。(Yesterday I took a walk in the park with a friend.)<br>★(3rd slot) = こうえん. Pattern: person と + place を + さんぽする. Verbs of motion through a space like さんぽする take を, not で.","n5-o20":"Full sentence: あの<b>めがねをかけている</b>人です。(That's the person wearing glasses.)<br>★(3rd slot) = かけて. めがねをかける + ている (an ongoing state) modifies 人. For glasses the verb is かける, and ている here means \"is (currently) wearing\", not an action in progress.","n5-o21":"Full sentence: まいあさ<b>六時におきてから</b>シャワーをあびます。(Every morning I take a shower after getting up at six.)<br>★(3rd slot) = おきて. Clock time + に + おきる + てから. てから means \"after doing\", so the て-form comes before から.","n5-o22":"Full sentence: この<b>りょうりはとてもおいしい</b>です。(This dish is very tasty.)<br>★(3rd slot) = とても. Pattern: topic は + adverb とても + adjective. The degree adverb goes right before the adjective it modifies.","n5-o23":"Full sentence: しゅうまつは<b>ともだちとえいがを</b>見ます。(On weekends I watch movies with a friend.)<br>★(3rd slot) = えいが. Pattern: person と + object を. The companion phrase with と comes before the object.","n2-o14":"Full sentence: <b>息子に会えたうれしさ</b>のあまり (out of sheer joy at being able to see his son). Word order: 息子→に→<b>会えた</b>→うれしさ, ★=<b>会えた</b>.<br>「〜のあまり」attaches to a noun of emotion; the clause 息子に会えた modifies うれしさ. The person you meet with 会う is marked by に.","n2-o15":"Full sentence: <b>双方が歩み寄らない</b>限り (unless both sides make concessions to each other). Word order: 双方→が→<b>歩み寄ら</b>→ない, ★=<b>歩み寄ら</b>.<br>「〜ない限り」= as long as ~ doesn't happen / unless ~; 歩み寄る = to meet halfway, for each side to give ground.","n1-o10":"Full sentence: <b>半年の試行期間</b>を経て (after a six-month trial period). Word order: 半年→の→<b>試行</b>→期間, ★=<b>試行</b>.<br>「半年の」modifies 試行期間 (試行+期間 is a compound noun and cannot be split); 「〜を経て」= after going through ~.","n1-o11":"Full sentence: <b>文句があると言わ</b>んばかりの顔 (a look that all but says \"I have a complaint\"). Word order: 文句→が→<b>ある</b>→と言わ, ★=<b>ある</b>.<br>「〜と言わんばかり」= as if to say ~; the quoted content 文句がある goes before と.","n1-o12":"Full sentence: <b>聞き手への配慮</b>に欠けるきらいがある (his explanations tend to lack consideration for the listener). Word order: 聞き手→へ→<b>の</b>→配慮, ★=<b>の</b>.<br>「への」= toward ~ / for ~ (へ plus の, so the phrase can modify a noun); 「〜に欠ける」= to lack; 「きらいがある」= to have a tendency to ~.","n1-o13":"Full sentence: <b>過去の失敗の教訓</b>を踏まえた (based on the lessons of past failures). Word order: 過去→の→<b>失敗の</b>→教訓, ★=<b>失敗の</b>.<br>「AのBのC」stacks modifiers one on the next: 過去の→失敗, then 失敗の→教訓; 「〜を踏まえる」= to take ~ as one's basis.","n1-o14":"Full sentence: <b>説明会での反応</b>いかんによっては (depending on the reaction at the information session). Word order: 説明会→で→<b>の</b>→反応, ★=<b>の</b>.<br>「での」= at ~ (place), used before a noun (で plus の); 「〜いかんによっては」= depending on ~ (N1 pattern).","n1-o15":"Full sentence: <b>不利な状況に置かれ</b>ながらも (even though placed in an unfavorable situation). Word order: 不利→な→<b>状況</b>→に置かれ, ★=<b>状況</b>.<br>The na-adjective 不利な modifies 状況; 「〜ながらも」= even though ~ (concessive).","n5-o24":"Full sentence: 日本語の<b>べんきょうはとてもおもしろい</b>です。(Studying Japanese is very interesting.)<br>★(3rd blank)=とても。Noun + は + adverb + adjective","n5-o25":"Full sentence: へやに<b>だれもいません</b>。(There is nobody in the room.)<br>★(3rd blank)=いま。だれも + negative = nobody / not anyone","n4-o16":"Full sentence: 日本語が<b>話せるようになりました</b>。(I've become able to speak Japanese.)<br>★(3rd blank)=なり。〜ようになる = to come to be able to ~ / to reach the point of ~","n4-o17":"Full sentence: 先生に<b>作文をなおしてもらい</b>ました。(I had my teacher correct my composition.)<br>★(3rd blank)=なおして。〜に〜てもらう + object + を = have someone do something for you","n4-o18":"Full sentence: この<b>ボタンを押すと</b>お湯が出ます。(If you press this button, hot water comes out.)<br>★(3rd blank)=押す。〜と = whenever / as soon as ~ (natural, automatic result)","n4-o19":"Full sentence: 荷物が<b>重すぎて一人では</b>持てません。(The luggage is too heavy to carry alone.)<br>★(3rd blank)=一人。〜すぎて = too ~, and as a result...","n4-o20":"Full sentence: 先生の<b>話によると明日</b>試験があるそうだ。(According to the teacher, there's a test tomorrow.)<br>★(3rd blank)=よると。〜によると = according to ~ (source of hearsay, pairs with そうだ)","n4-o21":"Full sentence: かぜを<b>ひかないように気</b>をつけます。(I'll be careful not to catch a cold.)<br>★(3rd blank)=ように。〜ないように = so as not to ~ / in order not to ~","n4-o22":"Full sentence: 京都へ<b>行ったことがあり</b>ます。(I have been to Kyoto.)<br>★(3rd blank)=が. 〜たことがある = have done ~ before (past experience)","n4-o23":"Full sentence: 日本に<b>来たばかりのころ</b>は大変でした。(It was tough when I had just come to Japan.)<br>★(3rd blank)=の. 〜たばかり = have just ~ed","n4-o24":"Full sentence: 雨が<b>降りそうな空だ</b>。(A sky that looks like it's going to rain.)<br>★(3rd blank)=空. Appearance そうな + noun","n4-o25":"Full sentence: 分からない<b>ところは先生に</b>聞いてください。(Please ask the teacher about the parts you don't understand.)<br>★(3rd blank)=先生. 〜は + person に + 聞く (ask someone)","n3-o16":"Full sentence: 彼は<b>留学の費用を</b>ためている。(He is saving up money for studying abroad.)<br>★(3rd blank)=費用. 留学の費用 (modifier + noun) + object marker を","n3-o17":"Full sentence: この薬を<b>飲んでからよくなり</b>ました。(I got better after taking this medicine.)<br>★(3rd blank)=よく. 〜てから + よくなる (after doing ~, it improved)","n3-o18":"Full sentence: 子どもの<b>ころよくこの川で</b>泳いだ。(As a child I often swam in this river.)<br>★(3rd blank)=この川. Time expression + place で","n3-o19":"Full sentence: 駅に<b>着いたら電話して</b>ください。(Please call me when you arrive at the station.)<br>★(3rd blank)=電話. 〜たら = when / if ~","n1-o16":"Full sentence: 彼は<b>努力があってこそ</b>成功した。(It was precisely because of his effort that he succeeded.)<br>★(3rd slot) = あって. 〜があってこそ = only because there is ~; it is precisely thanks to ~","n1-o17":"Full sentence: この計画は<b>支援なくしては実現</b>できない。(Without support, this plan cannot be realised.)<br>★(3rd slot) = は. 〜なくしては〜ない = without ~, ~ cannot happen","n1-o18":"Full sentence: 彼は<b>逆境にありながらも</b>諦めなかった。(Even while in adversity, he did not give up.)<br>★(3rd slot) = あり. 〜にありながらも = even though placed in ~, still ~","n1-o19":"Full sentence: 勝た<b>んがために手段</b>を選ばなかった。(He stopped at nothing in order to win.)<br>★(3rd slot) = ために. 〜んがために = in order to ~ (literary style)","n1-o20":"Full sentence: 東京公演<b>を皮切りに全国</b>ツアーが始まる。(Starting with the Tokyo performance, a nationwide tour begins.)<br>★(3rd slot) = に. 〜を皮切りに = starting with ~; beginning from ~","n1-o21":"Full sentence: 彼の功績は<b>賞賛するに余り</b>ある。(His achievements deserve the highest praise.)<br>★(3rd slot) = に. 〜に余りある = more than ~ can express; ~ falls short of doing it justice","n1-o22":"Full sentence: 子ども<b>には子どもなり</b>の悩みがある。(Children have worries of their own.)<br>★(3rd slot) = 子ども. 〜なりの = ~'s own way of; suited to what ~ is","n1-o23":"Full sentence: 彼は<b>泣くまいとして</b>涙をこらえた。(Trying not to cry, he held back his tears.)<br>★(3rd slot) = と. 〜まいとする = try hard not to ~","n3-o20":"Full sentence: 努力<b>しない限り成功は</b>ない。(You won't succeed unless you make an effort.)<br>★(3rd slot) = 成功. 〜ない限り = \"unless / as long as you don't ~\"","n3-o21":"Full sentence: 彼は<b>約束を守る人</b>だ。(He's a man of his word.)<br>★(3rd slot) = 守る. 約束を守る modifies 人 (a plain-form clause before the noun)","n3-o22":"Full sentence: この道は<b>すべりやすいので注意</b>が必要だ。(This road is slippery, so you need to be careful.)<br>★(3rd slot) = ので. 〜やすい (easy to ~) + ので (because)","n3-o23":"Full sentence: 雨が<b>降り続いたため試合</b>は中止だ。(The match is cancelled because it kept raining.)<br>★(3rd slot) = ため. 〜ため = \"because of ~\" (states a cause)","n3-o24":"Full sentence: 先生に<b>やり方を教えてもらって</b>解けた。(I got the teacher to show me how to do it, and then I could solve it.)<br>★(3rd slot) = 教えて. Pattern: 〜に〜てもらう (have someone do something for you), with the object marked by を","n3-o25":"Full sentence: この店は<b>サービスがいいこと</b>で有名だ。(This shop is famous for its good service.)<br>★(3rd slot) = いい. 〜ことで有名 = \"famous for the fact that ~\"","n2-o16":"Full sentence: 急な残業で<b>約束をキャンセルせざるを</b>得なかった。(Sudden overtime left me with no choice but to cancel my plans.)<br>★(3rd slot) = キャンセル. 〜ざるを得ない = \"have no choice but to ~\"","n2-o17":"Full sentence: 努力した<b>かいがあって合格</b>できた。(My efforts paid off and I passed.)<br>★(3rd slot) = あって. 〜かいがあって = \"~ was worth it / paid off\"","n2-o18":"Full sentence: この問題は<b>複雑なだけに</b>解決が難しい。(Precisely because this problem is complex, it is hard to solve.)<br>★(3rd blank)=だけ. な-adjective + なだけに = precisely because / all the more so because","n2-o19":"Full sentence: 彼の発言は<b>あまりに唐突で</b>皆が驚いた。(His remark was so abrupt that everyone was startled.)<br>★(3rd blank)=唐突. あまりに〜で = excessively ~, so ~ that (gives the cause of the following result)","n2-o20":"Full sentence: この結果は<b>努力のたまもの</b>だ。(This result is the fruit of hard work.)<br>★(3rd blank)=たま. 〜のたまもの = the fruit/product of ~ (a positive outcome earned by effort)","n2-o21":"Full sentence: 天候不良<b>のため出発が</b>遅れた。(Departure was delayed because of bad weather.)<br>★(3rd blank)=出発. Noun + のため = because of ~ (a formal way to state a cause)","n2-o22":"Full sentence: 彼女は<b>家族にも相談</b>せず決めた。(She decided without even consulting her family.)<br>★(3rd blank)=も. 〜にも〜ず = without even ~ing to/with ~ (ず is a formal negative, = 〜ないで)","n2-o23":"Full sentence: 環境保護の<b>観点から言えば</b>問題が多い。(From the standpoint of environmental protection, there are many problems.)<br>★(3rd blank)=言え. 〜から言えば = speaking from ~, judging from the viewpoint of ~","n2-o24":"Full sentence: 締め切りに<b>間に合わせるしか</b>ない。(There is no choice but to make the deadline.)<br>★(3rd blank)=せる. Dictionary form + しかない = there is nothing to do but ~, the only option is ~","n2-o25":"Full sentence: 彼の実力は<b>高く評価されている</b>。(His ability is highly regarded.)<br>★(3rd blank)=されて. 〜されている = passive (する→される) plus ている, showing an ongoing state","n1-r9":"「両者を<b>使い分ける知恵</b>こそが、今求められている」— it's not either/or, but using each according to the situation.<br>The letter is only an <b>example</b> (〜ように), not the claim itself — picking the example as the conclusion is the most common way to lose points in N1 読解; what 「こそ」 marks is the real point.","n1-r10":"「データそのものより、<b>それをどう読むか</b>が問われている」— the focus is on interpretation.<br>Average annual income is cited as evidence that \"interpretation can drift away from real feeling\"; the amount of data collected, the calculation, and who published it are never mentioned — in the 「AよりB」 pattern, B carries the claim.","n1-r11":"「裏を返せば<b>誠実さの表れ</b>」「恐れを完全に消す<b>必要はない</b>」— fear is viewed positively.<br>「間違いを恐れるな」 is the conventional wisdom, which the writer overturns with 「だが」 — quoting common sense and then reversing it is a standard move in opinion essays, so follow the position that comes <b>after</b> 「だが」.","n5-r12":"Sentence 3: 「つくえの上にパソコンと本があります」→ answer ②. まど is off to the side and ベッド is near the door, so neither is on the desk.","n5-r13":"「えいがのあとで…ばんごはんを食べました」→ the movie came first, dinner after, so the movie was the first thing. Answer ②.","n5-r14":"「よるは のみません…ねむれないから」→ doesn't drink it at night. Answer ③.","n5-r15":"「ねこも すきですが、いえが せまいので かっていません」→ because the house is small. Answer ②. It's not that they dislike cats.","n5-r16":"「テストのあとで…カラオケに行くやくそく」→ after the test. Answer ③.","n1-o24":"Full sentence: 才能<b>に努力があいまって</b>大成した。(Talent combined with hard work led to great success.)<br>★(3rd blank)=が. 〜があいまって = \"A and B working together / reinforcing each other.\" Note the pattern: XにYがあいまって — the first noun takes に, the second takes が.","n1-o25":"Full sentence: 彼女は<b>涙ながらに事件</b>を語った。(She recounted the incident in tears.)<br>★(3rd blank)=に. 〜ながらに = doing something while in that state. 涙ながらに is a fixed expression meaning \"tearfully,\" and ながらに attaches directly to the noun.","n5-r7":"「<b>えきの前で 8時50分に会いましょう</b>」 — the meeting place is in front of the station.<br>としょかん is where they'll study, not where they'll meet. N5 reading loves putting two locations in one passage and asking which one is used for what, so match each place to its own action.","n5-r8":"「毎朝 <b>りんごを食べます</b>」 — what's eaten every morning is an apple.<br>バナナ is \"liked\" but \"rarely bought.\" Liking something isn't the same as eating it often — don't let 「もすきです」 pull you to the wrong answer.","n5-r9":"「しゃしんを<b>とらないでください</b>」 — what's forbidden is taking photos.<br>Drinks *can* be bought from the machine by the entrance (〜ことができます = can / may). The contrast between 「ないでください」 (prohibition) and 「ことができます」 (permission) is at the heart of N5 reading.","n5-r10":"「わたしは<b>ケーキを作りました</b>」.<br>It was 父 who bought the flowers. Matching each person to their action (人物と行動の対応) is the most basic reading skill there is — and keep 「作った」 and 「買った」 straight while you're at it.","n5-r11":"「冬は<b>水がつめたいですから</b>、およぎません」 — the reason comes right before から.<br>It's not that they can't swim (they do in summer); the water is simply too cold. When a question asks 「どうして」, go back to the text and look for 「から」 or 「ので」.","n4-r7":"「来週の<b>月曜日</b>は〜授業がありません」, and 「火曜日からは、ふつうどおり」 (as usual).<br>「ふつうどおり」 = classes run just like normal. Miss this word and you'll pick the wrong answer — it's a key term in notice-style passages.","n3-r11":"「参加する方は、<b>今月末までに班長にお知らせください</b>」——sign up first.<br>「動きやすい服装で」is a note about 当日 (what to wear on the day, not something to go buy)——read the deadline marker 「までに」together with the sequence word 「まず」.","n2-r7":"「定期的な見直しが<b>欠かせない</b>」(欠かせない = indispensable)——the writer recommends reviewing your subscriptions regularly.<br>The point is the balanced view in 「便利な反面〜」, not telling you to cancel everything; 「多く契約」is the opposite of the argument——to find the writer's claim, watch for expressions like 「べきだ/欠かせない/必要だ」.","n2-r8":"「原本の提出が不要となり、<b>画像を専用システムにアップロード</b>」——from October you submit a photo.<br>Submitting the original was the old rule, in force only through September; what is no longer needed is 「原本の提出」, not the receipt itself——read carefully to see which word 「不要」modifies.","n2-r9":"「分析し、次の行動を変えて<b>初めて</b>、失敗は価値を持つ」——〜て初めて = only after ~; failing by itself achieves nothing.<br>「失敗すればよいわけではない」directly rules out option 2, and 「繰り返すのは単なる不注意」rules out option 3——whatever the writer negates is the wrong answer.","n2-r10":"「大切なのは形式ではなく、<b>場面と相手に応じた適切さ</b>」——in the 「AではなくB」pattern, B is the claim.<br>「過剰な敬語はかえって〜」is precisely a denial of \"the politer the better\"; nor does the writer say to drop 敬語 altogether——both extreme options are traps.","n2-r11":"「<b>階段をご利用ください</b>」——during the construction (Tuesday 9:00 to noon) the elevator is off limits, so take the stairs.<br>「ご利用いただけません」is the polite way of saying \"cannot be used\"——you need to be able to convert the honorific phrasing of notices (「ご不便をおかけしますが」and the like) back into plain speech.","n1-r7":"The concluding sentence: 「十分な議論と、少数意見に耳を傾ける姿勢が<b>不可欠</b>」.<br>What the writer questions is 「常に正しい<b>とは限らない</b>」(not necessarily always right)——not a wholesale rejection of majority rule (nothing about 廃止), and not the reverse claim that the minority is always right——the correct answer in N1 reading is always the qualified claim; extreme options are bait.","n1-r8":"「真に理解している者ほど、<b>平易な言葉で本質を語れる</b>」——平易 = 易しい (plain, easy).<br>Firing off technical jargon is what the writer criticises as 「一見高度に見えるが」(it only looks impressive at first glance)——when 「一見〜が」appears, the writer's real point comes after it.","n4-r8":"「雨の日<b>だけは、バスで</b>行きます」——on rainy days only, she takes the bus.<br>Normally she cycles, and the train comes up only as a comparison — three modes of transport all appear. The question asks about 雨の日, and the conditional 「だけは」 is the signal that marks the answer.","n4-r9":"「田中さんに<b>連絡して</b>、新しいものを借りてください」——contact Tanaka first, then borrow a new one.<br>It's Yamada who goes to the meeting room first (わたし = the person who wrote the memo); 「直してください」 is never said. When a question asks まず (first), you have to put the actions in order.","n4-r10":"「気温は<b>低い</b>ですが、湿度が高いので、<b>暑く感じます</b>」——the temperature is lower, but it feels hot.<br>You have to read both sides of 「〜が」 (but). Read only the first half and you'll pick 涼しい; read only the second half and you'll pick 気温が高い.","n4-r11":"「<b>レジで申し込んで</b>ください」——just apply at the register.<br>The card is 無料 (free); the 300円 is the rule for earning 1 point per 300 yen spent, not a fee for the card. Don't mix up what each number is there for.","n3-r7":"「夜は〜時間が取りにくいが、<b>朝なら邪魔が入らない</b>」——no one interrupts in the morning, and that's exactly the reason.<br>夜は時間が取りにくい (which is not the same as 集中できる); a company order and 朝の残業 are never mentioned in the text. The most common trap in reading options is 本文にない情報 — information that isn't in the passage.","n3-r8":"「<b>ただし</b>、金曜日は〜<b>開店前に</b>行います」——what follows ただし (however) is the exception: on Fridays the cleaning is done before opening.<br>After closing is the ordinary rule. In notices, watch for the exception clauses that begin with ただし or なお — that's almost always where the question is.","n3-r9":"「実際には〜<b>想像以上に快適だった</b>」——it was more comfortable than imagined.<br>「寂しいのではないか」 was only the worry held *before* departure, and 「が」 overturns it in what follows. This before/after contrast (出発前 ⇔ 実際) marking a change of feeling is a staple of N3 reading.","n3-r10":"「物の価値を十分に生かせていないことを<b>惜しむ気持ち</b>」——the passage states the definition outright.<br>For questions asking どんな気持ち / どういう意味, the answer is almost always sitting in a sentence with 「〜を表す」 or 「〜という意味」. Just go back and find it.","n5-r17":"「日曜日は…夜6時にしまります」→ Sundays it closes earlier, at 6 p.m. Answer ②. 8 p.m. is only on weekdays.","n4-r12":"「自転車で会社に通っています」is the usual means of commuting. The bus is only 「雨の日だけ」. Answer ②.","n4-r13":"「ケーキも作りたかったが…できませんでした」→ the cake never got made. The flowers were given and they did eat at the restaurant. Answer ②.","n4-r14":"「漢字を書くのがまだ苦手…書く練習をもっと」→ they want to get better at writing kanji. Answer ③.","n4-r15":"The time changed from 3時 to 2時, and the place from 5階 to 3階. Both were changed. Answer ②.","n4-r16":"「1日3杯までにしています」→ they limit themselves to three cups a day. Answer ②. At night they don't drink any.","n4-r17":"「ほかの人が予約している本はのばせません」→ you can't renew a book when someone else has reserved it. Answer ②.","n3-r12":"The reason is 「外食より安く、好きな味に調整できる」— cheaper than eating out, and you can adjust the taste to your liking. Wasting ingredients is a drawback, not a reason. Answer ②.","n2-r15":"The main point: 「何もしないでいることこそ、最大の失敗」 — doing nothing is itself the biggest failure. The chain is 行動 → 学び → 成功: you act, you learn from it, you succeed. Answer ②.","n2-r16":"It acknowledges the appeal, but adds 「収入は下がる場合が多く、その現実を理解したうえで」 — income often drops, so you should go in fully understanding that reality. Answer ②.","n2-r17":"「教科書では教えられないこと」 (things textbooks can't teach) and 「人との付き合い方を学ぶ」 (learning how to get along with people) → the value lies in learning to handle relationships with others. Answer ③.","n1-r12":"The main point: 「効率という名のもとに失われるものにも、目を向けるべき」 — we should also pay attention to what gets lost in the name of efficiency. Answer ②.","n1-r13":"「真に新しいものは、しばしば同時代の理解を超えている」 — genuinely new things often go beyond what their own era can understand. Answer ③.","n1-r14":"「何が本当に必要かを見きわめ…捨てる力」 (the ability to judge what is truly necessary and discard the rest) and 「選ぶとは捨てること」 (to choose is to discard). Answer ②.","n1-r15":"「自分の見方もまた一つの見方にすぎないと自覚することが、対話の出発点」 — recognising that your own view is only one view among others is where dialogue begins. Answer ③.","n1-r16":"「形を変えながら本質を受けつぐ」 (the form changes while the essence is handed down) and 「変わらないためにあえて変わり続ける」 (keeping on changing precisely in order to stay unchanged). Answer ②.","n3-r13":"「中学の先生がすすめてくれた一冊をきっかけに」→ a book his junior-high teacher recommended was what got him started. Answer: ③.","n3-r14":"Normally the schedule is flexible, but 「水曜日だけは全員10時出社」 — Wednesday is the one day everyone must be in by 10. Answer: ②.","n3-r15":"「重くて、片手で使いにくいのが少し残念」→ it's heavy and hard to use one-handed. Answer: ③. The writer is still satisfied with it overall.","n3-r16":"「前ほどきんちょうしなくなった」→ not as nervous as before (though still not good at it). 「〜ほど〜ない」 compares with a past state. Answer: ②.","n3-r17":"「私の場合は、その土地の人と話すことがいちばんの目的」→ for this writer, talking with local people is the main goal. Answer: ③.","n2-r12":"The writer admits the performance is good, but worries that 「機械にたよりきると、自分で学ぼうとする気持ちがうすれる」 — relying entirely on machines weakens the will to learn for yourself. Answer: ③.","n2-r13":"The writer says 「味だけが理由ではない」 — taste isn't the only reason — and stresses the warmth of an owner who remembers your face and preferences and greets you. Answer: ③.","n2-r14":"「よく聞き、要点を整理してから」 and 「話す量よりタイミングと中身」 — listen well and organise the key points first; timing and substance matter more than how much you say. Answer: ③.","n1-r17":"The point is that the cause of a failure is usually clear, which \"gives you concrete guidance for your next attempt.\" Answer: ②.","n5-l5":"「<b>土曜日と日曜日は休みです</b>」— closed Saturday and Sunday.<br>「月曜日から金曜日」is when it's 開いています (open). Both sets of days get read out, and the question asks about 休み (closed) — listen to the question carefully before matching.","n5-l6":"It's 「<b>4月2日(しがつ ふつか)</b>です」.<br>ふつか (the 2nd) and はつか (the 20th) differ by a single sound, and 4月(しがつ) / 2月(にがつ) are easy to confuse too — hearing dates correctly is a guaranteed N5 listening trap.","n5-l7":"「その<b>しお</b>を取ってください」= Please pass me the salt.<br>しお(塩), さとう(砂糖) and しょうゆ(醤油) are all things on the table, so you can only tell them apart by catching the word — 「〜を取ってください」 is the set phrase for asking someone to pass something.","n5-l8":"「遠足は<b>来週の水曜日にします</b>」— it's been moved to next Wednesday.<br>「あした」was the original date (cancelled because of bad weather), and 「〜にします」 means \"we'll make it / decide on\" — listening tests love rescheduling, and the new date is the answer.","n4-l5":"「あ、<b>やっぱり5枚</b>お願いします」—「やっぱり」 signals a change of mind, so the number said last is the one that counts.<br>The earlier 「3枚」 is overruled, and it's not 3+5=8 either — this is the classic 訂正パターン (correction pattern); the moment you hear やっぱり or あ、すみません, be on alert.","n4-l6":"「今月の<b>20日(はつか)まで</b>休みです」— closed until the 20th of this month.<br>はつか (the 20th) is a special reading just one sound away from ふつか (the 2nd), and you need to catch 「今月」 accurately too — don't let your mind turn it into 来月.","n4-l7":"「燃えるごみの日<b>ではなくて</b>、<b>資源ごみの日に</b>出してください」— with 「AではなくてB」, B is the correct answer.<br>The 「燃えるごみの日」 you hear first is the part being negated — in a negation-plus-correction pattern, the answer is always in the second half.","n4-l8":"\"3階はいっぱいだったので、<b>5階</b>の会議室を取っておきました\".<br>The 3rd floor was full (that's why it wasn't booked); 「取っておく」 means it <i>was</i> reserved in advance, so \"the booking failed\" is wrong too — listen for the reason (いっぱいだった) and the result (5階を取った) as two separate pieces.","n3-l5":"\"<b>バスへの振り替え輸送</b>を行っております\" — 振り替え輸送 means alternative transport, switching passengers to another service.<br>The announcement never mentions タクシー, 徒歩, or 払い戻し. Station announcements combine humble/polite forms (おります/ください) with technical vocabulary (振り替え輸送) — exactly the hurdle N3 listening tests for.","n3-l6":"\"荷物は<b>業者に頼んだ</b>から大丈夫\" — a moving company has already been hired.<br>\"ありがとう。でも〜\" and \"気持ちだけもらっておく\" are set phrases for politely declining. Japanese refusals rarely use 「いいえ」, so recognising an indirect no is a key N3 listening skill.","n3-l7":"\"表示価格から<b>3割引き</b>\" = 30% off the listed price (you pay 70%).<br>「3割の値段になる」 would mean paying only 30% (70% off) — completely different. 「3割引き」 vs 「3割の値段」 is where number-based listening questions catch people out.","n3-l8":"8時半 minus <b>30分早める</b> = meet at 8:00.<br>「早める」 means to move something earlier; hearing it as 「遅らせる」 (postpone) leads you to 9時, and the original 8時半 is a trap answer too. Time questions in the listening section almost always require catching two numbers and doing the arithmetic.","n2-l5":"\"電源を<b>お切りの上</b>、かばんに<b>おしまいください</b>\" — switch it off <i>and</i> put it in your bag; both actions are required.<br>「お切りの上」 = 切った後で, and 「おしまいください」 = the honorific form of しまってください (put away). Unpacking keigo back into plain forms is the core N2 listening skill.","n2-l6":"\"基本的には賛成<b>だけど</b>、予算を詰めてから<b>もう一度持ってきてほしい</b>\" — conditional approval: revise the budget first, then resubmit.<br>「賛成」 is followed by 「だけど」, so you can't stop at the first half and choose \"carry it out right away\". 「詰める」 here means to tighten up / work out the figures of the budget.","n2-l7":"\"欠航となる<b>可能性がございます</b>\" — the flight <i>may</i> be cancelled; nothing is decided yet.<br>Choosing \"すでに決まった\" mistakes a possibility for a settled fact. Expressions of uncertainty like 「可能性がある / おそれがある / 〜かねない」 are exactly what N2 listening distractors are built around.","n4-l14":"「朝と夜の2回」 and 「昼は飲まなくてもいい」 → twice a day. Answer ②.","n3-l9":"「となりの席に出してしまった」 → it was served to the wrong table. Answer ②.","n3-l10":"「参加者が多くて、会場がせまくなりそう」 — too many participants, so the venue looks likely to be too small. Answer ②.","n3-l11":"「内容はよく書けて…グラフが見にくい…そこだけ直して」 → only the graph needs fixing. Answer ②.","n3-l12":"「会社のはこわれ…自分のを持ってきて」 — the company's one is broken, so bring your own. Answer ②.","n3-l13":"「朝早い時間ならすいて…6時ごろ出発がおすすめ」 — it's empty early in the morning, so leaving around 6 is recommended. Answer ②.","n3-l14":"「今では30分続けられる」 — 30 minutes is what he can manage now. Answer ③. 5 minutes was at the beginning.","n2-l9":"「地方ではのびなやみ…地方向けの宣伝に力を」 — sales are sluggish in regional areas, so put effort into advertising aimed at them. Answer ②.","n2-l8":"「先のものは<b>破棄して</b>いただけますよう」— 破棄する = 捨てる (discard, throw away).<br>「改めてお送りしました」 = the correct version has already been re-sent, so the old one doesn't need to be returned, corrected, or even kept. Business keigo vocabulary (破棄 / 改めて / お手数ですが) is the dividing line in N2 listening.","n1-l5":"「結論を申し上げるのは<b>差し控えたい</b>」— 差し控える = to (politely) refrain from doing something, to hold back.<br>「賛否が分かれて」 = opinions are split internally, so he is not stating his own position. Catching roundabout expressions like 差し控える is exactly what separates N1 listening from N2.","n1-l6":"「操作性は好評」 + 「処理速度については<b>改善を求める声が少なくない</b>」— one positive, one negative.<br>「少なくない」 is a double negative = かなりある (quite a few). Hear 「少なくない」 as 「少ない」 and the meaning flips completely — a favourite N1 trap.","n1-l7":"「納期の面で<b>折り合いがつかず</b>、他社にお願いする」— 折り合いがつかない = unable to reach an agreement; a euphemistic refusal.<br>「内容は魅力的」 is softening politeness (no problem with the content); the real reason is the delivery schedule. In business Japanese's \"praise first, then refuse\" structure, the point always comes after 「が」.","n1-l8":"「価格を<b>改定</b>させていただきます」— together with the preceding 「原材料価格の高騰」 (raw material prices soaring), 改定 is a euphemism for 値上げ (a price increase).<br>「一部製品」 ≠ すべて; 「不本意ながら」 = though it is not what we wanted (a polite apology). Business announcements never say 「値上げ」 outright, so understanding the euphemisms is understanding the message.","n5-l9":"「明日は雨がふるでしょう」→ it will rain, and she reminds him to take an umbrella. Answer ②.","n5-l10":"「今日はパンがなかったので、ごはんを食べました」→ today he ate rice. Answer ②.","n5-l11":"「1時50分までに来てください」. Answer ②.","n2-l10":"「午前中は別の予定が入っている方が多い」— most people already have other plans in the morning, so that slot is ruled out. Answer ②.","n2-l11":"Adding more staff was rejected because it would go over budget, so in the end they 「期限を1か月のばす」— push the deadline back by one month. Answer ③.","n2-l12":"「値段より質を重視…質を高める方向で」— quality matters more than price, so they decide to go in the direction of raising quality. Answer ②.","n2-l13":"「実際に手を動かす」and 「体で覚える」both point to actually doing the work yourself — hands-on practice. Answer ②.","n2-l14":"「払いもどしできる…来月の試合に使うことも」narrows it down to two choices: take the refund, or put it toward next month's match. Answer ②.","n1-l9":"「単なる安さではなく、環境への配慮」and 「多少高くても環境に良い商品を」— it is not simply about being cheap; they will pay a bit more for environmentally friendly products. Answer ②.","n1-l10":"「延期ではなく中止…契約自体が白紙に」— not a postponement but an outright cancellation, with the contract itself wiped clean (白紙に). Answer ②.","n1-l11":"「現場の声を十分にくみ取ることが欠かせない」— fully taking in the views of people on the ground is essential; imposing things from above only causes confusion. Answer ②.","n1-l12":"Note 「技術もさることながら…心に問いを投げかける」 and 「考えさせるところに価値」. The point is not the craft itself but that the work poses a question to the viewer. Answer ②.","n1-l13":"Note 「新規…それ以上にリピーター」 and 「リピーターこそが土台」. New customers matter, but repeat customers are what the business rests on. Answer ②.","n1-l14":"「失敗を学びととらえる文化こそが…組織を強くする」 — it is the culture of treating failure as learning that makes an organisation strong. Answer ②.","n5-r18":"For Sunday the text says 「うちでせんたくをして、へやをそうじします」 → answer ③.<br>Watching a film and meeting a friend belong to <b>土曜日</b>. Reading passages often list two days side by side, so circle which day is being asked about first.","n5-r19":"「<b>水曜日は休みです</b>」 → answer ①.<br>10時〜8時 are the opening hours for <b>every day except the closed day</b>; the question is about 水曜日, so don't apply the opening hours to it.","n5-r20":"For today the text says 「<b>晴れですが、風がつよい</b>」 → answer ④.<br>Rain = きのう, snow = あした. Three days of weather listed together is the classic N5 reading trap.","n5-r21":"「姉は大学生で、<b>東京にすんでいます</b>」 → answer ①.<br>The four family members living together are 「わたし・父・母」 plus the writer's household; 姉 is already away, and the text never says she lives with them.","n5-r22":"「パンも買いたかったですが、<b>ありませんでした</b>」 → answer ③.<br>「〜たかったですが」 means the writer wanted to buy it but couldn't; たまご and ぎゅうにゅう were both bought successfully.","n5-r23":"The note says 「<b>えんぴつとけしゴム</b>をもってきてください」→ answer ②.<br>The other three items are never mentioned — in reading questions you can only choose <b>what the text actually says</b>, not what common sense would add.","n5-r24":"The route is: 駅 → まっすぐ → turn <b>右</b> at ゆうびんきょくの前 → the 病院 is <b>左に</b> → answer ③.<br>For directions questions, trace each step in order — where you turn, and which side things end up on.","n5-r25":"「でも、<b>あらいものはすきじゃありません</b>」→ answer ④.<br>What follows 「でも」 is the real point. At N5, whenever you see 「でも/しかし」, pay close attention to the second half of the sentence.","n5-r26":"「急ぐ人は、<b>となりのホームの電車に</b>のってください」→ answer ②.<br>This train 「ぜんぶの駅にとまります」, so it is slower — people in a hurry need to change trains.","n5-r27":"「<b>本だなの上に</b>とけいがあります」→ answer ③.<br>まどのそば is where the <b>つくえ</b> is. For position questions, match each object to its own location and don't let the lines cross.","n5-r28":"「<b>時間がなかったからです</b>」states the reason outright → answer ②.<br>「〜からです」 is the pattern for giving a reason, so wherever it appears, that's where the answer is.","n5-r29":"「わたしは<b>9時50分に</b>駅へ行きます」→ answer ①.<br>10時 is the time they <b>agreed to meet</b>, not the time this person arrives — when two times appear, first check whose time the question is asking about.","n5-r30":"Leaving at 8時 + 30 minutes = <b>8時30分ごろ</b> → answer ③.<br>N5 reading does test simple arithmetic, and 「〜かかります」 tells you how much time something takes.","n5-r31":"「今日は<b>もう元気です</b>」→ answer ②.<br>The headache was <b>きのう</b> (yesterday); 「もう」 means \"already / now,\" signalling that the state has changed.","n4-r18":"火曜日 falls inside the range 「月曜日から水曜日まで」, and what's unavailable is the <b>2階</b> — the 1階 stays open as usual → answer ②.<br>With notice questions, first check whether the date falls inside the stated range, then check which part is affected.","n4-r19":"「<b>先に始めていてください</b>」 is the only request → answer ③.<br>The first half just explains why the speaker will be late; the request form (〜てください) is what marks 「してほしいこと」 (what they want you to do).","n4-r20":"「前の日の夜に出すと、<b>カラスにあらされます</b>」→ answer ①.<br>「〜と、〜」 states an inevitable result, so this sentence is exactly the reason for the prohibition.","n4-r21":"For first-timers the order is 「受付で用紙に記入<b>してから</b> → 2番の窓口」→ answer ③.<br>Going straight to 窓口 2番 is for people on their <b>second or later</b> visit; 「〜てから」 makes the sequence clear.","n4-r22":"「通勤が<b>20分短くなりました</b>」 is the benefit → answer ②.<br>「部屋はせまい」 is a <b>drawback</b> (the 〜が links two contrasting halves), so don't pick the opposite.","n4-r23":"「30分ほど<b>お待ちいただくことがあります</b>」→ answer ④.<br>「〜ことがあります」 means \"there are times when,\" not \"always\"; the notice never says you can't enter or that the price goes up.","n4-r24":"「車の運転は<b>しないでください</b>」→ answer ③.<br>食事 refers to when to take the medicine, not something forbidden; 寝るのは is just the natural result of getting sleepy, and isn't prohibited either.","n4-r25":"「<b>雨でも行います</b>」— it's only <b>大雨</b> (heavy rain) that cancels the event → answer ①.<br>What follows 「ただし」 is the exception, so read carefully and distinguish <b>雨</b> from <b>大雨</b>.","n4-r26":"「土曜日か日曜日のどちらかに入れる方は、<b>特に歓迎</b>」→ answer ②.<br>Working 3+ days a week is the <b>basic requirement</b>; 「特に」 marks what the question is actually asking about.","n4-r27":"「<b>春か秋に行くのがおすすめです</b>」→ answer ③.<br>Summer is singled out as 「とても暑い」, which is the reason it isn't recommended.","n4-r28":"「前の日までに<b>お願いします</b>」, and on top of that 「当日は受け付けておりません」→ answer ②.<br>「〜ておりません」 is a polite negative — it means it simply isn't accepted.","n4-r29":"「インターネットのほうは<b>手数料がかかりません</b>」→ answer ②.<br>Both methods work for applying; the only difference is the fee, and the question asks about not wanting to pay one.","n4-r30":"「番号に<b>電話する</b>か、<b>インターネットで</b>お申し込みください」→ answer ②.<br>「〜か、〜」 presents two options to choose between, so you need to catch both of them.","n4-r31":"「お持ちでない場合は、その日は<b>全額をお支払いいただきます</b>」→ answer ②.<br>It doesn't say you can't be seen — you just pay the full amount that day (usually refundable later).","n3-r18":"The water is shut off 「午前9時から午後3時まで」, and noon falls inside that window → <b>水が使えない</b>.<br>The elevator is explicitly ruled out: 「通常どおり動きます」. Electricity and going out are never mentioned in the text at all.<br>Answer ①: 水が使えない","n3-r19":"「<b>会場が変更</b>になりました」→ the venue moves from the head office to the second building in front of the station.<br>「開始時間と終了時間に変更はありません」 rules out the times directly; the date and the content are never said to change.<br>Answer ②:研修の場所","n3-r20":"The shop takes cash only, and points the way: 「近くにコンビニの<b>ATM</b>がございますので、ご利用ください」→ go withdraw cash.<br>電子マネー is explicitly ruled out; neither discussing it nor coming back another day is mentioned.<br>Answer ③:ATMで現金をおろす","n3-r21":"「途中で<b>考え込む時間</b>のほうに価値がある」 is the sentence carrying the writer's claim.<br>「早く読もうとすると…削られてしまう」 is exactly what the writer worries about, not what they advocate.<br>Answer ④:読みながら考える時間","n3-r22":"「土曜日と日曜日のどちらかは<b>必ず出勤できる方に限ります</b>」 is the only restriction given.<br>Inexperienced applicants are in fact 「歓迎します」, so they are not being excluded.<br>Answer ①:土日のどちらも出勤できない人","n3-r23":"The deadline is pushed to Monday, but 「ただし、月曜日の<b>午前中まで</b>」 is the real cut-off.<br>The condition after 「ただし」 is what decides the answer — stopping at 「月曜に延ばす」 and picking Monday evening is the trap.<br>Answer ②:来週の月曜日の午前中","n3-r24":"「遅れた日数と<b>同じ日数だけ</b>」→ 3 days late means 3 days without borrowing.<br>2週間 is the loan period, not the penalty; no fine is mentioned anywhere.<br>Answer ③:3日間、本を借りられない","n3-r25":"「<b>材料を使い切る計画</b>のほうが難しい」, followed by the example of buying too much and letting it spoil.<br>「料理そのものより」 shows that the cooking itself is not the hard part — with a 「より」 comparison, check which side is being favoured.<br>Answer ④:材料を無駄なく使い切ること","n3-r26":"「次まで<b>30分ほど待つ</b>ことになります」.<br>Two buses an hour = one every 30 minutes, so the numbers match; none of the other options appear in the text.<br>Answer ①:次のバスまで30分ほど待つ","n5-l12":"「いもうとが一人」→ the only sibling is one younger sister. 父母は含まない、犬も除く。Answer ①.","n5-l13":"「ぎんこうのとなりにありますよ」→ the post office is next to the bank; となり means right beside it. Answer ②.","n5-l14":"「電車ではなく、歩いて帰ります」→ not by train — they walk home. ではなく rules out the first option. Answer ③.","n4-l9":"「土曜日は雨…日曜日に変えました」→ Saturday was rain, so 〜に変えました: they switched it to Sunday. Answer ②.","n4-l10":"「5時前に来ていただければ、席があります」. The 〜ば conditional: if you come before 5, there will be seats. Answer ②.","n4-l11":"一度10部と言ったが「部長のぶんも…11部」に訂正。Answer ②.","n4-l12":"「修理の人を呼び…午後3時ごろ来る…家にいなければ」→ the repair person comes around 3 p.m., so they have to stay home and wait. Answer ②.","n4-l13":"「3階の会議室ではなく、2階のホール」. Not the 3rd-floor meeting room but the 2nd-floor hall. Answer ②.","n2-r21":"「車いすをご利用の方は<b>警備室にお申し出</b>いただければ、係員が誘導いたします」is a special instruction for a specific group.<br>General visitors use the normal entrance, but wheelchair users get a separate arrangement — with notice-style questions, watch for exceptions that apply only to certain people.<br>Answer ②: 警備室に申し出る","n2-r22":"It says「お支払いが<b>1か月遅れる</b>ことになります」.<br>It never says payment will be withheld or reduced — 「遅れる」and「もらえない」are two different things, so don't read in more than is written.<br>Answer ③: 支払いが1か月遅れる","n2-r23":"What the writer regrets is that「一冊を<b>繰り返し読むことの価値</b>は、以前ほど語られなくなっている」.<br>The increase in available information is just background; the text says nothing about book prices or the number of readers.<br>Answer ④: 同じ本を読み返す価値が語られなくなったこと","n2-r24":"The text says「支店勤務の方は<b>指定医療機関で</b>」.<br>「いずれの場合も費用は会社負担」applies to both groups alike, so it says nothing about the location; it is not a free choice.<br>Answer ①: 指定された医療機関","n2-r25":"The first half reports the delay, and the second half makes a request:「つきましては…<b>お打ち合わせのお時間をいただけないでしょうか</b>」. Only both halves together give the purpose.<br>「代替案をご提示したく」shows the writer intends to continue the work, not cancel the order.<br>Answer ②: 遅れを伝えたうえで打ち合わせを申し入れること","n2-r26":"「<b>休みを取りにくい空気</b>が残っていれば意味がない」and「使われない制度は、ないのと同じ」.<br>「申請すれば通る仕組みがあっても」shows the system and the procedures themselves are fine — the problem is the atmosphere.<br>Answer ③: 制度があっても使いにくい雰囲気が残っていること","n2-r27":"It says「2月末までに<b>ご自身で印刷または保存</b>しておいてください」.<br>The old data will not be carried over, so looking it up or asking about it after the migration will be too late.<br>Answer ④: 2月末までに自分で印刷や保存をしておく","n2-r28":"It says things disappear「<b>使う場面が失われるから</b>消えるのだ」, and adds「話す相手がいなくなれば…残ることはできない」.<br>The opening sentence「不便だから消えるのではない」already rules out ① directly.<br>Answer ①: 方言を使う場面と相手が失われるから","n3-r27":"「一度覚えてしまえば、<b>今までの半分の時間で</b>同じ作業が終わる」.<br>「慣れるまでに時間がかかる」says it takes a while to get used to, so \"easy\" is wrong; the contrast only becomes clear after you read past 「しかし」.<br>Answer ②: 覚えてしまえば作業が早く終わる","n3-r28":"「行き先を決めたら、<b>まず宿から押さえたほうがいい</b>」is the writer's advice.<br>Rooms are full two months ahead, so looking on the day won't work; the text never says to pick less popular places to stay.<br>Answer ③: 早めに宿を予約する","n3-r29":"「短くても<b>毎日続けたほうが効果がある</b>」, because a long break lets your body slide back to where it started.<br>「週に一度長い時間」is exactly the option being rated lower.<br>Answer ④: 短くても毎日続ける","n3-r30":"「この間は<b>ログインできません</b>」.<br>「データはそのまま残ります」is reassurance, not something you're blocked from doing; the other two options aren't mentioned in the text.<br>Answer ①: ログインすること","n3-r31":"「すでに決まったことを<b>確認する場</b>になりやすい」is stated outright in the final sentence.<br>Because the 「根回し」 has already happened beforehand, it isn't a place where decisions get made on the spot or where people argue it out.<br>Answer ②: すでに決まったことを確認する場","n2-r18":"「申請は<b>所属長を通して人事部へ</b>ご提出ください」specifies the route clearly.<br>Sending it straight to HR skips your 所属長; and even when something comes up you still have to apply — it isn't granted automatically.<br>Answer ③: 所属長を通して人事部に申請する","n2-r19":"The request itself is 「<b>差し替えをお願いいたします</b>」.<br>「なお、金額に変更はございません」is supplementary information (everything after なお is an aside), not an action being asked for.<br>Answer ④: 資料を差し替えること","n2-r20":"The claim comes in the last sentence: 「数を追うのではなく、<b>地域が受け入れられる範囲を先に決めるべき</b>」.<br>The writer grants that there are economic benefits, so this isn't a wholesale rejection of tourism; 「数を追うのではなく」is precisely what rules out \"increase visitor numbers.\"<br>Answer ①: 受け入れられる人数の上限を先に決めるべきだ","n2-r29":"「現在<b>確認中である旨をお伝えする</b>ことも、回答に含まれます」 — saying \"we're still checking\" counts as a reply.<br>「結論が出ていなくても構いません」 directly rules out \"wait until you have a conclusion, then reply.\"<br>Answer ②: 確認中であることを伝える","n2-r30":"「問題は失敗の多さではなく、<b>失敗が見えなくなる仕組み</b>のほう」 — the cause is 「報告した人が責められる」.<br>The text states outright that the number of failures is not the problem, so ① is precisely what gets denied.<br>Answer ③: 報告した人が責められ、失敗が見えなくなること","n2-r31":"「実際にお引っ越しされた後であっても、<b>1か月分の家賃をお支払いいただく</b>」.<br>No deposit or penalty fee is mentioned — with terms-and-conditions questions, don't add penalties the text never wrote.<br>Answer ④: 引っ越した後も1か月分の家賃を払う","n1-r18":"It is 「判断の材料を与えてくれるとき」, not taking the judgement over on your behalf, and 「<b>決めるのは常に当事者</b>」.<br>The writer affirms the value of advice, only separating \"seeking advice\" from \"handing over the decision\" — this is not a rejection of experts.<br>Answer ①: 専門家は材料を与えるが、決めるのは当事者である","n1-r19":"The concluding sentence is 「乱れと見えるものの多くは、その言葉がなお<b>生きていることの証拠</b>にすぎない」.<br>「変化を止めた言語＝使われなくなった言語」 means that staying unchanged is what marks a dead language, so ④ is denied.<br>Answer ②: 言葉の変化は、それが生きている証拠である","n1-r20":"「効率化が約束したのは余裕ではなく、<b>密度</b>だった」. The time freed up gets filled with new work.<br>The writer points out that the result of efficiency differs from what was expected; they never claim it is \"meaningless,\" nor do they argue for cutting the workload.<br>Answer ③: 効率化しても余裕は増えず、仕事の密度が上がること","n1-r21":"「記録に残らなかった出来事は、なかったことにされやすい」 → so writing is a 「<b>確かにあったと主張する手段</b>」.<br>「単なる保存以上の意味」 signals that the answer is not on the \"storage/memory\" level; the other three all stay at the level of personal skill or enjoyment.<br>Answer ④: 出来事がなかったことにされるのを防ぐこと","n1-r22":"「<b>前提を確かめないまま結論だけを争っても</b>…平行線をたどるほかない」.<br>What actually differs is the 「手前にある前提」 rather than the opinions themselves — this sentence is the key turning point of the whole passage.<br>Answer ①: 前提のずれを確かめないまま結論を争うこと","n1-r23":"「<b>意識されないということ自体が、その仕組みの完成度を示している</b>」.<br>Whatever follows 「裏を返せば」 is the flip the author is driving at — N1 reading passages often hide the main claim inside this kind of reversal.<br>Answer ②: 意識されないことは、その仕組みが完成している証拠である","n1-r24":"「異なる意見が実際に<b>通る回路が用意されているか</b>…は別の問題」 and 「掲げることと機能させることのあいだには、なお大きな距離」.<br>The first sentence already says organisations that 掲げる it have 「確実に増えた」, so ② is explicitly ruled out.<br>Answer ③: 多様性を掲げても、異なる意見が通る仕組みがないこと","n1-r25":"「相手の能力不足ではなく、<b>こちらの整理不足</b>である」— the author pins the cause squarely on the person doing the teaching.<br>「相手の能力不足ではなく」 directly negates ①.<br>Answer ④: 教える側の整理が足りないこと","n1-r26":"The concluding sentence: 「匿名性は<b>欠陥であると同時に、ひとつの機能でもある</b>」.<br>The author grants the 「孤独の原因として語られる」 side, but uses 「しかし」 to add the other side, so this is not a one-sided rejection.<br>Answer ①: 欠点であると同時に、利点でもある","n1-r27":"「悪用する少数を想定して条件を厳しくし…その結果、<b>本当に必要としている多数が申請にたどり着けなくなる</b>」.<br>The text says 「悪用する<b>少数</b>」, so ①'s 「多すぎる」 contradicts the original.<br>Answer ②: 悪用を防ごうとして、必要な人が使えなくなること","n1-r28":"「実際には<b>何を捨てるかを選ぶ作業</b>に近い」.<br>「すべてを残そうとした訳文は…読むに堪えない」 rules out ① outright; 「思われがちだが」 marks ② as the common impression the author overturns.<br>Answer ③: 何を捨てるかを選ぶ作業","n1-r29":"「数えられない仕事は<b>存在しないことになる</b>」 and 「評価の網からこぼれ落ちた働きが…組織を支えている」.<br>The problem is not the technology or the number of systems, but the structure in which \"unmeasurable equals nonexistent\".<br>Answer ④: 数値にならない働きが評価されず見落とされること","n1-r30":"「<b>早く言い出した人が損をしない設計</b>になっているかどうかが、その組織の強さを決める」.<br>What the author criticises is precisely the culture that treats asking for help as weakness, so ① (enduring it) runs counter to the claim.<br>Answer ①: 早く問題を言い出せる設計になっているか","n1-r31":"「本来問われるべきは、それを<b>維持し続ける意思と体力</b>が、この社会に残されているかどうか」。<br>\"Whether it has value\" is exactly the mistaken question the author says leads to things being 置き換えられてしまう, so it is a distractor, not the answer.<br>Answer ②: whether society still has 維持し続ける意思と体力","n5-l15":"20% of 1000円 is 200円, so 1000 − 200 = <b>800円</b>.<br>「20%引き」means 20% off the price, not 20% added on — it does not become 1200円. Among the options, 1000円 is the original price and 200円 is the discount amount; neither is the final price.","n5-l16":"The key line is 「3階の301教室<b>ではなく</b>、5階の501教室に変わりました」.<br>「〜ではなく」means \"not the one just mentioned,\" so you want the place named afterwards: 5階501教室. 3階301教室 is the old room that was cancelled — don't jump on the first place you hear.","n5-l17":"The key point is 「会議の前に、<b>資料をコピーしておいてください</b>」, meaning the documents must be copied before the meeting starts.<br>The meeting room, the phone call, and lunch are never mentioned in the audio — they are distractors.","n5-l18":"田中さん has already taken medicine (<b>薬を飲みました</b>) but still has pain, so 「これから<b>病院に行きます</b>」 is what comes next.<br>「薬を飲む」 is an action already completed, not something planned for 「これから」 — watch out for this tense trap.","n5-l19":"「<b>まず</b>、ゴミを出して」— 「まず」 means \"first of all,\" so the very first task is taking out the rubbish.<br>「そうじきをかける (vacuuming)」 comes after 「それから」, so don't reverse the order; 洗濯 and 料理 are never mentioned in the audio at all.","n5-l20":"The key point is 「雨のため、<b>来週の土曜日に変更</b>になりました」.<br>「今週の土曜日」 was the original date, cancelled because of rain. Don't be fooled by the first half of the sentence — listen through to the 「変更」 at the end.","n5-l21":"The station attendant says <b>次の電車は10時ではなく、10時20分に来ます</b>, so the answer is 10時20分. 10時 was the original scheduled time but no train actually comes then, so it is wrong. 10時10分 and 9時20分 never appear in the passage.","n4-l18":"Audio: 「いつも夜9時に閉まります<b>が</b>、今日はセールのため、<b>夜11時まで</b>開いています」— normally the store closes at 9 pm, but today it stays open until 11 pm for a sale.<br>What comes before 「が」 is the usual situation; the answer about \"today\" comes after it. Don't pick the usual 9 pm.","n4-l19":"Audio: 「台風のため<b>中止になりました</b>。<b>次の週末に延期します</b>」= cancelled because of the typhoon and postponed to next weekend.<br>It is neither going ahead as planned nor pushed back a month — the speaker clearly says 「次の週末」 (next weekend), so listen carefully for 週 (week), not 月 (month).","n4-l20":"Audio: 「午前10時<b>ではなく</b>、<b>午後2時に変更になりました</b>」= not 10 am; it has been changed to 2 pm.<br>This is the same 「ではなく」 logic: the 「午前10時」 mentioned first is only the old time, and the real answer comes in the second half of the sentence.","n4-l21":"Key line: 「<b>山田さん、6時から9時までお願いできる?</b>」The manager asks 山田 to work starting at 6, and 山田 agrees. 9時 is the finishing time, 3時 never appears in the script, and 「田中さんと一緒に休む」 is a distractor — 田中 is the one taking time off, not 山田.","n4-l22":"Key line: 「<b>金曜日でしたら3時に空いています</b>」The staff member says Friday at 3 is free, and the woman replies 「それでお願いします」, accepting the change. The Saturday she originally wanted to book is fully reserved, so 土曜日 is not the answer.","n4-l23":"Key line: 「<b>朝9時に来てくれると嬉しいな</b>」The man is saying he'd like his friend to come and help at 9 in the morning. The other options — 8時, 12時 and 夕方 — are never mentioned in the script; they are just distractors.","n4-l24":"Key line: 「<b>念のため来月もう一度検査を受けてください</b>」The doctor says there's nothing to worry about for now, but asks the patient to come back for another test next month just to be safe. Hospitalisation, surgery and taking medicine daily are never mentioned in the script.","n4-l25":"Key line: 「<b>雨が降ったら、体育館で行います</b>」The teacher explains that if it rains, the event moves to the gym. The park is where they go in fine weather; the classroom and cancelling are never mentioned and are only distractors.","n5-l22":"The teacher says <b>1ページから3ページまでです</b>, so the homework runs up to page 3. Page 4 is ruled out because the teacher explicitly says 「やらなくてもいい」(you don't have to do it). Pages 1 and 2 are numbers inside the range, not the page the range ends on.","n5-l23":"The shop assistant explains <b>今、Sサイズしかありません</b>, so the only size you can buy today is S. The M size is due to arrive next week, so it can't be bought yet. The L size is never mentioned at all, so it can't be the answer.","n5-l24":"The man suggests 4 o'clock and the woman answers <b>いいですよ、4時にしましょう</b>, so they settle on meeting at 4:00. 3 o'clock was the first suggestion, but it was changed, so it's wrong. 2 and 5 o'clock never come up in the conversation.","n5-l25":"The forecast says <b>あさっては雨が降るでしょう</b>, so it will rain the day after tomorrow. Today is sunny and tomorrow is cloudy — each belongs to a different day, so be careful not to mix them up. Snow is never mentioned.","n5-l26":"The text says <b>お姉さんと、お姉さんの子どもが遊びに来ます</b>, so these two are the ones coming to visit. The mother is clearly stated not to be coming because she has work. The father is never mentioned, and 田中さん is the one staying at home, so neither is correct.","n4-l15":"Audio: 「事故のため、<b>15分遅れて来ます</b>」— because of an accident, it will arrive 15 minutes late.<br>The 「バスをご利用ください」 that follows is only an alternative suggestion; it does not mean the trains have stopped running. 「時間どおり」 and 「30分遅れ」 are distractors — listen for the number 15分.","n4-l16":"Audio: 「月曜日<b>ではなく</b>、<b>水曜日に行います</b>」= not Monday, but held on Wednesday.<br>Like 「が」 and 「でも」, 「ではなく」 signals that the information is being corrected: the answer always comes after it, so don't get caught by the 「月曜日」 mentioned first.","n4-l17":"The audio first says 「5部」, then changes to 「あ、やっぱり<b>6部</b>お願い」— the corrected number at the end is the right one.<br>N4 listening often uses 「あ、やっぱり」 to signal a change of mind, so wait for the final line before choosing; don't answer as soon as you hear the first number.","n3-l22":"Key line: 「人数が変わりまして、4人から<b>6人になりました</b>」. The booking was originally for 4 people and was then changed to 6. The 4人 option is the number before the change; 5人 and 7人 are never mentioned in the script.","n3-l23":"Key line: 「301は空調が故障しているそうで、<b>302に変更になった</b>んです」. Room 301 had a broken air conditioner, so the meeting moved to 302. Room 303 is already being used for another meeting, and 304 is never mentioned in the script.","n3-l24":"Key line: 「お急ぎのお客様は、<b>バスのご利用をおすすめ</b>いたします」. The 山田線 is delayed by heavy rain, and although the 田中線 is an alternative, its service has also been reduced — so passengers in a hurry are advised to take the bus. Taxis are never mentioned in the script.","n3-l25":"Key line: 「<b>総務課に共用のがある</b>ので、そちらで借りられると思いますよ」. The man has lent his stapler to someone in the next section, so he doesn't have it on hand and suggests borrowing the shared one from the General Affairs Section. The next section is only where the man's stapler happens to be right now, not where the woman should go to borrow one.","n3-l26":"Key line: 「水曜日の授業ですが…休講になります。その代わり、<b>木曜日の3時限目に補講を行います</b>」. Wednesday's class is cancelled because the teacher is away on business, and a make-up class is held in the third period on Thursday — so it isn't that there is no class at all. Friday is never mentioned in the script.","n2-l15":"The announcement first states 「15日から17日まで<b>休館</b>」, then uses 「なお」 to add an exception: 「休館中も<b>インターネットでの予約受付は通常通り</b>行っております」.<br>「なお」 signals \"even though we are closed, this one thing is still available\" — only online reservations are unaffected; borrowing books, using the reading room, and watching DVDs are all suspended during the closure.","n2-l16":"The announcement first says 「中央線は運転を<b>見合わせて</b>おります」 (service is suspended), then offers an alternative: 「振替輸送として、<b>地下鉄丸の内線</b>をご利用いただけます」.<br>Only the 丸の内線 is designated as the substitute route; the other train names never appear in the announcement and are distractors.","n2-l17":"After the receptionist explains 「診察開始が1時間遅れる」, they offer two choices: 「<b>お待ちいただくか、明日にご予約を変更する</b>ことも可能です」.<br>The pattern 「〜か、〜も可能」 presents both as workable options, so Mr. Tanaka can either wait an hour or reschedule for tomorrow. The other options (being seen immediately, cancelling the appointment, going to a different hospital) are never mentioned.","n2-l18":"The teacher first says 「水曜日と木曜日の部活動は<b>中止</b>」, then uses 「ただし」 to introduce a contrast: 「<b>金曜日は通常通り</b>活動を行います」.<br>What comes before 「ただし」 are the cancelled days; what follows is the day activities still go ahead. Only Friday has club activities — Monday is never mentioned at all, so that option is a distractor.","n2-l19":"The announcement states the condition clearly: coupons go 「レジにて<b>2000円以上</b>のお買い物をされた方に」.<br>The purchase amount is the only condition; no membership registration or questionnaire is mentioned, and 1000円 is a deliberately planted distractor figure.","n2-l20":"The announcement says 「今回は定員に達したため、<b>受付を終了させていただきました</b>」, meaning applications for this session are already closed.<br>「来月開催予定」 refers to the <b>next</b> session, not a postponement of this one; 「無料」 and 「まだ申し込みができる」 are never mentioned and are common trap options.","n2-l21":"The key line 「<b>会場をより広い市民センターに変更する</b>」 shows that what changed is the venue, not the schedule or the number of participants.<br>「日程は変わりません」 explicitly rules out a schedule change; the number of participants is only the reason for the change, not the change itself; no speaker is mentioned in the passage.","n2-l22":"The key line 「<b>同じ型が…ございませんので、上位モデルを同じ価格でご用意させていただきます</b>」 shows the clerk has decided to substitute an upgraded model — not to repair the item, issue a refund, or reorder the same model.","n2-l23":"The key line 「<b>迂回路として環状七号線の利用を呼びかけています</b>」 means drivers are being urged to detour via Route 7 (環七), rather than simply to stop using the expressway or the ordinary roads. The passage also contains no particular call to avoid the accident site.","n2-l24":"The key line 「<b>急遽午前10時からも受付を開始する</b>」 shows that a morning reception slot has been added — the hours were not shortened, and participant numbers did not drop. No change of venue is mentioned either.","n2-l25":"The key line 「<b>代わりに経験のある佐藤さんに行ってもらうことにしました</b>」 shows that since 鈴木さん cannot get away, 佐藤さん will go on the business trip instead — not 鈴木 himself and not the section chief.","n4-l26":"Key line: \"<b>まず店の中を案内しますね</b>\" — the manager says that on the first day he will show the new employee around the shop first, and only after that teach her how to use the register. So the first thing she does is get shown around the store, not practise on the register.","n3-l15":"The announcement first gives the original due date as 「火曜日」, but then adds 「月曜日は祝日で休館のため、<b>水曜日までに返却してください</b>」 — because the library is closed on Monday for a holiday, the deadline is pushed back to Wednesday.<br>「火曜日」 is the original (old) deadline, not the final answer — you have to listen to the end for the change.","n3-l16":"Hearing only 「野外コンサートは中止」 makes you want to pick option 0, but the speaker continues with 「<b>体育館での代替公演は、予定通り行います</b>」, so although the outdoor event is cancelled, the indoor replacement performance still goes ahead.<br>Watch out for 「なお」 — it usually introduces extra information or a correction to what was just said.","n3-l17":"The basic rule is 「セールは<b>本日まで</b>」, and 「会員のお客様に限り、あしたまで」 is a special exception for <b>members only</b>.<br>The question asks about a customer who is <b>not</b> a member, so the extension does not apply and the answer is \"today\".","n3-l18":"The man's 「3時からでしたよね」 is him confirming what he remembers (the old information), and the woman corrects him: 「部長の予定が変わって、<b>2時からになった</b>」.<br>The revised time is the correct answer — don't be misled by the 「3時」 that comes up first.","n3-l19":"「急用ができた」 is only the reason; the actual request comes at the end: 「<b>4時に変更していただくことはできますか</b>」.<br>He doesn't want to cancel the booking, and he doesn't want it moved earlier — he just wants to push the time back, so the answer is \"to change the time of the reservation\".","n3-l20":"The announcement clearly states 「来週は祝日の関係で、月曜日ではなく<b>火曜日に変更</b>」, so for next week only, rubbish day is Tuesday.<br>Note that 「再来週からは、また元の月曜日に戻ります」 is about <b>the week after next onwards</b>, so it is not the answer for next week.","n3-l21":"Key line: 「店頭に展示していた商品に限り、<b>さらに10%引かせていただきます</b>」. Items are already 20% off, and display items get another 10% on top, making 30% in total. The 10% is only the extra reduction, 20% is the price for new stock, and 40% never appears in the script.","n1-l22":"The key phrase is 「<b>名前がないからこそ、読者一人一人が自分の経験を投影できる</b>」. The reason is the author's aim of having readers overlay the protagonist with themselves. Option 3 is the exact opposite of the author's intention, and the other options are not mentioned.","n1-l23":"The key phrases are 「<b>数量を現在の倍にしていただけるなら、こちらも二割引きで対応いたします</b>」 and 「それでしたら、ぜひその条件でお願いします」. They agreed on a 20% discount in exchange for doubling the order quantity. The other options are merely proposals raised partway through the negotiation.","n1-l24":"The key phrase is 「<b>改革の方向性そのものは評価できますが、拙速な導入は避け…慎重な制度設計が求められます</b>」. The speaker affirms the direction while arguing for a cautious approach. The other options either contradict the passage or are not mentioned.","n1-l25":"The key phrase is 「<b>厄介なのは、本人がこの偏りに気づきにくいという点です</b>」. What is treated as the biggest problem is that the person involved does not notice their own bias. The other options are examples or countermeasures, not the \"most troublesome point.\"","n1-l26":"The key phrase is 「<b>急きょ会議室Cに変更となりました</b>」. Because the air-conditioning inspection is running long, today's meeting will be held in 会議室C. 会議室A was the originally scheduled location, 会議室B is not mentioned, and cancellation is wrong because the start time is unchanged.","n2-l26":"The key line「<b>20時30分からのお席でしたら…ご案内できます</b>」shows the staff member is offering to move the reservation to 20:30 — not to change the date, reduce the party size, or cancel the booking. 「〜でしたら」presents a conditional alternative, and「ご案内できます」means they can seat you under that condition.","n1-l15":"「欠航ではなく」first rules out a cancelled flight; the real point is「出発時刻を大幅に<b>繰り上げて</b>」.「繰り上げる」is a frequently tested N1 verb meaning to move something *earlier*, not to postpone it and not to keep the original time. The other options contradict the passage or have no basis in it.","n1-l16":"The professor first shows sympathy with「気持ちは分かりますが」, then softly refuses with「<b>難しいかと存じます</b>」. This is the classic Japanese indirect refusal — 「難しい」here does not mean \"difficult but possible\"; it means no. Don't be misled by the considerate tone of the first half; the meaning lies in the polite refusal that follows.","n1-l17":"「方向性自体は悪くない」is just a softening opener. The real message is「<b>根本から練り直していただいたほうが</b>」—「根本から」means from the ground up and「練り直す」means to rework/rethink, so together they call for a major overhaul, not minor tweaks and not outright rejection.","n1-l18":"Customer service explicitly rules out discontinuation with「生産終了ではなく」: the item is「<b>一時的に品切れ</b>」, temporarily out of stock, and「来月には入荷の予定」means new stock is due next month. So the correct answer is a temporary shortage; every other option contradicts the passage.","n1-l19":"The key phrase is「<b>積もるほどの降雪にはならない</b>」—「〜ほどの」marks the degree, so it won't snow *enough to accumulate*. Temperatures will drop, but there will be no snow build-up: different from the heavy snow originally forecast, and not the same as no snow at all. Hence \"temperatures drop but no snow accumulates.\"","n1-l20":"「異常値が見られたわけではなく」clearly denies that anything abnormal was found; the point is「<b>念のため</b>」— the re-test is being scheduled just to be safe, not because a problem was detected or because the first test was faulty. The re-test will still go ahead, but purely as a precaution.","n1-l21":"The key sentence is「<b>目に見えないこの機会費用を考慮しなければ、本当に合理的な選択はできない</b>」: without factoring in this invisible opportunity cost, you cannot make a truly rational choice. The professor's claim is that considering opportunity cost is essential to rational decision-making. The other options are merely examples from the text or actions that run counter to the argument."};
